"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Ransomware': {
        'body': "\nRansomware is software that encrypts stored files or locks a computer, then demands payment for a key the victim may never receive. The lock and the demand are the definition. A self-spreading carrier can drop the program, and a useful-looking installer can drop it too. Neither delivery method renames the crime. The payment note is what makes the program ransomware.\n\nThe program walks folders the signed-in user can change, encrypts documents, and leaves the originals unreadable. Crews that want speed encrypt each file with a symmetric cipher such as AES-256, then wrap that file key with the attacker's public key, commonly RSA 2048, so the victim cannot unwrap it. Before the rewrite, intruders often delete Volume Shadow Copies with vssadmin delete shadows and turn off Windows recovery with bcdedit. A note on the desktop names a deadline and a payment address. Some crews copy files out first and threaten to publish them. The lock is still the core.\n\nPayroll clerk Dana opens the finance share on FS01 on Monday morning. Every spreadsheet has a new extension, and a text file on the share says payment is due before the end of the day. Disk activity on FS01 stays high. Process creation shows vssadmin.exe with delete shadows a few minutes before the extensions changed, and the backup catalog that lived on that same server is gone. The note and the shadow-copy deletion are the two facts the ticket needs.\n\nContainment means isolating the affected hosts and disabling any stolen account that is still signing in. Isolation stops more shares from being encrypted, and isolation is not eradication. Keep the note, a few encrypted samples, and the process logs before anyone formats a disk. Eradication, after that evidence is preserved, means removing the program and closing the hole it used, such as a missing patch or a guessed password. Recovery uses backups that were offline or otherwise beyond the reach of the encrypted server, and only after a test restore proves the files open. Paying the note does not remove the program.\n\nStudents pick a worm, a virus, or a trojan because one of those delivered the payload. A worm spreads by itself from one computer to the next. A virus needs a host file, and a user or a process has to run that file before more files can be infected. A trojan pretends to be useful. Choose ransomware when the visible result is encrypted or locked data plus a demand for payment.\n",
        'r0': "\nModern locker crews usually encrypt each document with AES-256 and wrap the per-file key with an RSA 2048 public key the victim does not hold. Process creation Event 4688 often shows vssadmin.exe delete shadows /all /quiet, and bcdedit with recoveryenabled set to No, in the minutes before extensions change. Those commands remove local Volume Shadow Copies so a same-disk restore fails. The desktop note names a deadline and a payment address. Containment is the handling phase that comes first: isolate the host and disable a stolen account. Eradication comes after the note and the 4688 records are copied, and eradication means deleting the program and patching the hole. Recovery uses backups that stayed offline. Payment is not a handling phase. The wrapped file key stays unreadable without the attacker's private key.\n",
        'r1': '\nThis malware locks files and then asks for money. The files are made unreadable on purpose. A note on the screen names a deadline. The demand for payment is the whole point. Local restore copies are often deleted first. That deletion makes a quick undo fail. Staff see junk names where spreadsheets were. Separate the sick computer from the network. Keep the note and one sample file. Restore from a backup that stayed offline. A worm travels from machine to machine alone. A virus rides inside a file someone opens. A fake useful program can drop this locker. The lock plus the demand names the problem.\n',
        'r2': "\nPicture a warehouse where a night crew wraps every carton in steel bands and leaves one invoice on the dock. Forklifts cannot open a carton. The crew also emptied the office cabinet of spare cutters, so yesterday's local spares are gone. Paying the invoice might produce a cutter that does not fit, and the bands stay on. The reliable stock sits in a second warehouse across town that the night crew never entered. Managers photograph a few banded cartons and the invoice before they cut anything, so they can show what happened. The banded cartons are the encrypted files, and the invoice is the ransom note. The second warehouse is the offline backup, and the empty cabinet is the loss of local restore copies.\n",
        'r4': "\nWrite down the note's deadline, the payment address, and the new file extension. Save the note and one encrypted file onto clean media. Check process logs for vssadmin, wbadmin, or bcdedit in the minutes before extensions changed. List every account that was logged on to the file server. Isolate the hosts, disable any stolen account, and do not format the disk yet. Do not pay from the affected machine. Do not restore over the encrypted copies until an offline backup has been proven to open.\n",
        'exam': '\nA ransom note plus unreadable files is ransomware even if a worm delivered the program. Self-spread with no lock and no demand is a worm. A host file that infects the next file when opened is a virus. A program that only pretends to be useful is a trojan. Isolating the host and disabling a stolen account are containment. Deleting the program and patching the hole, after the note and logs are saved, is eradication. Paying is not eradication, and the key may never arrive.\n',
    },
    'Trojans': {
        'body': "\nA trojan is a program that presents itself as something useful or harmless and also does something harmful. The disguise is the definition. The program does not have to spread by itself, and the program does not have to attach to other files. A person, or a process the person started, has to choose to run the lure. Until that run, the harmful half stays inside the disguise.\n\nThe harmful half can be a remote-control tool, a quiet downloader, or a later payload. The lure is an invoice viewer, a codec, a game, a toolbar, or a fake update. After it runs, the program often copies itself into a user folder and adds a Run key or a scheduled task so the program returns after reboot. The parent process in the log is commonly the browser, the mail client, or the installer the person just clicked. The file's SHA-256 hash will not match the real tool whose name was copied.\n\nAccounts clerk Luis receives a message that looks like a supplier invoice and downloads InvoiceViewer.exe. Luis opens the file because the month-end folder is full of similar names. No invoice appears. Sysmon Event ID 1 shows InvoiceViewer.exe as the parent of a second executable that is absent from the software list, and a new scheduled task named Invoice Sync is set to run at logon. The second program then opens an outbound session. The ticket fact is the mismatch between the invoice icon and the child process.\n\nDefenders hash both files, quarantine the host, and remove the scheduled task only after the event log is copied. An application allow list stops the unknown viewer from launching on the next machine. Opening supplier documents from the vendor portal, rather than from an unexpected attachment, removes the easy click. Endpoint software may quarantine the file, and the ticket should still record how Luis was persuaded. Isolating the workstation is containment. Deleting the program and the scheduled task, once the log copies are safe, is eradication.\n\nStudents call this a worm as soon as a second computer is involved, or a virus as soon as a file is involved. A worm moves on its own without anyone opening a lure. A virus attaches to a host file and infects further files when that host runs. A trojan stays the right answer when the trick is pretending to be useful, even if the hidden half later drops a locker or a key recorder.\n",
        'r0': '\nThe lure is a standalone executable, not a macro glued into a document. When Luis runs InvoiceViewer.exe, Sysmon Event ID 1 records that image as the parent of a child missing from the allow list. Persistence is a Run key under the user hive or a scheduled task set to run at logon, often named like an updater. Compute SHA-256 of both files and compare the digests with the software catalog. A mismatch is the finding. The child may call out on TCP 443, which blends with ordinary browsing, so the hash and the parent-child pair matter more than the port. Containment isolates the workstation. Eradication removes the executable and the Run key after the event log is copied. A worm would spread without this click. A virus would alter a host document.\n',
        'r1': '\nThis program looks helpful and also does harm. The disguise is what makes the trick work. Someone has to choose to run the program. An invoice tool can hide a remote control. A new startup task can bring the program back. The file name can copy a real tool. Check the file against the known good copy. A worm spreads without a person opening a lure. A virus attaches to a file you already use. Take the computer off the network. Save the startup task name before you delete it. Remove the program only after those notes are saved. Allow only programs your workplace has named.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic kitchen sets out a sample tray labeled as the usual soup. The bowl looks right, and a staff member takes it because the label matches lunch. Under the lid the soup is spoiled, and a second container taped beneath the tray is not food at all. Nobody chased the staff member down the hall. The label did the work. A kitchen that serves only recipes from a posted card will refuse the tray even when the label is familiar. A photo of the tray, taken before the trash run, lets the kitchen explain the label later. The labeled soup is the useful-looking program. The hidden container is the harmful tool, and the posted card is the list of programs allowed to run.\n',
        'r4': '\nHash the lure and the child process with SHA-256 and compare both with the software catalog. Write down the parent image from Sysmon Event ID 1, the scheduled task or Run key, and the outbound destination. Copy those logs before you delete anything. Isolate the workstation. Do not open the viewer again to see what the viewer does. Remove the persistence entry only after the copies exist. Ask what the user thought they were installing, and record that sentence in the ticket.\n',
        'exam': '\nPretending to be useful is the fact that selects a trojan, even when the hidden half is a remote tool. A worm that spreads overnight with nobody opening a lure is a different answer. A virus that attaches to a template and infects the next document is a different answer. A rootkit fits only when local tools are being lied to. Isolating the workstation is containment. Removing the program and the Run key after the logs are saved is eradication. The invoice icon is the disguise, not proof the file is harmless.\n',
    },
    'Worms': {
        'body': "\nA worm is malware that copies itself from one computer to another without a person opening a host file. The spread is the definition. The program looks for the next machine on its own, often by scanning addresses and using a flaw that does not require a click. No disguise has to persuade anyone, and no document has to carry the code.\n\nA typical worm picks a neighbor on the same subnet, connects to a listening service, and sends a copy of itself. On many Windows networks the listening service is file sharing on TCP port 445. One vulnerable server can infect the next host on that segment overnight. The user of the second machine may be logged off. The process tree shows the network service as the parent, not a person double-clicking a document. Some worms also carry a second payload. Self-spread is still what makes the program a worm. A passenger program can ride along, and the passenger does not rename the carrier that walked the segment alone.\n\nOn-call analyst Mei checks the imaging VLAN at 06:10. Server IMG02 was missing a file-sharing patch. By morning, three other hosts on the same VLAN show new services and a spike of connections to TCP 445 from IMG02. Nobody was in the building. The firewall log between that VLAN and the rest of the hospital shows the scan, host after host, with IMG02 as the first source. Mei records IMG02, port 445, and the 06:10 start time before any patch is applied. The ticket should name IMG02 first and list the later hosts in time order.\n\nCutting the VLAN off from its neighbors is containment, because the walk stops while the scope is learned. After a copy of memory and the suspicious binary are stored, eradication means deleting the worm and applying the patch that closes the hole. Segmentation limits how far a scan can travel. An application allow list on workstations can refuse the dropped copy even if one packet gets through. Backups still matter if a passenger program encrypted files, but the passenger's payment demand is a separate topic from the self-spread.\n\nThe exam trap is the virus, because both multiply. A virus cannot move until a host file runs, and a person or a process has to run that host. A trojan waits behind a disguise until someone launches the disguise. When the question stresses that nobody opened a file and the copies still appeared across a segment, the answer is a worm.\n",
        'r0': '\nSelf-propagation is the mechanism. The worm scans neighbors and delivers a copy without a user opening a file. On many Windows incidents the listening service is SMB on TCP 445, and the parent of the new copy is the service rather than a person at the keyboard. One missing patch on IMG02 was enough for the copy to walk the imaging VLAN overnight. Log the first source address, destination port 445, and the time each new host starts scanning. Containment isolates that VLAN. Eradication deletes the binary and applies the patch once a forensic copy exists. A virus cannot make this hop until a person or a process runs the host file. The count of new scanners before 06:10 belongs in the ticket.\n',
        'r1': '\nThis malware copies itself to the next computer. No person has to open a file. The program searches nearby machines on its own. One weak server can infect the next overnight. The building can be empty while copies appear. The parent of each copy is a service. Separate that network area from its neighbors. Save a copy of the program before cleanup. Install the missing fix after the copy is saved. A virus must ride inside a host file. Someone must run that host before more files change. A fake invoice program waits for a click. Spread with no click is the fact that fits here.\n',
        'r2': '\nOne sick patient in a clinic leaves the bed and walks room to room, touching the next chart, without a nurse carrying them. Doors that should have been latched are open, so the walk continues all night while the building is empty. No one delivers a package. The patient moves alone. Closing the hallway doors stops new rooms from being entered, and treating each room comes after the path is written down. A parcel that infects other parcels only when a clerk opens it is a different problem, because the parcel waits for the clerk. The walking patient is malware that spreads by itself. The open doors are the unpatched services that let the walk continue.\n',
        'r4': '\nList the first host that scanned neighbors and the time each later host began the same scan. Record destination port TCP 445 and the parent process of the new binary. Copy the binary and its hash before cleanup. Isolate the segment so the walk stops. Do not patch over the evidence, and do not reimage as the first move. After the copy is stored, delete the worm and apply the missing patch. Note whether anyone opened a file. If nobody did, write that fact down.\n',
        'exam': '\nCopies that appear across a segment while nobody opened a file are a worm. A virus needs a host file and a person or process to run that host before more files are infected. A trojan needs a disguise someone chooses to launch. If the copies also encrypt files and demand payment, the locker is ransomware and the self-spread is still a worm. Isolating the segment is containment. Deleting the worm and patching, after a forensic copy, is eradication. Do not pick virus merely because the program multiplied.\n',
    },
    'Spyware': {
        'body': "\nSpyware is software that hides and collects information, such as keystrokes, screen images, or files, then sends that information out. Quiet collection is the definition. A ransom note is a different crime, and infecting every document in a folder is a different mechanism. The user is supposed to keep working while the copy leaves.\n\nThe program often arrives as a browser add-on, a bundled installer, or a second stage dropped by something the user already ran. Once resident, the program hooks a browser, a keyboard path, or the file system, stores what it sees, and posts the collection to an address the workplace did not approve. The traffic can be small and steady, so a single loud alert may never fire. A browser add-on the user does not remember installing is a common home for this behavior, because the browser already holds history, typed passwords, and the pages on screen.\n\nNurse Andre uses shared workstation FD02 at the front desk. A week later the proxy log shows that browser posting session history every few minutes to a domain outside the clinic allow list. The add-on list contains Coupon Helper, which nobody in the unit requested. The extension was installed on Tuesday, and the posts start the same afternoon. Andre never requested Coupon Helper on the software form. The proxy line is a repeating post of page addresses, and the add-on identifier belongs on the same ticket line as that destination. The ticket fact is the add-on name plus the repeating outbound posts of browsing history.\n\nRemove the add-on's rights only after the extension file and the proxy log are copied. Isolate FD02 if the posts include passwords or patient pages. Reset passwords that were typed while the add-on was installed, because the collection may already have succeeded. A browser policy that blocks unapproved extensions keeps the next image clean. Monitoring for a new extension and a new outbound destination is the detection path. Disk encryption does not stop this program, because the program reads data after the user has unlocked the session.\n\nA virus is the wrong label when nothing attaches to other files. A virus attaches to a host file and spreads to other files when that host runs. Extra toolbars that a vendor preinstalled, without a hidden collection channel, are bloatware. A tool whose only job is to record keys is the narrower neighbor. Choose spyware when the behavior is hidden collection of what the user sees, types, or stores.\n",
        'r0': '\nThe collection channel is the clue on FD02. A browser extension the user did not request posts history on a timer, about every three minutes, to a domain outside the proxy allow list. The extension folder under the browser profile, the extension identifier, and one sample POST belong in the ticket. If keystrokes are included, the same host may load an extra DLL, but the broader finding is hidden collection rather than keys alone. Hash the extension package with SHA-256 before the profile is deleted. Resetting passwords typed during the collection window is recovery work, after containment has stopped the posts. Encryption of the disk at rest does not interrupt a tool that reads an unlocked session. A virus would change other files. This add-on only needs its own store.\n',
        'r1': '\nThis software hides and collects private information. The program may take keystrokes, screens, or files. Then the program sends what it collected outward. A browser add-on is a common hiding place. The user often does not remember installing it. Browsing history leaves in small repeated posts. The posts go to a site nobody asked for. The computer can look normal the whole time. Save the add-on before you remove it. Change passwords that were typed while it ran. Block extra browser add-ons with a workplace rule. A virus attaches itself to other files. Preinstalled junk without spying is a different problem. Hidden collection is the behavior to name.\n',
        'r2': '\nA hotel desk clerk keeps a carbon sheet under every form a guest signs and photographs the counter when the guest looks away. The clerk stays quiet, smiles, and files the copies in a back room that sends a packet of pages to a stranger each hour. Guests still receive their keys. The theft is the copy, not a lock on the door. The manager keeps one carbon sheet as proof before the forms are switched. A new house rule says only hotel forms, with no extra layer, may sit on the desk. The carbon sheet is the hidden collector on the computer. The hourly packet is the history or the typed text leaving the machine.\n',
        'r4': '\nExport the browser add-on list and copy the extension folder before you remove it. Write down the outbound domain, how often the posts repeat, and one sample of what was sent. Note whether the posts include history, screens, or files. Isolate the host if patient pages or passwords were included. Do not clear the browser profile yet. Reset affected passwords from a clean machine after the evidence copy. Record the add-on install time and the user who was logged on.\n',
        'exam': '\nHidden collection of keystrokes, screens, or files is spyware. A virus answer requires a host file that infects other files when that host runs. Preinstalled toolbars that waste resources without a secret collection channel are bloatware. A keylogger is the narrower choice only when the evidence is the keys alone. Disk encryption does not stop collection inside an unlocked session. Isolating the host to stop the posts is containment. Removing the add-on after the folder is copied is eradication.\n',
    },
    'Bloatware': {
        'body': '\nBloatware is extra software that ships on a device, using memory, disk, and startup time, and adding programs the owner did not ask to run. The problem starts in the image or the vendor bundle, not in a secret infection of other files. The extras may be clumsy rather than covertly malicious. The extras still expand the number of bugs, updaters, and listeners the workplace must carry.\n\nA new laptop arrives with trial suites, a game, and several toolbars, plus an updater that phones home. Each extra can run at boot, hold files open, and expose its own update channel. The game was on the disk when the box was opened. The game was not a payload someone emailed. A publisher signature tells you who built the file. A signature does not mean the clinic needs the file. An old toolbar that nobody updates becomes a convenient place for a later flaw.\n\nTechnician Omar images a replacement laptop from the factory setup instead of the clinic standard image. On first boot, Task Manager shows a game, two shopping toolbars, and a third toolbar Omar cannot name. Idle memory sits about 1.2 GB above the standard build. Idle memory, not a ransom note, is the symptom Omar measures. Omar writes the four product names on the unbox ticket before the laptop leaves the bench. The clinic standard image contains none of those four names. The inventory ticket lists those four items as present at unbox, with no request from the employee who will receive the machine. That inventory line is the fact to keep.\n\nThe defender rebuilds from the managed image, or removes the extras before the laptop is handed to anyone. Record the product names and versions that were removed so the next shipment can be checked against the same list. Leaving a trial updater in place for a week leaves a standing task with its own network path. Configuration enforcement can flag a machine whose installed set drifts from the baseline. An application allow list that names only clinic software will refuse the game even if someone restores the factory disk later.\n\nEvery unwanted program is not a virus. A virus attaches itself to a host file and infects more files when that host runs. Hidden collection of history is spyware. Preinstalled extras that mainly waste resources and widen the surface are bloatware, even when a vendor logo is on the box.\n',
        'r0': "\nCount what the factory image added. Omar's laptop showed a game plus three toolbars, and idle RAM sat about 1.2 GB above the clinic standard image. Those four products start at boot and keep their own updaters. A publisher signature names the builder. The signature does not place the file on the allow list. The fix is to reimage from the managed build, or to uninstall and record each product name and version on the ticket. Configuration enforcement should flag the machine if those names return. No published flaw has to exist for the removal to be correct. The extra surface is the problem: more code, more update channels, more places a later flaw can land. This is not a host-file infector and not a hidden collector.\n",
        'r1': '\nExtra programs arrive already installed on the device. The owner did not ask for those programs. The programs use memory, disk, and startup time. The programs also add more things that can break. A new laptop may include toolbars and a game. Those items were in the box at unboxing. A familiar publisher name does not mean you need the program. Rebuild the machine from your own clean image. Write down every extra name you remove. Do not leave a trial updater running. A virus infects other files when a host runs. Hidden spying is a different behavior. Unwanted extras that waste resources fit this label.\n',
        'r2': '\nA moving truck delivers the sofa you bought and also unloads three extra armchairs, a game table, and a lamp you never ordered. The apartment is crowded, and the walkways shrink. Each extra piece can break, and each piece came from the truck rather than from a thief in the night. The delivery paper carries a shop name you recognize, which does not make the lamp yours. Sending the truck back and refurnishing from your own list is the clean fix. Writing down each unwanted piece keeps the next delivery honest. The extra furniture is the software that shipped on the device. The crowded walkways are the wasted memory and the added ways the machine can fail.\n',
        'r4': '\nInventory every program that was present at unbox and was not on the request. Write down the product name, the version, and whether the program starts at boot. Measure idle memory against the standard image and record the gap. Do not leave trial updaters installed while you decide. Rebuild from the managed image, or uninstall and keep the list. Do not add those publisher names to the allow list merely because the files are signed. Compare the next shipment with the same list.\n',
        'exam': '\nFactory extras that consume resources and add unwanted programs are bloatware, even when a known publisher signed them. A signature names the builder and does not mean the workplace asked for the program. A virus must attach to a host file and infect further files when that host runs. Spyware must hide and collect information. A game and toolbars on a new laptop, with no infection of other files, are not a virus. Reimaging to the managed build is the fix. A ransom note is a different problem.\n',
    },
    'Viruses': {
        'body': "\nA virus is malware that attaches to a host file and runs only when that host runs. A user or another process has to launch the host. After the host runs, the virus tries to attach the same way to more files. The host can be a document with a macro, a script, or an executable. Without that host, the virus has nowhere to live and no moment to act.\n\nThe infection step is the part students skip. Virus code is inserted into the host, or a macro is added to a template. Opening the host executes the virus in the context of that application. The code then looks for other documents or programs it can modify. On a file share, one template can do a lot of harm because many people open the template during a normal day. The virus does not scan the subnet and break into the next computer by itself. Copies on other machines appear because a person or a process opened an infected host there, or because the infected file was copied and later opened.\n\nAnalyst June notices that the shared template Normal.dotm changed at 08:12. By noon, twelve other documents in the same folder contain a new macro that yesterday's copies lacked. Each author who opened the template that morning saved a document afterward, and those documents now carry the macro. The endpoint log shows the word processor as the parent, and the user names match people who had the share open. No scan of port 445 appears. The template's new macro, and the list of files that gained a macro only after the template was opened, are the ticket facts.\n\nContainment means stopping writes to the share, or pulling the template out of reach, after an infected sample is saved for the ticket. Tell people to stop opening the affected documents. Eradication means removing the macro from each infected file and fixing the permission that let an ordinary user rewrite the shared template. Least privilege on the template, so only the template owner can edit it, removes the easy path. A scanner helps after a signature exists, and a scanner is not a reason to leave the template writable by everyone.\n\nThe neighboring trap is the worm. A worm spreads by itself, with no host file a person must open. A trojan is a standalone program that pretends to be useful, rather than code glued into a document someone already meant to open. When the payload rides inside a template and infects the next document at open time, the answer is a virus.\n",
        'r0': "\nThe host file is mandatory. June's host was Normal.dotm on the department share, changed at 08:12, and by noon twelve other documents had gained a macro that yesterday's copies lacked. The word processor is the parent process, and the user is whoever opened the template. There is no scan of TCP 445, and no copy runs before the document opens. Save the template and one infected document, hash both with SHA-256, and only then remove the macro. Permissions should leave the template writable by one owner, not by the whole share. Tightening that permission, paired with cleaning each file, is eradication of the easy path. A worm would have spread overnight with nobody opening a document. The order to remember is open first, infect the next file second.\n",
        'r1': '\nThis malware lives inside a host file. The malware runs only when that host runs. A person or a process must start the host. Then the malware tries to attach to more files. A shared template can infect many documents. People spread the malware by opening the file. The malware does not break into the next computer. Stop writes to the shared folder. Save one infected file before you clean. Strip the bad part from each saved document. Let only the owner edit the master template. A worm copies itself with no host file. A fake useful program is a separate trick. Riding inside a file is the key fact.\n',
        'r2': '\nA kitchen keeps one master recipe card that cooks are told to copy. Someone brushes glue onto that card. The glue does nothing until a cook picks the card up and starts the dish. Then the glue sticks to every new card the cook writes and files. Cards that stay in the box untouched stay clean. The glue cannot walk to another kitchen by itself. A cook, or a machine that handles the card, is required. Locking the master card so only one cook may change it stops the glue from being renewed. The master card is the host file. The glue is the virus, and the glue spreads only when that host is used.\n',
        'r4': '\nSave the shared template and one newly infected document, and hash both before cleanup. Write down the time the template changed and the users who opened the template afterward. List files whose macros appeared only after that open. Stop writes to the share. Do not open the template on an admin machine to inspect the macro. Remove the macro only after the samples are stored. Change the template so only its owner can edit it, and record that permission on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': '\nA host file, execution by a user or a process, and infection of more files make a virus. A worm spreads by itself with no host to open. A trojan is a separate program that pretends to be useful, not code glued into a document. Ransomware is a lock plus a payment demand. The order "opened the template, then other documents gained the same macro" selects a virus. Stopping writes to the share is containment. Cleaning the files and limiting who can edit the template, after samples are saved, is eradication.\n',
    },
    'Keyloggers': {
        'body': "\nA keylogger records what a person types. Passwords, messages, and search boxes are all in scope, because each of them passes through the keyboard path. The narrow job is the recording of keys. Screen photos, browser history, and whole-file theft can sit beside a keylogger inside a larger spy tool. A question about typed characters is still about this topic.\n\nA software keylogger hooks the operating system's keyboard path or polls the key state, then writes the characters to a hidden file or sends the characters out. A hardware keylogger is a small device between the keyboard and the computer, and a hardware device needs no process in Task Manager. Either way, the output is a stream of what was typed. On a workstation, the clue is often a short outbound burst that lines up with the moment someone submits a login form, because the tool flushes a buffer when the form is sent. Those characters include the password, which your own portal does not need to ship to an outside host.\n\nAt the billing desk, host BIL04 starts sending a small post to an unfamiliar address every time clerk Nia types into the billing portal's login form. The posts stop when Nia stops typing, and the posts resume on the next login. The process list shows an extra DLL loaded into the portal process, and that DLL is not part of the portal's signed set. The ticket should quote one login time, the destination of the burst, and the DLL name. If the bursts continue from a clean image and a spare keyboard, inspect the cable for a hardware device before blaming the software image.\n\nContainment means isolating BIL04, unplugging any suspect inline device, and retiring every password that was typed there. Preserve the DLL and one packet capture of a burst before anything is removed. Reset the affected accounts from a clean machine. Eradication means removing the hook or the hardware device after that evidence is stored. A host control that blocks a new keyboard hook, plus a desk check for inline devices, is the practical pair. Full-disk encryption does not stop a keylogger, because the keys are captured after the person types them into a live session.\n\nStudents pick spyware for every collection tool. Spyware is the broader habit of hidden collection, including screens and files. Choose the keylogger answer when the evidence is specifically the recording of keystrokes, especially bursts tied to a password box. A logic bomb waits for a date and then does damage. A rootkit's specialty is hiding the view, not the act of recording keys.\n",
        'r0': "\nSoftware hooks the keyboard path, often with a WH_KEYBOARD hook, or polls key state and flushes a buffer when a form is submitted. On BIL04 the flush was a small outbound POST aligned with each billing login. Record the DLL name, the DLL's SHA-256, and one timestamp that matches Nia's login. A hardware logger on the USB cable has no DLL and creates no process-creation Event 4688. If bursts continue after a clean image, inspect the cable before you reimage again. Passwords captured this way must be reset from a different machine. Full-disk encryption does not cover keystrokes entered after unlock. Spyware is the wider collection category. Use the keylogger label when the artifact is the key stream itself.\n",
        'r1': '\nThis tool records the keys a person presses. Passwords are included because passwords are typed. Software can hook the path from the keyboard. A small device can sit on the cable instead. A cable device may show no running program. Short outbound bursts can match each login. Each burst lines up with typing in a form. Unplug the computer from the shared network. Save the extra program file before removal. Check the keyboard cable for a strange adapter. Change passwords from a different clean computer. Disk encryption does not stop a live capture. Broader spying may take screens as well. Choose this label when the keys themselves are taken.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic puts a second, thinner sheet under the sign-in clipboard. Every letter a patient writes presses through onto that sheet. The visible form still looks normal, and the patient still reaches the desk. At the end of each sign-in the thin sheet is slipped into an envelope and handed to a stranger outside. A camera photo of the whole room would be a broader kind of watching. This sheet only cares about the letters. Staff who find the sheet should bag the sheet before anyone throws it away, and they should rewrite any secret that was written while the sheet was there. The thin sheet is the keylogger. The letters pressed through are the passwords and the other typed text.\n',
        'r4': '\nPair one login time with the outbound burst that followed, and write down both. Record the extra DLL name and hash, or note that no new process exists. Check the keyboard cable and any inline adapter before you reimage. Isolate the host. Do not type fresh passwords on that keyboard. Copy the capture buffer or the packet sample first. Reset affected passwords from a clean machine. If you find a hardware device, bag the device and leave the device out of production.\n',
        'exam': '\nRecording keystrokes, especially bursts tied to a login form, is a keylogger. Hardware on the cable still counts, even with an empty task list. Spyware is the broader collection of screens, files, or history. Pick spyware when the question is not specifically about keys. A rootkit hides what local tools display. A logic bomb waits for a condition and then causes damage. Full-disk encryption does not catch keys typed into a live session. Isolating the host is containment. Removing the hook or the device after the sample is saved is eradication.\n',
    },
    'Logic bombs': {
        'body': "\nA logic bomb is code that stays quiet until a condition is true, then does harm. The condition can be a date, a file that appears, or the moment a particular person's account is disabled. Until that test passes, the system looks ordinary. The harm is intended by the person who planted the test. Nothing has to infect neighboring files, and nothing has to scan the subnet.\n\nThe code is often a script or a scheduled job that an insider was allowed to edit. Each run checks the condition and exits while the condition is false. On the day the test succeeds, the job deletes shares, corrupts a database, or encrypts a folder. The trigger is the interesting part. A script in a finance folder that deletes shares when one employee's account is disabled is the pattern to remember. The account-disable event is the fuse, and the deletion is the charge.\n\nFinance admin Chris had rights to a folder of nightly scripts. The script reconcile.py checks whether the account chris is disabled and, only then, deletes the department share. The check sat in the file for months and did nothing, because the account was active. On the Friday Chris is dismissed, the identity team disables the account at 16:40. The script's next run deletes the share. Change history shows Chris edited reconcile.py in March, and the delete command sits in the same function as the account check. Write down the condition, the destructive command, and the account event that made the condition true.\n\nDetection is a diff against the approved script, not a scan that only knows published file signatures. A file-integrity check on the script directory would have flagged the March edit. Least privilege would have stopped one finance admin from being the only person who could change production jobs. When the job fires, containment means stopping the scheduled task and keeping a copy of the script. Restore the share from backup only after that copy exists. Do not edit the script in place before the copy is stored. Eradication means removing the malicious check and requiring a second person on production job changes.\n\nA virus or a worm is the wrong name for a destructive script that never replicates. A virus rides a host file and infects more files. A worm spreads by itself. A keylogger records typing. A logic bomb is about waiting for a condition. When the clue is a date, a departure, or a disabled account, and the damage starts only then, choose the logic bomb.\n",
        'r0': "\nThe condition is the mechanism. reconcile.py tested whether account chris was disabled and only then deleted the department share. The identity log's disable event, commonly 4725, at 16:40 is the fuse, and the script's next scheduled run is the detonation. The March diff that placed the check beside the delete is the planting. There is no replication step and no port scan. Copy the script and a SHA-256 of the script before anyone edits the file. Stopping the scheduled task is containment. Removing the check and requiring two-person control on production scripts is eradication. A virus would have attached to other files by noon. A worm would have appeared on a second host without a condition. The quiet months before the account was disabled are part of the evidence.\n",
        'r1': '\nThis code waits until a condition becomes true. The condition can be a date or a departure. A disabled account can be the trigger. Until then the system looks ordinary. The harm was planned by whoever planted the code. A finance script can delete shares on that cue. The script does not need to infect other files. The script does not need to travel by itself. Compare the script with the approved copy. Save the script before anyone edits the file. Stop the scheduled job so the job cannot run. A virus rides a file and infects more files. A worm spreads on its own.\n',
        'r2': "\nA warehouse sprinkler is tied to a calendar and to one employee's badge. Most nights the sprinkler checks the calendar, sees the wrong date, and stays dry. The stock looks safe for months. On the evening that badge is revoked, the next check opens the valve and soaks one aisle. Nothing spread to the building next door. The harm was sitting in the valve the whole time, waiting for a condition the planter chose. Photograph the valve linkage before anyone cuts a wire. The sprinkler is the hidden script. The revoked badge is the disabled account, and the soaked aisle is the share deleted only after that condition becomes true.\n",
        'r4': '\nOpen the script diff and write the condition beside the destructive command. Record the account event or the date that made the condition true, and the scheduled run that followed. Hash the script before anyone edits the file. Disable the scheduled task. Do not fix the file in place first. Restore damaged data from backup only after the script copy is stored. Note who could edit production jobs alone, and leave that access unchanged until the ticket records the name.\n',
        'exam': '\nHarm that starts only when a date, an event, or a disabled account becomes true is a logic bomb. Quiet months beforehand do not make the code harmless. A virus must infect other files from a host. A worm must spread by itself. A keylogger must record typing. Do not pick ransomware unless a lock and a payment demand are present. Stopping the scheduled task is containment. Removing the malicious check after a copy is saved is eradication. The insider edit right explains the planting and is not a separate species of program.\n',
    },
    'Rootkits': {
        'body': "\nA rootkit hides software, files, or network connections, often by lying to the tools an analyst would use to find them. The task list, the file browser, and even some security tools on the live system can be fed a cleaned-up picture. The lie is the definition. The hidden payload might be a remote-control tool, a recorder, or a locker. The hiding layer still has its own name.\n\nUser-mode kits tamper with the interfaces programs call when they ask what is running. Kernel-mode kits load a driver and intercept those questions lower down, so careful tools on the live system can still be misled. The hidden process can talk on the network anyway. Packets leave the machine, and a switch mirror or a firewall log sees the packets, because the network device does not ask the infected kernel for permission to notice a packet. The gap between a calm task list and a live connection is the classic clue.\n\nOn host LAB17, Task Manager and a local process listing show the usual agents and nothing else. During the same minute, the firewall records a repeating outbound session from LAB17 to an address no other lab machine uses. A directory taken by booting from trusted media shows a driver file that the live system refused to list. The ticket should say that local tools omitted the process, the firewall still saw the session, and the offline listing showed the driver. Those three lines prove the view was a lie.\n\nDo not trust a cleanup tool that asks the suspected operating system what remains. Capture memory if the host must stay up briefly, then boot from trusted media and copy the hidden files from outside. Network isolation of LAB17 is containment, so the hidden process cannot keep calling out. Eradication means removing the driver and the hidden payload from an offline copy, or rebuilding the machine when the kernel cannot be trusted. A host sensor that only asks the local kernel what is running can be the tool that was blinded. Prefer a second vantage point: network logs, an external disk image, or a view the guest cannot edit.\n\nStudents confuse a rootkit with a trojan. A trojan's trick is to look useful so someone will run it. After launch, a trojan may or may not hide. Choose a rootkit when ordinary tools on the host present a false clean picture while an outside log still shows the activity. A logic bomb waits for a condition and then acts. Hiding is a different behavior from waiting.\n",
        'r0': '\nThe lie sits on the query path. A kernel driver intercepts process and file listings, so Task Manager on LAB17 stays clean while the firewall still shows a repeating outbound session on TCP 443 from that host. An offline listing, after a boot from trusted media, reveals the driver the live system hid. Capture memory if the host must stay up for a few minutes, then isolate the host. Do not run a removal tool inside the suspected kernel and treat a clean local report as proof. Compare three views for the same minute: the local process list, the external connection log, and the offline disk. The mismatch is the finding. A trojan is defined by a useful disguise at launch. A rootkit is defined by the false report after installation.\n',
        'r1': '\nThis software hides from the tools that should show it. The task list on the computer can be a lie. Files you expect to see may stay hidden. The hidden program can still talk outward. A network log may show a call the task list hides. Boot from trusted media before you trust the disk list. Do not trust a cleanup that asks the live system. Cut the machine off the network. Save an outside copy of the disk first. A useful-looking lure is a different trick. Hiding the truth from local tools is this problem. Waiting for a date is not the same behavior.\n',
        'r2': "\nA building's hallway monitors show an empty corridor all evening. The pictures look smooth and convincing. Meanwhile a door sensor, watched from a guard shack across the street, keeps clicking because someone is walking. The monitors were not broken by accident. A box behind the panel is feeding the monitors a stand-in picture. Guards who only stare at the lobby screen will write that the hall was quiet. Guards who compare the screen with the outside sensor will not. Unplug the panel and look behind it before anyone trusts the screen again. The stand-in picture is the false report local tools receive. The outside sensor is the network log that still sees the hidden activity.\n",
        'r4': "\nCompare three lists for the same minute: the local process list, the firewall view of the host's connections, and an offline file listing from trusted boot media. Write down the outbound address that local tools omit. Copy memory or the hidden driver before you delete anything. Isolate the host. Do not trust a removal tool that asks the live system what remains. Rebuild if you cannot trust the kernel. Record that local tools disagreed with the outside log, because that disagreement is the evidence.\n",
        'exam': '\nA clean task list that contradicts an outside connection log or an offline disk listing is a rootkit. The lie to local tools is the defining fact. A trojan is a useful-looking lure and may not hide at all. A logic bomb waits for a condition, then does damage where people can see it. Spyware collects data without falsifying the task list. Cutting the host off the network is containment for the hidden caller. Removing the driver from an offline copy, or rebuilding, is eradication. A cleanup that only asks the infected system proves nothing.\n',
    },
    'Physical brute force': {
        'body': '\nPhysical brute force means breaking a barrier by force. A kicked door, a smashed lock, a cut fence, or a rammed gate fits the definition. The attacker does not trick a reader and does not guess a password. The lock, the frame, or the gate is damaged until the way is open. What remains afterward is broken material, not a cloned credential and not a long list of failed logins.\n\nForce leaves traces a camera and a sensor can agree on. A door contact opens, the frame splinters, and an alarm sensor may be ripped off so the circuit cannot be trusted. The badge log for that door shows no valid read at the same minute, because no badge was used. A password-guessing storm would show thousands of failures against one account in an authentication log. That storm is a different topic, aimed at a secret, and the storm does not splinter wood. Mixing the two meanings of brute force is the usual exam mistake.\n\nFacilities staff find the clinic rear door at 06:30. The frame near the strike is split, the cylinder is on the floor, and the magnetic contact hangs by a wire. The door controller shows the contact change at 02:14 and no successful badge read. The camera above the door shows a person using a tool on the lock and then entering. Write the controller time, the absence of a badge event, and the physical damage. Those three items separate a forced entry from a staff member who only propped the door.\n\nTreat the room as unsecured until it is cleared, then preserve the camera clip and the damaged hardware. Replace the door and the sensor after the photos exist, and review what an intruder could reach in those hours: servers, keys, or paperwork. A stronger frame, hinges that are not exposed on the outside, a contact that alarms when the circuit is cut, and cameras on the rear approach all help before the next attempt. None of that work is a password policy. A longer password would not have held this door.\n\nRFID cloning is the neighbor students choose when any door is involved. A copied badge opens an undamaged door, and the same badge number can appear in two places. Environmental harm uses heat, water, or power and leaves the frame intact. When the evidence is a broken frame, a smashed lock, or a ripped sensor, and the badge log is quiet, the entry was physical force.\n',
        'r0': '\nForce is the mechanism, and the artifacts are physical. At 02:14 the rear door contact opened, the frame at the strike was splintered, the cylinder was on the floor, and the magnetic sensor was ripped off the wire. The door controller recorded the contact change and zero successful badge reads. Match the camera clock to 02:14 so the clip and the controller tell the same minute. Password guessing would instead show a storm of failures, often Event 4625, against one account, with no broken wood. A cloned badge would show a valid read and an intact frame. Write the damage, the missing badge event, and the camera time on the ticket. Photograph the hardware before anyone repairs the strike. A longer password does not address this entry.\n',
        'r1': '\nSomeone breaks a door, a lock, or a gate by force. The person does not trick the reader. The frame can end up splintered. The lock can end up on the floor. A sensor can be ripped off the frame. The badge record shows no successful read. A camera may show the tool and the entry. Password guessing would fill a login record instead. A copied badge would leave the door intact. Save the camera clip and the broken parts. Check what a person could reach inside. A stronger frame helps before the next attempt. Broken material is the evidence to trust.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic locks the rear door for the night. An intruder does not talk to the badge reader and does not guess a word. The intruder hits the frame until the strike splits, the lock falls, and the little sensor rips off the screws. The doorway is open because the material failed. In the morning the wood tells the story, and the reader has no successful badge to offer. A copied card would have left the paint unbroken. Changing a password on the office computer would not have held this frame. Photos of the split come before the carpenter arrives. The splintered frame is forced entry. The quiet badge reader shows that no token was used.\n',
        'r4': '\nPhotograph the splintered frame, the lock hardware, and the ripped sensor before any repair. Write down the door-controller time and the badge result, including a result of no read. Match the camera clip to that time and export the clip. Do not remount the sensor until the photos exist. List the rooms the open door could reach, and check whether keys or equipment moved. Keep the broken hardware. Do not call the case a password incident unless an authentication log actually shows a guessing storm.\n',
        'exam': '\nSplintered frames, smashed locks, rammed gates, and ripped sensors are physical brute force. A quiet badge log supports that reading. Password brute force is a storm of guesses against one account, with no broken door. RFID cloning leaves the door intact and shows a credential the reader accepted, often in two places at once. Heat, water, cold, or power point to an environmental attack, not a broken strike. A longer password is not the control for a kicked door. Preserve photos before the frame is repaired.\n',
    },
    'RFID cloning': {
        'body': "\nRFID cloning copies a badge or a tag so a second token behaves like the real one. The door opens for the copy. Nothing has to be smashed. The reader sees a credential the reader already trusts, because the copy presents the same identifier, or a response the reader accepts, as the card on the employee's lanyard.\n\nMany older proximity badges speak a simple identifier in the clear when a reader energizes them at close range. A visitor who stands near a badge, with a small reader in a bag, can capture that identifier and write the identifier onto a blank card. The encounter takes seconds and looks like someone waiting in line. Stronger cards do not hand over a reusable secret that way. Stronger cards run a challenge and a response, and a casual copy of the visible number is not enough. After a successful clone, the symptom is impossible presence. The same badge number is accepted at two doors at once, or at a door the owner could not have reached.\n\nReception keeps a 125 kHz proximity badge, number 10482, on a lanyard. A visitor leans in to ask for directions and holds a bag against that lanyard. That afternoon the access log shows badge 10482 at the loading dock while the owner is badged into the third-floor office, and the two reads are four seconds apart. Both doors are undamaged. No password failed. The ticket fact is one credential in two places at once, plus the earlier moment a stranger stood close enough to energize the badge.\n\nContainment means disabling badge 10482 in the controller so the original and the copy both stop. Issue a new credential on a card that answers a challenge instead of speaking a fixed number. Do not take the card from the employee and hand the same number back, because the copy still works until the number is revoked. A PIN on sensitive doors means a copied identifier is not sufficient. An alert on the same badge at two readers inside a walk time no person can meet turns the afternoon log into a page instead of an audit surprise.\n\nPhysical brute force would have broken the frame. Heat, water, or power aimed at a room would be an environmental attack. Password spraying guesses logins and never speaks to a door reader. When the door is intact and a copied token explains the opens, the topic is cloning.\n",
        'r0': "\nOlder 125 kHz proximity badges transmit a facility code and a card number when a reader powers them from a few centimeters away. A handheld reader can capture badge 10482 and write that number onto a blank token. Stronger 13.56 MHz cards that run a challenge and response, such as a modern DESFire exchange, do not yield a reusable static number to a casual skim. The detection figure is temporal: the same credential accepted at two readers four seconds apart, which no person can walk. Disable 10482 in the controller immediately so the original and the clone both fail. Issue a card that does not speak a static identifier. Alert when one badge hits two readers inside a shorter interval than the doors' walk time. A splintered frame would be force, not a clone.\n",
        'r1': '\nA copied badge opens the door like the real one. Nothing on the frame has to break. Some older badges speak their number nearby. A stranger standing close can capture that number. A blank card can then act as the original. The same badge number can show at two doors at once. The owner cannot be in both places. Disable that badge number right away. Issue a new card that answers a challenge. Add a second check on important doors. Alert when one badge appears in two places. A smashed door is a different problem. Heat or water aimed at a room is different too.\n',
        'r2': "\nAt a clinic desk, a visitor stands close to a nurse's badge and quietly copies the pattern that opens the side door. Later two people present that same pattern at two doors, seconds apart, and both doors open. The frames are unmarked. The reader is doing what the reader was built to do, because the copy speaks the pattern the nurse's badge speaks. Taking the nurse's badge home does not stop the copy. The pattern has to be retired and replaced with one that answers a question instead of shouting a fixed number. The copied pattern is the cloned badge. Two doors opening at once are the log entry that shows one credential in two places.\n",
        'r4': '\nPull the access log and write down both readers, both times, and the badge number that appears twice. Confirm the owner could not have walked the distance between those reads. Disable that badge number in the controller before a long investigation. Do not hand the same number back and call the badge fixed, because the copy still matches. Note that the frames are unbroken so the case is not later called a forced door. Record any moment a stranger stood close to the badge. Order a replacement that does not transmit a static number.\n',
        'exam': "\nA second token that the reader accepts as the real badge is cloning. The same badge number at two doors at once, with intact frames, is the classic sign. Physical brute force breaks the door and does not need a valid read. Heat, cold, water, or power do not copy a token. Password spraying guesses logins and never speaks to a badge reader. Disabling the copied number stops the clone. Taking the employee's card home while the number stays active does not. A static identifier captured nearby is the weak mechanism.\n",
    },
    'Environmental attacks': {
        'body': "\nEnvironmental attacks use heat, cold, water, humidity, or power to damage systems or to force a bad decision. The target is the room the computers depend on, not a password and not a badge. A machine that shuts down to protect itself, or a sensor that drives an evacuation of a rack, can be the attacker's goal. People under pressure then bypass a control, open a door, or fail over to a weaker site.\n\nClosets and small server rooms trust a few sensors and a cooling unit. A portable heater aimed at the temperature sensor makes the reading climb until gear hits a thermal cutoff or an alarm clears the rack. A water leak aimed at a floor sensor, or a breaker that is thrown, sits in the same family. The logs show environment alarms, not a stream of bad passwords. The door hardware may be undamaged and the systems may still be down. A careful attacker aims at the sensor the shutdown logic believes, not at the server lid.\n\nFacilities logs for closet B show the inlet temperature rising from a steady 22 C to 40 C in twenty minutes, starting at 18:05. A portable heater is on the floor, plugged into the housekeeping outlet, with the nozzle toward the sensor rather than the hallway. Servers in that closet shut down on the thermal threshold, and the after-hours clinic lost the local file share. No door was forced. The hall camera shows a person entering with a valid badge and leaving the heater behind. The ticket should record the temperature curve, the heater's aim, and the shutdown time.\n\nKill the heater, restore cooling, and bring systems up only after the temperature is back in range. Preserve the camera clip and the sensor log before the room is tidied. Treat the badge that entered as a lead, because the environment was the weapon and the access is a separate question. Lock housekeeping outlets, place sensors where a passerby cannot bake them, alarm on a fast temperature rise rather than only on a high final value, and keep a UPS so a thrown breaker is not an instant outage. A page on the rise gives someone the twenty minutes.\n\nPhysical brute force breaks the lock or the door. Here the door can be intact and the damage comes from the room. RFID cloning copies a badge and does not heat a sensor. When the evidence is a heater, a flood, a freeze, or a power cut aimed at equipment, choose the environmental attack.\n",
        'r0': "\nThe weapon is the room. Closet B's inlet sensor rose from 22 C to 40 C in twenty minutes, starting at 18:05, with a portable heater aimed at the sensor from a housekeeping outlet. Servers shut down on their thermal cutoff, and the local file share went with them. The door log can show a valid badge and no forced contact. That valid badge is a lead, not the definition of the attack. The temperature curve, the heater's aim, and the shutdown timestamp define the case. Alarming on a rapid rise, not only on a final high threshold, pages someone during those twenty minutes. Locked outlets and a UPS address the easy versions. A smashed lock is physical force. A dual badge read is cloning. Heat, water, cold, or power selects this topic.\n",
        'r1': '\nHeat, cold, water, or power is used as the weapon. The room is the target, not a password. A heater can be aimed at a temperature sensor. The reading climbs and machines shut themselves down. The door can stay perfectly intact. Logs show temperature or power alarms. The logs do not show a long run of bad passwords. Remove the heater and cool the room first. Bring machines back only after the room is safe. Save the sensor log and the camera clip. Alarm when the temperature rises fast. A broken lock is a different kind of harm. A copied badge does not heat a closet.\n',
        'r2': "\nA clinic stores medicine in a cooled closet. Someone sets a portable heater on the floor and aims the heater at the thermostat, not at the medicine. The thermostat believes the room is far too hot and shuts the equipment down. The door lock is untouched. The harm came from the air and from the sensor the room trusts. A kicked door would show broken wood. A copied badge would show two entries and a normal temperature. Staff should photograph the heater's aim and the temperature chart before anyone moves the heater. The heater on the sensor is an attack on the room's conditions. The shutdown is equipment protecting itself from heat the attacker created.\n",
        'r4': '\nRecord the sensor name, the starting temperature, the peak, and the minutes between them. Photograph the heater, the outlet, and the direction the heater points before you unplug it. Export the shutdown time of the gear. Do not power the servers back on until the room is cool. Note whether the door was forced. Trace the badge that entered, and treat the badge as a lead rather than the whole explanation. Save the camera clip. Do not stop at a hardware-failure label while a heater is aimed at the sensor.\n',
        'exam': '\nHeat, cold, water, humidity, or power used to damage systems or force a shutdown is an environmental attack. A heater aimed at a closet sensor fits, even when a valid badge opened an intact door. Physical brute force requires a broken barrier. RFID cloning requires a copied token and does not move the temperature. A credential may explain how the person entered. The credential does not rename the heater. Bring systems up only after conditions are safe. The sensor curve is the evidence. A password change does not answer a thermal cutoff.\n',
    },
    'Distributed denial of service': {
        'body': "\nA distributed denial of service is an attack that uses many computers to make a service unavailable. The attack exhausts bandwidth, open connections, or CPU until real users cannot get through. A distributed denial of service does not keep servers healthy. Success for the attacker is a failed service, not a repaired one.\n\nThe flood can saturate the link, fill a table of half-open connections, or spend CPU on requests that look almost legitimate. Many sources take part, which is what distributed adds to a denial of service from one machine. Some floods reflect off third parties. The attacker sends a small query with a forged source address, and an open service sends a much larger reply to the victim. Open DNS resolvers become reflectors when they answer the whole internet. Reflected traffic is still this attack, because the goal is to drown the victim. Filtering, rate limits, anycast absorption, and closing open DNS recursion are defenses. None of those phrases names the attack. Closing recursion stops your resolver from becoming a fire hose aimed at someone else.\n\nAt 09:10 the clinic booking site stops loading for patients. The edge router shows inbound traffic rising from about 200 megabits per second to several gigabits per second, from thousands of addresses. The booking process on the origin host is still running, and the CPU chart sits near a normal morning, because the link is full and most attack packets never reach that process. Patients still cannot book. A calm CPU chart is a misleading picture. The chart is not evidence that the attack spared the servers or kept the servers healthy. The service is unavailable to the people who need it. The ticket should record the clock time, the traffic rate, and the number of sources.\n\nDefenders ask the upstream provider to filter the flood, place rate limits on the exposed service, and, where the design allows, steer the name toward an anycast network that can absorb the volume. SYN cookies keep a connection table from being the scarce resource during a SYN flood. If the clinic's own resolvers answer recursive queries for the entire internet, close that recursion so the clinic is not the next reflector. Those steps reduce an attack. Those steps are not the attack. A single internal job that pegs one CPU, with no crowd of outside sources, is a different incident.\n\nStudents lose the question by naming a DNS attack, or by naming a defense as if the defense were the flood. Cache poisoning, spoofed DNS answers, and hijacked name records send people to the wrong host. Those techniques are not a bandwidth flood. DNSSEC only validates that a DNS response is authentic, and that check leaves a volumetric flood untouched. When the site is unreachable under traffic from many sources, the answer is this attack, including the case where an origin CPU chart still looks ordinary.\n",
        'r0': '\nSuccess for this attack is an unavailable service. In the booking incident, inbound traffic moved from about 200 Mbps to several Gbps from thousands of source addresses, and patients could not load the site. Origin CPU stayed near the morning baseline because the scarce resource was bandwidth, not the booking process. Other cases exhaust the connection table with a SYN flood or exhaust CPU with heavy requests. Reflection enlarges a flood: a small spoofed query to an open resolver on UDP port 53 draws a larger answer onto the victim. Defenses, and only defenses, include upstream filtering, rate limits, anycast scrubbing, SYN cookies, and closing open recursion so your resolver is not the reflector. DNSSEC does not drop that volumetric flood. A calm CPU chart does not mean the servers were kept healthy.\n',
        'r1': '\nThis attack tries to make a service unavailable. Many sources take part at the same time. The sources can fill the pipe, the connections, or the processor. Real customers cannot get through. The attack does not keep servers healthy. A calm chart on one screen can mislead you. The service has still failed for the public. Filtering and speed limits are defenses. Spreading the load across many sites is a defense. Closing a loose name server is a defense, not the attack. A lying name lookup is a different attack. Checking that a name answer is real stops no flood.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic opens the front doors for the morning patients. A hired crowd fills every doorway and the sidewalk, shoulder to shoulder, so people with appointments cannot step inside. Exam rooms down the hall can look tidy, and a nurse who glances only at an empty room might think the building is fine. The crowd did not come to clean or to heal anyone. The crowd came to use up the entrances until care stops. Ushers who filter the sidewalk, limit how fast people approach, and spread arrivals across many doors are defending the entrance. The crowd is the flood from many sources. A blocked doorway is exhausted bandwidth, and a tidy exam room is a console that can look calm while the service has already failed.\n',
        'r4': '\nRecord the start time, the traffic rate before and after, and how many source addresses appear. Note whether the scarce resource is bandwidth, the connection table, or CPU. If origin CPU looks ordinary while users are blocked, write that down and still call the service unavailable. Do not label rate limiting, filtering, anycast, or closed recursion as the attack. Those are defenses. Ask the upstream provider to filter. If your resolver answers the whole internet, plan to close recursion, and do not expect a signature check on name answers to shrink a bandwidth flood.\n',
        'exam': '\nThis attack makes a service unavailable by exhausting bandwidth, connections, or CPU from many sources. The attack does not keep servers healthy. A calm CPU chart while customers are blocked still means the attack succeeded. Rate limiting, filtering, anycast, and closing open DNS recursion are defenses, not names of the attack. Cache poisoning and hijacking send people to the wrong address. Those are DNS attacks. DNSSEC validates that a response is authentic and does not stop a volumetric flood. A flood is not a defense. Choose the flood when many sources make the service unreachable.\n',
    },
    'DNS attacks': {
        'body': "\nDNS attacks corrupt or redirect name lookups so people reach an attacker while typing a name they trust. The real service at the true address can be fully up. Users still land on the wrong host, because the answer they received was false or the place they trust for answers was taken over. The harm is a lie about a name, not a pipe filled with junk traffic.\n\nThree patterns cover the questions. Cache poisoning inserts a forged answer into a resolver, which then remembers the lie until the cached record expires. The forged reply has to match the query the resolver is waiting on, including the transaction identifier, so the attacker races a spoofed response. Hijacking changes the name's delegation at a registrar, swaps the resolver a computer uses, or edits a hosts file, so the lookup is steered before a cache is involved. A spoofed response is the fake packet itself, aimed at a resolver or at a client that will accept the packet. In each pattern a trusted name maps to an unexpected address.\n\nStaff who type the payroll name reach a lookalike site. On the clinic resolver, the cached record for that name points at an address that is not in the change ticket, and the time to live still has hours left. A query sent straight to the authoritative server, from a machine that does not use the poisoned resolver, returns the address operations recorded last month. The two answers disagree. The ticket should quote both addresses, the resolver that served the lie, and the remaining cache lifetime. Browsers may also show a certificate warning when the lookalike lacks a certificate the clients already trust.\n\nDNSSEC validates that a response is authentic. Signed records let a validating resolver reject a forged answer that lacks a valid signature chain. Turn validation on at resolvers you run, and sign zones you publish. That check stops acceptance of a poisoned or spoofed lie. A failed signature does not stop a volumetric flood, because a flood is a capacity problem and rejected packets can still fill the pipe. Closing open recursion is a defense used so your server is not a reflector in someone else's flood. Closing recursion is not cache poisoning, spoofing, or hijacking. Detection here is a disagreement between a resolver and a known-good authoritative answer, plus unexpected changes at the registrar.\n\nA distributed flood from many sources, aimed at exhausting bandwidth or CPU, is a denial-of-service attack. Rate limits, filtering, and anycast defend against that flood, and those controls are not the name of a poisoned cache. When the harm is a wrong address for a right name, choose the DNS attack, and treat DNSSEC as the authenticity control rather than a flood control.\n",
        'r0': "\nCache poisoning forges an answer a resolver then stores. The attacker races a spoofed response that must match the outstanding query, including the 16-bit transaction ID, and the resolver keeps the lie for the record's TTL. Hijacking changes registrar name servers, a hosts file, or the resolver address handed out by DHCP. A spoofed response is the fake packet in flight. In the payroll case the poisoned resolver returned an address the authoritative server did not serve. DNSSEC lets a validating resolver check signatures and reject that lie. The authenticity check does not absorb a gigabit flood of UDP port 53. Closing open recursion stops your server from amplifying someone else's denial-of-service flood. Closing recursion does not prove a payroll answer is authentic. Quote both addresses and the remaining TTL on the ticket.\n",
        'r1': '\nThese attacks make a trusted name point somewhere wrong. People type a name they trust. People arrive at an attacker instead. A poisoned memory remembers a forged answer. A spoofed reply is a fake message. Hijacking steals the place that gives answers. The real service can stay up the whole time. Compare the answer with a source you trust. A signature check shows the answer is authentic. That check does not stop a flood of traffic. A crush of traffic from many places is different. Speed limits and filters defend against a flood. Those controls are not the name of this lie.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic phone book lists the pharmacy number. Someone slips a forged page into the book at the front desk, or steals the book and reprints the pharmacy line. Callers dial a name they trust and reach a stranger, while the real pharmacy phone keeps working. A wax seal from the real publisher lets the desk reject a page that lacks the seal. The seal does not push a mob off the sidewalk. Extra doors and a limit on how fast people may enter are how a mob is handled. The forged page is a poisoned or hijacked name lookup. The wax seal is the authenticity check on an answer, and the seal does nothing to clear a flood.\n',
        'r4': '\nQuery the suspected resolver and write down the address, the remaining lifetime, and the time. Query an authoritative server that did not use that resolver, and write that answer beside the first. If the answers disagree, save both. Check whether signature validation is on, and do not turn that validation off to make the name load. Look for registrar changes, hosts-file edits, and a resolver address you did not assign. Record the two answers before you close open recursion. Do not call the incident a bandwidth flood unless the pipe is actually full.\n',
        'exam': '\nCache poisoning, spoofed responses, and hijacking are DNS attacks. A trusted name points at the wrong place while the real service can still be up. DNSSEC validates that a response is authentic. DNSSEC does not stop a volumetric flood. A flood from many sources that exhausts bandwidth or CPU is a distributed denial of service. Filtering, rate limits, anycast, and closing open recursion defend against that flood. Those defenses are not cache poisoning. Two different addresses for one name select the DNS attack. A full pipe and an unreachable service do not.\n',
    },
    'Wireless attacks': {
        'body': "\nWireless attacks abuse the radio rather than a name record or a flooded website. The set to remember is a rogue access point, an evil twin, and cracking a weak passphrase from nearby. Each one depends on being close enough to hear a network or to advertise one. The lobby and the parking lot matter as much as the server room.\n\nA rogue access point is an unauthorized radio plugged into a network you trust, sometimes by an employee who wanted better coverage. Whoever associates to that radio is bridged toward the inside. An evil twin advertises the same network name as the real clinic, often with a stronger signal, and does not have the clinic's sign-in behind it. Laptops join the name that looks familiar. Cracking a weak passphrase is usually done offline. The attacker captures the handshake clients already send, then tests dictionary guesses against that capture without talking to the access point again. A short or common passphrase falls. A long random passphrase does not. Bursts that knock clients off the radio can force a reconnect so the handshake can be captured. Those bursts support the crack. The bursts are not a separate topic.\n\nIn the lobby, a nurse's laptop shows two networks that both carry the clinic name. The laptop joins the stronger signal. That radio is a small travel router on a side table, not one of the ceiling access points. The wireless controller's list of legitimate radios does not include that hardware address, and the laptop's next sign-in page is not a page the clinic hosts. The ticket should record the radio's hardware address, the lobby location, the association time, and whether any password was typed into the fake page. If a handshake capture is part of the case, note that the shared passphrase was a short clinic slogan, which is why an offline guess was realistic.\n\nRemove the rogue radio, rotate any passphrase or user password that may have been typed, and put the real network on a design with per-user credentials rather than one shared slogan. WPA3 is the stronger choice when the clients allow it, and WPA2 with individual accounts removes the single shared secret. Wireless detection should alert when a second radio advertises the clinic name or when an unknown radio appears on the corporate side of the wire. Turn off push-button joining features that make a rogue easy to use. A guest network belongs on its own segment, so a lobby radio cannot open a path to payroll even if someone plugs the radio in.\n\nA poisoned name record does not require a second radio with the clinic's name. A flood from thousands of addresses is a denial-of-service problem on a link, not a lookalike network in the lobby. When the question has an impersonated network name, a rogue radio, or a weak passphrase cracked from nearby, stay with the wireless attack.\n",
        'r0': "\nAn evil twin advertises the clinic network name with a hardware address absent from the controller's authorized list. The lobby laptop associated to the stronger signal. A rogue access point is the related case: the radio is plugged into the trusted network. Passphrase cracking captures the WPA2 four-way handshake, often after deauthentication frames force a reconnect, then tests guesses offline. A short slogan falls. A long random passphrase does not. WPA2-Enterprise with 802.1X removes the shared secret, and WPA3 is the stronger choice when clients support it. Record the unknown hardware address, the channel, and the association time. Rotate credentials typed into the twin's page. Name poisoning would change an address for a name with no second radio. A multi-gigabit flood would show on the edge router, not as a second network name.\n",
        'r1': "\nThese attacks use the radio near the building. An unwanted unit can be plugged in without permission. A copycat can reuse your network's name. Laptops join the copy with the stronger signal. A weak shared password can be guessed from nearby. The guess can use a saved sign-in exchange. The lobby is a common place for this trick. Remove the extra radio from the room. Change any password people typed into the copy. Prefer a long unique secret or separate logins. Watch for a second copy of your network name. A lying name book is a different problem. A crowd blocking a website is different too.\n",
        'r2': '\nA clinic has one real reception desk. Someone sets a second desk in the lobby, paints the same name on the sign, and speaks a little louder. Visitors walk to the louder desk and tell that desk their secrets, because the sign matches. Separately, a stranger near the real desk listens once to a short spoken code and later tries common phrases against what was heard, without asking the desk to repeat the morning. A long random code does not fall to that guessing. Taking the second desk away, and refusing a shared slogan, puts visitors back at the real counter. The second desk is the copycat radio using the clinic name. The guessed code is the weak shared passphrase cracked from nearby.\n',
        'r4': "\nFrom the lobby, record every network name, hardware address, signal, and channel you can see. Mark any hardware address missing from the controller's authorized list. Write down which laptop joined, and whether a password was typed into a page the clinic does not host. Do not join the unknown radio from an admin laptop. Remove the rogue hardware after the notes exist. Rotate secrets that may have been entered. If a saved sign-in exchange is part of the case, record that the shared passphrase was weak and retire it.\n",
        'exam': '\nA rogue access point, an evil twin using your network name, or a weak passphrase cracked from nearby is a wireless attack. A second radio in the lobby is the clue. Cache poisoning changes an address for a name and needs no radio. A multi-source flood that blocks a website is a distributed denial of service. Rate limits and anycast do not answer an evil twin. The impersonated radio selects this topic. Remove the extra radio and retire the shared slogan. A signature check on name answers does not fix a lookalike network name.\n',
    },
    'On-path attacks': {
        'body': "\nAn on-path attack places the attacker between two parties so the traffic can be read or changed as the traffic flows. The attacker is in the conversation, not merely presenting a token that was stolen earlier. Both sides believe they are talking to each other. The attacker forwards what arrives and sometimes edits the bytes before forwarding them. Older notes call the same position a man in the middle.\n\nOn a local network the usual setup poisons address mappings so each victim sends frames to the attacker, who copies the frames and passes them on. On an open cafe network the attacker sits on the same wireless segment and relays sessions that have no protection. When the login page is plain and unprotected, the password and the session token cross the attacker in the clear, and the relay can change a line in a request. Protection that uses a certificate the client actually checks closes that reading and that quiet editing. A warning the user clicks through reopens the path, because the user has accepted the attacker's certificate. The attacker must stay in the path for every packet they want to change. Leaving the path ends the ability to edit the next packet.\n\nSam joins the cafe network and signs into clinic webmail through an old bookmark that still uses an unprotected page on port 80. A tool on the next table relays that session. The webmail password appears in the clear on the relay, and a message Sam did not write shows up in the sent folder during the same session. The clinic mail log shows the account authenticated from the cafe address. The concrete fact is the unprotected login plus a change Sam did not make while the session was live. No second copy of a token is used hours later from another city. The alteration happened in the middle of that conversation.\n\nUse protected transport everywhere, and a browser rule that refuses the old plaintext bookmark on the next visit. Use a private tunnel when staff must work from a cafe. On the clinic LAN, controls that block forged address announcements make the local redirection harder. Those controls are defenses. Detection is a certificate warning, an unexpected address-mapping change, or a session that contains an action the user denies while they were connected. Containment for a live case means dropping the session, moving the user off the shared network, and resetting the password that crossed in the clear.\n\nCredential replay is the trap. Replay reuses a captured login or token later, and the attacker does not have to remain between the two parties at the moment of reuse. Malicious code on the network is a program moving between hosts, not a person reading a live session. When someone sat in the middle and read or changed the traffic, the answer is the on-path attack.\n",
        'r0': "\nForged address mappings on a LAN, or a shared cafe segment, put the attacker on the forwarding path. Sam's bookmark used cleartext on TCP port 80, so the webmail password crossed the relay, and one outbound message was altered in that same session. Transport protection on port 443, with a certificate the client validates, stops that quiet read and edit. A browser rule that refuses the plaintext bookmark blocks the same mistake on the next visit. There is no second use of a stolen cookie hours later from another network. That later use would be credential replay. Detection artifacts are a changed address mapping, a certificate warning, or a user-denied action inside a live session. Containment drops the session and moves the user off the open network. Reset the password that was exposed on the relay.\n",
        'r1': '\nSomeone stands between two people who are talking. That person can read the messages as they pass. That person can also change a message and send it on. Both sides think they still talk to each other. An open shop network makes the trick easier. An unprotected login shows the password. A protected session stops the quiet reading. Accepting a warning opens the path again. The attacker has to stay in the middle. Using a saved pass later is a different trick. A program moving between computers is different too. Move the person off that shared network. Change any password that crossed in the open.\n',
        'r2': '\nA courier carries lab envelopes from the clinic to the lab across town. One afternoon a stranger steps onto the route, opens each envelope, reads the page, sometimes changes a line, and seals the envelope again. The clinic and the lab both believe the courier is the usual person. The stranger has to remain on the route to change the next envelope. A seal that only the lab can open stops the quiet reading. Showing up later with a photocopy of an old visitor band is a different stunt, because the route itself is empty by then. The stranger on the route is the attacker standing in the middle. The opened envelope is the traffic being read or changed in flight.\n',
        'r4': '\nAsk which network the user joined and whether the site was protected. Write down any certificate warning the user accepted. If the session is on your local network, capture the address-mapping table and export the log that shows the altered action. Do not keep the session open to watch. Move the user off the shared network, drop the session, and reset the exposed password from a trusted machine. Save the log before you reset anything. Note that the change happened during the live conversation, not hours later from another city.\n',
        'exam': '\nStanding between two parties and reading or changing the traffic is an on-path attack. The attacker has to be in the path while the change happens. Credential replay uses a captured token later and does not require staying in the middle. Malicious code on the network is a program copied between hosts, not a modified conversation. A certificate warning the user accepted is a clue, as is an unprotected login on an open network. Transport protection that the client actually checks is the defense. Accepting the warning defeats that defense.\n',
    },
    'Credential replay': {
        'body': "\nCredential replay reuses a captured login or token so the attacker can act without learning the password again. The capture can happen earlier, through malware, a lookalike site, or an unprotected session. The reuse is a separate step. At the moment of reuse, the attacker presents the already-accepted proof, and the server treats the proof as the original user.\n\nA session token is the clearest workplace case. After a real sign-in, the application sets a token that means this browser already authenticated. A copy of that token lets another computer inherit the session until the token expires or the server revokes the token. A captured admission ticket can be presented again in the same spirit, instead of a fresh password proof. Short lifetimes, one-time challenges, and protected transport so the token is not stolen on the wire are the defenses. A value the server will accept only once stops a simple repeat of an old authentication message. None of that requires the attacker to sit between the two parties during the reuse.\n\nUser Mara signs in to the clinic portal at 09:00 from her desk, and the application issues a session token with an eight-hour life. At 09:20 the same token is presented from a hosting address Mara has never used, and the portal shows a second session. The password log does not show a new successful password proof for that second session. The log shows the token. The ticket should record both source addresses, the token's issue time, and the fact that the second entry skipped the password prompt. That skip is the replay.\n\nRevoking the session on the server is containment for the stolen proof. Reset the password as well if the original capture may have included the password. Preserve the access log before anything is rotated. Then find the capture path: a malicious add-on, a lookalike page that took the token, or an old unprotected page. Mark the token so scripts in the browser cannot read it, send the token only on protected transport, shorten the lifetime, and issue a fresh session identifier at sign-in. Detection is two uses of one token from places that do not fit one person, especially when the second use performed no password check.\n\nAn on-path attacker reads or changes traffic while sitting between the parties. Replay can happen long after the capture, from a network the victim is not on. A program traveling between machines is malicious code, not a reused proof. Sending a payment request a second time so money moves twice is a repeat of a transaction, not a reuse of a credential. This topic is the captured login or session token used again.\n",
        'r0': "\nMara's portal issued a session token at 09:00 with an eight-hour lifetime. At 09:20 that same token arrived from a second address, and the authentication log showed no fresh password success for the new session. The reused proof was enough. Presenting a captured Kerberos ticket again, instead of a new initial exchange, follows the same pattern. A one-time nonce, or a server that rejects a repeated authenticator, stops a simple replay of an old message. Protected transport keeps the token off an open network. Flags that hide the token from scripts, plus a new session identifier at login, shrink the window. Revoking the session is containment. An on-path attacker would have been changing packets during Mara's original conversation. The second computer only had to present the captured token.\n",
        'r1': '\nA captured sign-in pass is used again later. The attacker skips learning the password a second time. The capture and the reuse can be far apart. The pass from a finished sign-in is a common steal. Another computer shows that pass and gets in. The password question is skipped on that second entry. Cancel that session on the server. Save the access record before you change secrets. Shorten how long a sign-in pass stays valid. Protect the pass so stealing it is harder. Standing in the middle of a live talk is different. A program moving between machines is different too.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic gives Mara a wristband after she proves who she is at the front desk. The band means the check already happened. Someone photographs the band and prints a copy. An hour later the copy is shown at a side door, and the door accepts the copy, even though the stranger never spoke the password. The stranger does not need to stand between Mara and the desk at that later moment. Canceling the band at the desk stops the original and the copy together. A person who opens envelopes on the delivery route is doing something else, because that person is live in the middle of a trip. The copied wristband is the stolen session token. Showing the copy at the side door is the replay.\n',
        'r4': "\nFrom the application log, write the token's issue time, the token's lifetime, and every source address that presented the token. Note whether the later session skipped the password check. Revoke the session on the server before you spend time on the client. Copy the log first. Do not type the user's password into the suspect machine. Hunt the capture path only after the token is dead. Record the token flags and the lifetime you observed. Do not shorten lifetimes in production until those values are written down.\n",
        'exam': '\nReusing a captured login, token, or ticket without a new password proof is credential replay. The second session can come from a network the user is not on. An on-path attack reads or changes traffic while the attacker remains between the parties. Malicious code on the network is a program in transit, not a reused proof. Sending a payment request twice so money moves twice is a repeat of a transaction, not this topic. Revoking the session is containment. Short lifetimes are a defense. A skipped password prompt on the second entry is the clue.\n',
    },
    'Malicious code on the network': {
        'body': '\nMalicious code on the network is a harmful program moving between hosts. The payload is in transit on a share, a download, or a sideways copy, not sitting only as an unread attachment in a mailbox. The thing to notice is the code itself traveling, ready to run on the next machine. A password crossing the wire is a different object, and a repeated session token is a different object.\n\nA worm is the clearest example, because the worm pushes or pulls a copy of itself across the segment. A person can also copy a tool from one workstation to another through an admin share. In the capture you see a file write, often over file sharing on TCP port 445, of an executable the destination did not have a minute earlier. The parent on the destination may be a service, a scheduled task, or a user who did not mean to launch anything new. The bytes are a program. Mail filtering never sees this path, which is why watching only the mailbox leaves a blind spot.\n\nWorkstation WS14 pulls a worm copy from WS08 over the department file share at 02:16. The share path is a temporary folder nobody uses for real work. The file is a short executable, and the hash matches a sample already quarantined on WS08 that morning. The connection is workstation to workstation, which the clinic baseline says should be rare. The network sensor flags the same hash inside the file transfer. The ticket should name both hosts, the share path, the hash, and the time. Nobody opened a mail message to make that copy move.\n\nIsolating both hosts, and blocking workstation-to-workstation file sharing while the hash is hunted, is containment. Preserving one copy of the file and the transfer log comes before deletion. Eradication means removing the program from every host that has the program and patching the hole that let the program run, after the evidence copy exists. An application allow list on the destination refuses the executable even if the file write succeeds. Segmentation removes the easy road between ordinary computers. Mail controls still matter for attachments, and mail controls do not see this east-west copy.\n\nAn on-path attacker reads or changes a live conversation. Credential replay reuses a captured login or token. Neither of those is an executable moving from WS08 to WS14. A virus that stays inside documents people open is a host-file problem, not this transfer. When the evidence is a program copied from one host to another across the network, this is the topic, even when the mailbox is empty.\n',
        'r0': '\nThe artifact is a file in motion. At 02:16 WS14 read an executable from a temporary share on WS08 over SMB on TCP 445. The SHA-256 matched a worm sample already quarantined on WS08. Workstation-to-workstation 445 is outside the clinic baseline, and the sensor hit on that hash during the transfer. No mail log contains the file. Preserve the binary and the transfer record, then isolate both hosts. That isolation is containment. Eradication removes every copy and patches the flaw that allowed execution, after the evidence copy exists. An allow list on WS14 would have refused the image even though the write succeeded. An on-path case would show observed or altered session bytes, not a new program hash. Credential replay would show a reused token, not an executable on a share.\n',
        'r1': '\nA bad program is moving between computers. The program is not only sitting in a mailbox. One machine pulls a copy from another machine. A shared folder is a common road for that copy. The bytes on the wire are a program. The program is ready to run on the next machine. Mail checks never see this road. Separate both computers from the network. Save the file and the transfer record. Stop ordinary computers from sharing files with each other. Allow only named programs to run. Reading a live conversation is a different problem. Reusing a stolen sign-in is a different problem. The traveling program is the fact to name.\n',
        'r2': '\nA clinic moves samples between rooms through pneumatic tubes. One morning a contaminated sample travels from one room to another through a tube, ready to be used as soon as the sample lands. The sample is not a letter waiting unopened in the mail room. The danger is the sample itself on the move. Staff close both rooms, keep one contaminated vial as evidence, and stop room-to-room tubes until they know how many vials match. A person who only reads an envelope on the sidewalk is not shipping a new sample. A copied wristband is not a sample at all. The traveling sample is the harmful program moving across the network. The tube between rooms is the share the copy used.\n',
        'r4': '\nIdentify both hosts, the time, the share path, and the file hash. Confirm the hash matches a sample already seen on the source host. Save the binary and the transfer log before you delete the file. Isolate both machines. Do not open the executable to see what the file does. Block workstation-to-workstation shares as temporary containment, and write down the rule you added. Search for the same hash elsewhere before you close the case. Leave mail logs in the ticket only to show the mail logs do not contain this file.\n',
        'exam': '\nA program copied between hosts, such as a worm pulled across a file share, is malicious code on the network. An empty mailbox can still leave this answer true. An on-path attack reads or changes a live conversation. Credential replay reuses a captured login or token. Those are not programs in motion. A virus that stays inside documents people open is not this transfer. Isolating the two hosts is containment. Deleting every copy and patching, after the sample is saved, is eradication. An allow list is a control, not the name of the transfer.\n',
    },
    'Injection attacks': {
        'body': "An injection attack places instructions in a field that was supposed to hold ordinary data, and a program treats part of that field as a command. The application glues the visitor's text into a database query, so the text changes the query instead of remaining a name or a number inside it. Command injection aims the same mistake at a shell. LDAP injection aims it at a directory search.\n\nA safe search is a fixed sentence plus one bound value. An unsafe search is a single string the engine parses from scratch. A quote can close the name early. A condition that is always true can make the filter match every row. A comment marker can hide the rest of the original statement. When the engine accepts a second statement, the extra text can try to change rows. Blind cases print none of the stolen rows. The caller changes one condition at a time and learns from a delay or from a yes-or-no shift in the page.\n\nPriya, a billing clerk, types a patient name into the clinic search page served on port 443. The application pastes that box into a WHERE clause and sends the string to SQL Server on port 1433. A failed probe shows up in the database error log as an unclosed quotation mark followed by the fragment the caller submitted. A probe that works stays quiet in the web log: an ordinary POST to the search path with a long parameter. The query log is the witness. It holds a SELECT against the account table whose condition was forced true, and the rows include accounts Priya's role cannot open on the screen. One search returned the whole table, and that fact belongs in the ticket.\n\nClose the hole where the query is built. Parameterized queries, also called prepared statements, keep the statement shape fixed and send the typed value beside it, so a quote stays a character in a name. Give the site a database login that can read the patient table and cannot read the credential store. Show visitors a generic failure, and keep the engine text in the server log. A filter in front may block one famous pattern and still miss a second encoding, so the filter is not the repair. Preserve the query log and the request before anyone rewrites the page. Once that evidence is saved, replacing the glued statement with a bound parameter is the patch that removes this defect.\n\nStudents see a strange field and reach for the wrong name. An application buffer overflow spills past a reserved block of memory and may crash the process, without rewriting what a query means. A quote that changes which rows come back is not a spill, and a long comment that only crashes a viewer is not a rewritten query. The separator is the interpreter. Cross-site scripting runs a script in a browser, and that script does not change a database query. Injection is the case where data becomes orders because the program parsed the field as syntax.\n",
        'r0': 'SQL injection changes a database query by concatenation. A clinic search on port 443 pastes the name box into a WHERE clause and sends it to SQL Server on port 1433. A value such as a quote followed by OR 1=1 can force the condition true and return the account table. A comment marker can cut off the rest of the original statement. Blind variants leak through timing or a yes-or-no change instead of showing rows. Command injection and LDAP injection aim the same mistake at a shell or a directory. Prepared statements keep the statement shape fixed and bind the value, so the quote stays data. The database login should read only the tables that page needs. Raw engine errors stay in the server log. Preserve the query text before you replace the glued statement with a bound parameter.\n',
        'r1': 'Someone hides orders inside a normal data field. A program then follows those orders. A database search can change because of this. A quote may return rows that should stay hidden. A shell command can be smuggled the same way. Keep the question fixed and the typed value apart. Then a quote stays part of the name. Show visitors a plain error, not engine text. Give the site a small database login. A script that runs in a browser is another fault. A second copy of a real payment is another fault. A crash from a huge field is another fault. Write down the field and the query shape.\n',
        'r2': "A clinic kitchen trusts every line on an order slip, and the cook performs the whole slip. A nurse writes a soup name in the dish blank. Later the blank says the soup name and, under it, an order to throw out today's soup and bring the staff meals instead. The cook does not treat the second line as a note in the margin. Nothing on the paper marks which ink is a dish and which ink is a command, so the kitchen carries both out. The meal that leaves the window is the one the extra line demanded. An injection attack is that slip in a program. A field meant as a name is parsed as a command, and the program carries the command out.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the query log and the web request side by side. Write down the parameter, the statement text, and any rows a normal search could not return. Note the time, the source address, and whether a quote or a comment marker changed the result. Save that evidence before a developer rewrites the page. Do not drop the database login to a full administrator just to test the form. Do not tell the clerk to stop searching until you know the statement is still glued together.\n',
        'exam': "A script that runs in the victim's browser is the trap answer for a changed database query. The separator is which program obeyed the field. Extra bytes that crash a viewer are a memory spill, not a rewritten query. A second copy of a valid payment is a replay of a real action, not new syntax. A filter that blocks one famous pattern is not the repair if the code still pastes input into the statement. Bound parameters keep the quote inside the value.\n",
    },
    'Application buffer overflows': {
        'body': "An application buffer overflow is a spill inside one program. The program sets aside a fixed block of memory, then a field or a file that the program parses writes past the end of that block. Bytes land on whatever sat next to the block: another variable, a saved return address, or heap bookkeeping. Here the spill is reached through that program's own field or file, rather than through a separate service you do not ship.\n\nThe copy is the mechanism. The parser trusts a length stored in the file, or it copies until it sees a marker, and the destination is smaller than the source. Picture a 512-byte stack buffer and a comment segment of about 8000 bytes. Everything past byte 512 overwrites the neighboring stack. A stack canary placed between the buffer and the return address will not match at function exit, and the process aborts if the check is on. With no canary, the overwritten return address can send execution to bytes the attacker chose. A heap spill instead corrupts allocator data and may surface later as a strange write. The crash is a clue that the length went too far, not a finished explanation.\n\nSam on the service desk takes a ticket from Lena in radiology. Her viewer, ImageDesk, closes the moment she opens report-q3.jpg from the modality share. The application log on the workstation records ImageDesk.exe faulting inside jpegparse.dll. A lab copy of the same file shows a comment segment near 8000 bytes, while that parser reserves 512 bytes for the comment. A rebuilt file with a 100-byte comment opens. In the crash dump the stack cookie does not match the canary the module expected. The ticket should say the long comment is being checked as more than a bad picture, because length is what decides whether the viewer lives.\n\nDefense starts in the copy and continues in the platform. Reject a comment longer than the buffer before any byte is written, and use a bounds-checked copy in the patched parser. Ship the viewer with stack canaries, address space layout randomization, and a non-executable stack so a smash is harder to turn into running code. Those controls lower the odds of code execution and still leave the unsafe copy in place. Keep the crafted file and the dump before anyone deletes them. If you suspect the smash already ran code, isolating Lena's workstation is containment. Installing the patched viewer after that sample is preserved removes the unsafe copy from the program.\n\nThe neighboring fault is injection, and the separator is what the extra input does. Injection changes the meaning of a command an interpreter runs, such as a database query. A spill overwrites memory because a copy ignored the size of the block. A query that returns every row can be short, and a comment that only crashes a viewer never has to contain a quote. Students see a long field and name it injection, or they see a crash and stop. Measure the length that crashes and a shorter length that does not.\n",
        'r0': 'A viewer reserves a 512-byte buffer for a JPEG comment and copies what the file claims. A crafted comment of about 8000 bytes writes past that block and can overwrite a saved return address. A stack canary that no longer matches will abort the process on the way out of the function. Address space layout randomization and a non-executable stack make a smash harder to turn into code, and they leave the unsafe copy in place. The application log shows ImageDesk.exe faulting in jpegparse.dll. A 100-byte comment opens, and the long comment does not. Heap spills corrupt allocator data rather than a return address. Keep the file and the dump. Isolation of the workstation is containment if code may already have run. The patched parser must reject the overlong comment before the copy.\n',
        'r1': 'One program sets aside a small block of memory. A field or a file writes past the end. Nearby memory is overwritten by the extra bytes. The program may crash at that moment. A crafted picture can do this to a viewer. A crash by itself does not prove the cause. Check whether length is what makes it crash. Changing a database search is a different kind of mistake. Sending a real payment twice is different too. Stop the write at the end of the block. A guard value can show the block was smashed. Do not label every crash as this kind of bug.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic storeroom has a shelf marked for one box of films. Someone slides in a crate much longer than the shelf, and the crate shoves the next bin onto the floor, including the card that says which room to visit next. Staff who hear the noise only see a crash of boxes. The length of the crate is what moved the card. A shorter box would have sat on the shelf and left the card alone. Checking the card later tells you it was knocked out of place. Buying a taller room does not shrink the crate. An application buffer overflow is that shove inside one program. Extra bytes from a field or a file land past the block the program reserved.\n',
        'r4': 'Save the file that crashed the viewer and the fault line from the application log. Write down the module name, the buffer size if you can learn it, and the input size that crashes. Also write down a shorter size that opens cleanly. Do not delete the sample from the share until a copy is in the ticket. Do not call the incident a query rewrite before you see memory being overwritten. Leave the workstation in place if you still need the dump.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call a long field a database injection when the evidence is a crash in one viewer. Injection changes what a command means. This spill writes past a reserved block, and a short input that opens helps prove the length. A stack canary, randomized layout, and a non-executable stack hinder code execution and do not replace a bounds check. A repeated valid payment is not a smashed buffer. A crash with no length difference is not yet this finding.\n',
    },
    'Application replay': {
        'body': "Application replay sends a valid request a second time so the action happens again. The body is not a hidden command and it is not a broken field. It is a transfer, a refund, or an approval that was genuine the first time. A server that treats every well-formed copy as a new order will perform the work twice. The attacker does not need to learn the password if the captured request still satisfies the checks the server actually makes.\n\nThe attacker saves the request when the user makes it: a hostile proxy on a network the user trusted, a browser willing to resubmit, or a log that stored the body. The attacker then posts that same body again. If the server only checks that the session looks signed in and the amount looks reasonable, the second post succeeds. An idempotency key stored on the server, rejected when it appears again, stops the duplicate before money moves. A short expiry and a one-time nonce issued for that form do the same job. Encryption on the wire stops a stranger from reading the body in transit. It does not save you when the attacker already holds the body and the service will honor it twice.\n\nChris in payroll approves a vendor transfer of 2500 dollars at 14:06. The payment service writes one ledger row and returns success. At 14:07 the same body arrives from a second address, still carrying Chris's session token and the same payment identifier, and another 2500 dollars leaves. The application log shows two POSTs to the pay path, identical bodies, two source addresses, one inside the clinic and one outside. The ledger holds two debit rows and no reversal. Chris did not click submit twice. The ticket fact is the second debit and the repeated identifier, not a bad password.\n\nRefuse work you have already performed. Remember each idempotency key and treat a repeat as a duplicate, not as a new transfer. Tie the request to a nonce that expires quickly and was minted for that screen. Keep full payment bodies out of logs that a wide group can read, because a logged body is a ready-made replay. For the live incident, preserve both requests and both ledger rows before anyone deletes the duplicate to tidy the books. Disabling Chris's account is containment only when the session was stolen. That disable does not fix a service that will pay twice for one key. The code change that rejects the repeated key, after the evidence is saved, is the patch for the hole.\n\nStudents hear the word replay and reach for credential replay. Credential replay reuses a captured login or a session so the attacker can act as the user without knowing the password again. The harm here is the business action occurring twice, and the first request was a real payment Chris meant to send. A stolen cookie that only opens the payroll site, with no second debit, is the other fault. The separator is a repeated valid action, proved by the same payment identifier leaving money twice.\n",
        'r0': 'A captured payment can be posted again if the server has no memory of the first one. Chris approved 2500 dollars at 14:06. At 14:07 the same body, the same session token, and the same payment identifier arrived from a second address, and a second 2500 dollars left. The log shows two POSTs and two ledger debits. An idempotency key stored before money moves lets the service reject the repeat. A nonce that expires in a short window, issued for that form, blocks a stale copy. Wire encryption does not replace that check once the attacker holds the body. Disabling the account contains a stolen session and leaves the duplicate-acceptance hole in place. Preserve both requests, then patch the service to refuse a key it has already accepted.\n',
        'r1': 'A real request is captured and sent again. The second copy still looks valid. A payment can leave the account twice. The first request was a genuine one. The attacker may not need a new password. Nothing in the copy has to be junk. Store a unique receipt number on the server. Reject a receipt number you already took. Let that receipt expire quickly. Changing what a query means is different. Smashing a memory block is different too. Reusing a stolen login is a different harm. Write down both times and the amount. Do not refund anyone until the duplicate is clear.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic front desk takes a paper order for a lab test and files a carbon copy. Later a stranger slides that carbon copy back across the desk, and the clerk, who files every complete form, schedules the test again. The form is signed, the patient name is right, and the test code is one the clinic offers. Nothing on the second slip looks forged or smudged. The harm is a second appointment and a second bill for one visit. A stamp that is crossed off in a book after the first filing would have stopped the clerk. Shredding the carbon after filing would have helped too. Application replay is that second filing. A valid request is accepted again, so the real action happens twice.\n',
        'r4': 'Pull both requests and both ledger rows before anyone reverses the extra debit. Write down the payment identifier, the amount, the two timestamps, and the two source addresses. Note whether the session token matches the first approval. Do not delete the duplicate row to make the ledger look tidy. Do not reset the payroll password as a substitute for a missing duplicate check. Leave the service accepting traffic only if a temporary block on that identifier is already in place.\n',
        'exam': "Credential replay is the trap. That neighbor reuses a login or a token so someone can sign in without the password. Application replay's evidence is a valid action happening twice, such as two debits for one payment identifier. Hostile syntax in a field would be injection. A crash from an overlong file would be a memory spill. Wire encryption does not stop a replay of a body the attacker already has. Disabling the user contains a stolen session and is not the code fix.\n",
    },
    'Privilege escalation': {
        'body': "Privilege escalation is the climb from a foothold the attacker already has to a right above that foothold. The foothold may be an ordinary user, a help-desk login, or a service account. The bigger right is often administrator on one host or domain admin across the clinic. The attacker spends a right the organization already handed out and points it upward.\n\nTwo directions show up on exams, and only one of them matches a jump in power. Vertical escalation climbs, for example from a user to an administrator. Horizontal movement reaches another person at the same rank, such as one nurse opening another nurse's mailbox, without becoming admin. The help-desk path is vertical when the goal is a domain admin. The help-desk role can reset passwords for staff. If that role can also reset a domain admin, the attacker resets the admin password, signs in, and holds the admin right. A user who can edit membership can add their own account to Domain Admins, which is the same climb by a different lever.\n\nJordan works the help desk and is allowed to reset passwords for clinic staff. On Tuesday at 16:40 the directory records event 4724, an attempt to reset the password on the account alex.admin. Jordan's account is the caller. Two minutes later event 4672 shows special privileges assigned to a new logon of alex.admin from Jordan's workstation. There is no change ticket for an admin password reset, and Alex is on leave. The ticket fact is that reset, followed by a privileged logon from the help-desk PC.\n\nContain the live foothold and then remove the path. If Jordan's account is in an attacker's hands, disabling that account is containment, and isolating the help-desk workstation is containment too. Neither step is eradication. The reset right is still on the role, so the next help-desk login can try the same climb. Eradication, after the 4724 and 4672 records are preserved, means taking password-reset rights off privileged accounts and keeping daily logins out of admin groups. Alert on 4724 against admin accounts and on 4728 when a member joins a privileged global group.\n\nStudents call this request forgery because Alex did not sit down to approve the reset. Forgery causes a browser or a server to send a request selected by the attacker. Jordan signed in as Jordan and spent a right the directory already listed. No outsider page submitted that reset inside Alex's browser. The separator is the right you finish holding. A climb from user to administrator is escalation.\n",
        'r0': "Vertical escalation climbs from a smaller right to a larger one, such as help desk to domain admin. Jordan's reset of alex.admin is Windows event 4724 at 16:40, and the privileged logon is event 4672 two minutes later from the help-desk PC. Event 4728 is the signal when someone adds a member to a privileged global group. Horizontal access reaches another user at the same rank and does not by itself create an administrator. Disabling Jordan's account and isolating that PC are containment. They are not eradication, because the reset right remains. After the events are preserved, remove password reset from privileged targets and keep daily logins out of admin groups. Alert on 4724 aimed at admin accounts.\n",
        'r1': "A small right is traded up for a bigger one. A normal user becomes an administrator. Help desk staff can reset passwords. That reset can be aimed at an admin account. The attacker then signs in as that admin. Reaching a peer's mailbox is a sideways move. Reading a file outside a folder is different. Tricking a browser into sending a form is different. Watch for admin passwords being reset. Watch for new members in admin groups. Separate daily accounts from admin accounts. Disabling a stolen account contains the damage. That disable does not remove the extra right. Write down who reset the password and when.\n",
        'r2': "A clinic building issues a closet key to the evening clerk so supply boxes can be restocked. The closet key also opens the cabinet where the master rings hang, because nobody split those locks. The clerk, or anyone who took the clerk's key, lifts a master ring and can open every wing. The clerk never kicked a door and never pretended to be the facilities chief at the front desk. The authority was sitting on the ring the job already had. Changing the closet lock after the master ring is copied still matters, and pulling the stolen master ring matters first so the tour stops. Privilege escalation is that trade. A small right the job already held is used to take a bigger one, up to an administrator.\n",
        'r4': 'Pull the password-reset event and the following privileged logon before you disable anything you might need to trace. Write down the caller, the target account, the workstation, and the timestamps. Check whether a ticket authorized an admin reset. Disable the stolen foothold account and isolate its PC if the session is still live, and label those moves as containment. Do not remove the help-desk role or rebuild the directory until the events are saved. Do not reset every admin password in a panic before you know which account was climbed.\n',
        'exam': 'Request forgery is the trap, because the admin did not mean for the reset to happen. Forgery makes a browser or a server send a request the attacker chose. Escalation spends a right the account already had and ends with a bigger right, such as domain admin. Directory traversal reads outside a folder and does not by itself grant that right. Disabling the stolen help-desk account is containment, not eradication. The reset right is still there until it is removed from privileged targets. A peer mailbox without a new admin right is horizontal access.\n',
    },
    'Request forgery': {
        'body': 'Request forgery causes a browser or a server to send a request the attacker selected. The person at the keyboard did not mean to send it, or the server did not mean to make that outbound call. One form targets the browser. The victim is already signed in, and the attacker\'s page submits a state-changing request with cookies the site already trusts. The other form targets the server. The application fetches a URL the attacker supplied, so the server contacts an address the attacker could not open directly.\n\nAn admin signed in to the clinic portal opens a site the attacker controls. That site posts to the portal\'s password-reset action. The browser attaches the portal cookie because the destination is the portal, not because the admin approved the reset. If the cookie alone is enough, the reset runs. A secret the attacker\'s page cannot read, checked on every state-changing post, breaks the trick. A cookie the browser withholds on cross-site posts helps, and a fresh password prompt helps on a reset. The server form is a different sender with the same idea. An "import this invoice URL" feature connects wherever the string points. An attacker aims it at an internal admin page or at the cloud metadata address 169.254.169.254, and the server\'s place on the network does the rest. Allow-lists, refusals of private and link-local ranges, and a metadata service that demands its own token are the matching controls.\n\nRiley, a clinic administrator, is signed in to the staff portal and then opens a page from a vendor-looking mail. The hostile page auto-submits a password reset for the nurse account maria, and the portal accepts it because the request carried Riley\'s cookie and no second secret. The portal audit line shows Riley\'s session posting to the reset action at 11:18, with a referrer that is not the portal. Maria\'s password-change time matches that minute, and Maria did not call the desk. In a second incident the invoice importer on the same portal logged an outbound fetch of http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/ from the application server, an address no clerk would type. The ticket has two forgery shapes: a browser used as the sender, and a server used as the sender.\n\nPreserve the audit line, the referrer, and Maria\'s account state before you roll her password. Revoking Riley\'s live session is containment. Revocation does not add the missing token, so it is not eradication of the defect. After the evidence is saved, require a form secret on the reset and block the importer from private and link-local addresses. The token and the block list are defenses. The attack is the unwanted request.\n\nStudents mix this with script in the browser because both can start on a hostile page. Script running under the portal\'s origin is a different fault, and this one does not need any script to run inside the portal. The browser or the server simply emits the request the attacker selected. A database query rewritten by a quote is injection, not this send. The separator is who transmitted the request, and that the attacker picked it.\n',
        'r0': "Cross-site request forgery uses a browser that is already signed in. Riley's session posted a password reset for maria at 11:18 because a hostile page submitted it and the portal trusted the cookie alone. The audit referrer was not the portal. A per-form anti-forgery token, a SameSite cookie that is not sent on cross-site posts, and a fresh password prompt on the reset stop that path. Server-side request forgery makes the application fetch an attacker-chosen URL. The invoice importer requested http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/, the cloud metadata address. Block link-local and private ranges, and prefer a metadata service that requires a session token. Revoking Riley's session is containment. Adding the missing checks, after the logs are saved, patches the hole. A changed database query is a different fault, and a dot-dot path is a different fault.\n",
        'r1': 'A browser or a server sends a request. The attacker picked the request that gets sent. The signed-in person did not mean to send it. An admin opens a hostile page. That page submits a password reset. The browser includes the current login cookie. A server can be pushed to call inward. The attacker chose the address it calls. A script running inside your site is different. Climbing out of a shared folder is different. Gaining a brand-new admin right is different. Put a fresh secret on sensitive forms. Stop the server from calling private addresses. Write down who appeared to send the request.\n',
        'r2': "A clinic clerk is signed in at the records window, and the window trusts the clerk's badge for the rest of the shift. A stranger hands the clerk a sealed transfer slip and asks that it be dropped in the internal tube. The clerk sends it, badge and all, to the password desk. The desk sees a valid badge and changes a nurse's password. The clerk never chose the nurse and never read the slip. In another wing, the mail robot is told to fetch whatever address is written on a card, and the card names a locked office the stranger cannot enter. The robot can enter, so the stranger gets the errand done. Request forgery is that errand. A browser or a server sends the request the attacker chose.\n",
        'r4': "Save the audit line, the referrer, and the timestamp before you reset the affected password. Write down which session sent the browser request, and which URL the server fetched if the call was outbound. Note whether a form secret was required. Revoke the live session if the user does not recognize the action, and call that containment. Do not add the attacker's address to an allow list to reproduce the fetch on production. Do not turn off cookie protections while you are still collecting the log.\n",
        'exam': 'Cross-site scripting is the trap, because a hostile page is involved in both stories. Script that runs in the browser under your site is not required here. Forgery makes the browser or the server send a request the attacker chose. Directory traversal escapes a folder with parent-path marks and does not need that outbound or cross-site send. A changed database query is injection. A bigger right is escalation. Revoking the session contains the live login and leaves the missing form secret unfixed.\n',
    },
    'Directory traversal': {
        'body': "Directory traversal uses parent-path marks such as ../ to escape the folder an application is allowed to serve. The attacker edits a file name or a path parameter until the server steps out of the download directory, the image directory, or the template directory. The file that comes back was never on the menu: a configuration file, a password store, or another application's data. The request stays a read of a path.\n\nThe code takes a base directory and appends the user-supplied name. If the name contains ../ segments, the joined path climbs. Four climbs from a deep download folder can reach a system file. Encoding hides the same climb from a naive filter: %2e%2e%2f is ../ written in hexadecimal, and mixed forms such as ..%2f or a doubled encoding get through filters that only look for the raw characters. After the join, a safe server canonicalizes the path and checks that the result still sits under the base directory. A weak server opens whatever path the join produced. The check still fails open if the site account can read the whole disk.\n\nNoah in billing clicks a download link for a monthly statement. The link is a path parameter, and a tampered request in the web log reads GET /download?file=..%2f..%2fweb.config with a result of 200. The bytes returned match the site's web.config, including a database connection string, and they do not match any statement PDF. The download folder on disk does not contain web.config. The application log shows the worker opening a path above the statement directory. The ticket fact is the 200 response and the climbed path, not a failed login.\n\nReject any supplied path that contains parent segments, then resolve the path and confirm the result stays inside the statement directory. Better, accept only a statement identifier and map it server-side, so the disk path never comes from the query string. Run the site as an account that cannot read configuration secrets. A filter that blocks only the raw characters ../ misses the encoded form. Preserve the access-log line and a copy of the response before anyone rotates the leaked connection string. Rotating that secret and fixing the path check, after the evidence is saved, removes the exposure. Brief downtime can contain a download that is still running. The attack is the climb.\n\nStudents call this request forgery because the server touched a file the user should not have had. Forgery sends a browser or a server toward a destination the attacker picked, such as a cross-site reset or an internal URL. Traversal needs no second destination. The parameter is a path, and ../ walks out of a folder. Becoming an administrator would be a bigger right, and a leaked connection string does not by itself grant that right. The separator is the path: parent segments left the directory the application was supposed to serve.\n",
        'r0': 'Parent segments such as ../ climb out of a served folder. The billing download log shows GET /download?file=..%2f..%2fweb.config returning 200, and the body is web.config with a database connection string, not a statement PDF. %2e%2e%2f is the same climb in hexadecimal, and a filter that only deletes literal ../ misses it. Four climbs can be enough to leave a deep download directory. Canonicalize the joined path and require it to remain under the base directory. Prefer a statement id that you map on the server, so the user never supplies a path. The site account should be unable to read secrets outside that directory. Preserve the log line before you rotate the leaked string. A fetch of an attacker-chosen URL would be a different fault.\n',
        'r1': 'A file name tries to climb out of its folder. Parent marks in the name do the climbing. The server may return a file you never offered. A download link can carry those marks. Encoded marks can hide the same climb. A configuration file can come back by mistake. Reject names that try to climb. Keep every download inside one known folder. Hand out an identifier instead of a raw path. A request sent to some other address is different. Becoming an administrator is different. A changed database search is different. Write down the path and the file that came back.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic file room lets visitors request charts by a slip that is supposed to name a chart inside one cabinet. Someone writes a slip that says to step out of the cabinet, cross the hall, and pull a folder from the locked office. The clerk who treats the slip as a location, and who holds a keyring for the whole floor, fetches the office folder and hands it over. The visitor never walked the hall. The climb was written on the slip as a step upward and out. A clerk who may open only that one cabinet, and who refuses any slip mentioning another room, would have stopped. Directory traversal is that slip. Parent marks in a path leave the folder the application was meant to serve.\n',
        'r4': 'Copy the access-log line and the returned file into the ticket before you rotate secrets. Write down the parameter, the decoded path, the status code, and whether the file sits outside the download folder. Note any encoded parent marks so a later reader sees the climb. Do not delete the log because the path looks ugly. Do not broaden the site account so you can reproduce the read more easily. Leave the mapping from identifiers to real files unchanged until the climbed request is saved.\n',
        'exam': "Request forgery is the trap. Forgery sends the browser or the server toward a destination the attacker picked. Traversal stays on a path and uses ../ or an encoding of those parent marks to leave a folder. Becoming an administrator is escalation, even if a leaked configuration file might help later. A database query that changes because a quote was glued in is injection. A filter that strips only the raw parent marks misses the encoded form. The site account's ability to read the whole disk keeps the climb valuable.\n",
    },
    'Downgrade attacks': {
        'body': "A downgrade attack pushes a connection onto an older, weaker method the attacker can break, even though a stronger method was available. The attacker interferes with negotiation and strips the modern choices. What completes is an old protocol or a weak cipher the server should have refused. A client that can offer only the old method is a compatibility problem. A downgrade is the push: a strong method was offered, and the connection did not get to use it.\n\nThe handshake is a menu. The client lists versions and cipher suites. The server picks one it also accepts. A device on the path can rewrite that list until only TLS 1.0 or SSL 3.0 remains, or until only an RC4 suite remains, and a careless server agrees. SSL 3.0 is the ground the POODLE attack needed. Some old clients still show a padlock after a weak handshake, so the log matters more than the icon. Refusing every version below TLS 1.2, and refusing the weak suites, leaves the attacker nothing to force. HSTS tells a browser not to follow a strip from encrypted browsing down to plain browsing, which removes protection entirely rather than weakening the handshake.\n\nMina runs the clinic mail gateway and pulls a handshake log from the submission service on port 443. A partner client offered TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.2. The completed line says TLS 1.0 with TLS_RSA_WITH_RC4_128_MD5, while the listener policy is TLS 1.2 or newer. The same client completed TLS 1.2 an hour earlier, before a new device appeared on the path. The ticket fact is that negotiated version, not a guess that the partner only owns old software.\n\nDefense is a refusal, plus a watch for the refusal failing. Disable SSL 3.0 and TLS 1.0 on the server, and do not keep an internet-facing exception for one vendor. Alert when a handshake completes below the policy floor. Preserve the handshake log before you touch the path device. Removing that device from the path is containment if it is rewriting hellos. Making the server unable to negotiate the old version, after the log is saved, removes the attacker's choice. The old protocol is the attacker's goal, not a defense.\n\nStudents call a merely old client a downgrade. The separator is whether a stronger method was offered and then suppressed. If the client hello never listed TLS 1.2, you have a legacy client to replace, and the server should still refuse the weak hello. A hash collision is a different neighbor: two inputs share one fingerprint, and that pair does not force a handshake downward. The downgrade finding is the completed weak negotiation that a stronger offer should have won.\n",
        'r0': 'A downgrade forces a handshake that could have been strong onto a weaker method. On port 443 a partner client offered TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.2, and the completed negotiation was TLS 1.0 with TLS_RSA_WITH_RC4_128_MD5. Policy for that listener is TLS 1.2 or newer. SSL 3.0, the version POODLE abused, belongs on the same refusal list as TLS 1.0. The server should have no weak suite left to accept. HSTS stops a browser from being walked from HTTPS down to plain HTTP, which removes encryption rather than merely weakening it. Alert when any completed version sits below the floor. Preserve the handshake log, then disable the old versions. A client hello that never offered TLS 1.2 is a legacy client, not this push.\n',
        'r1': 'A connection is pushed onto an older method. The older method is weaker and easier to break. The server should have refused that method. Someone on the path strips the strong choices. The two sides could have stayed strong. An honestly old client is a different problem. That client never offered a stronger choice. Refuse old versions on the server. Watch for a sudden return of weak handshakes. Two files with one fingerprint are not this. Counting pairs among fingerprints is not this. Write down the version the two sides used. Do not turn the old method back on for ease.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic door has a new lock and an old lock on the same frame, and staff are supposed to use the new one. A stranger on the stoop covers the new lock so the only keyhole left is the old one, which a copied key still opens. Visitors see a door that opened and assume the strong lock did the work. The cover is the whole trick. A door whose new lock was never installed is a different problem, because there was no strong choice to hide. Taking the old lock off the frame ends the game. A downgrade attack is that cover on a connection. A stronger method was available, and the connection was pushed onto the weaker one.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the handshake log and write down every version the client offered plus the version that completed. Note the suite that was chosen and the lowest version the policy allows. Record whether that same client used a strong version earlier the same day. Do not re-enable the old version so a partner can connect while you are still reading. Do not swap the path device until the completed negotiation is copied into the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is a client that never offered a strong method. That legacy system should be replaced, and the server should still refuse the weak hello. A downgrade suppresses a stronger offer and finishes on a weaker method the server should have rejected. Two inputs that share a fingerprint are a collision, not a handshake. Math about pairs and short fingerprints is a birthday attack, not a protocol push. Leaving the old version enabled is what gives the push somewhere to land.\n',
    },
    'Hash collisions': {
        'body': 'A hash collision means two different inputs produce the same hash value. The files or messages are not the same. The fingerprint is. A check that stores only that fingerprint cannot tell the inputs apart, so a swapped file can pass a review that never looks at the bytes. Hashing is not encryption. A collision unlocks nothing, because the fingerprint was a summary, not a locked box.\n\nAttackers can build two different files that share an MD5 digest or a SHA-1 digest. Attackers can build two different files that share one of those hashes. SHA-256 is the hash to teach for file identity, because a practical collision against it is not a known, usable attack. The workplace shape is a pair the attacker influenced, not a lucky match against a random file. The attacker builds a harmless installer and a malicious installer so they share an MD5. A reviewer hashes the harmless one, writes that MD5 on the change ticket, and approves the change. The attacker then swaps in the malicious installer. The ticket MD5 still matches. SHA-256 of the two files does not, so a ticket that stored SHA-256 would have rejected the swap.\n\nDevon in change management records the MD5 of clinic-setup.exe on ticket CHG-4418 before the Wednesday deploy. The file that lands on the pilot PCs that night carries the same MD5 and a different SHA-256 from the copy Devon hashed on the build share. Those PCs start calling an address the build notes do not list. The ticket fact is the matching MD5 beside the differing SHA-256, on the release path. Nobody edited the ticket text. The fingerprint the ticket trusted was too weak to notice the swap.\n\nStop trusting a broken hash as an identity. Record SHA-256 and compare it at deploy time with the approved build. Sign the build with a key held apart from the release share, so a swap fails the signature even if a short hash collides. When the digests disagree, preserve both files before anyone overwrites the folder. Isolating the pilot PCs is containment. Replacing the bad installer and dropping MD5 from the approval check, after both files are saved, closes the weak check. A matching MD5 is not proof the files are identical.\n\nStudents paste the birthday attack onto any match they see. The collision is the outcome, two inputs and one hash. The birthday attack is why such an outcome shows up much sooner than people expect: the search compares pairs among many candidates, rather than hunting one input that equals one fixed hash. Matching one chosen file to a hash you already published is the harder job, and a collision does not hand you that job for free. A downgrade is a different neighbor. It forces a weaker handshake and does not require two files to share a fingerprint.\n',
        'r0': "A hash collision is two different inputs with the same hash. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collisions, and SHA-256 is the hash to record when a file's identity matters. On ticket CHG-4418 the approved clinic-setup.exe and the copy that reached the pilot PCs share an MD5 and do not share a SHA-256. The attacker shaped both files so the harmless one would be hashed and approved, then swapped the other into the release path. A matching MD5 does not prove the bytes are the same. Compare SHA-256 at deploy, and sign the build with a key that does not live on that share. Isolating the pilot PCs contains the bad installer. The collision decrypts nothing, because hashing is not encryption.\n",
        'r1': 'Two different inputs can share one fingerprint. A check that trusts only that fingerprint can be fooled. A swapped file can keep the recorded fingerprint. The change ticket then looks clean. Weak methods make a crafted pair practical. A stronger fingerprint makes the swap obvious. This is not the same as hiding a message. Hiding a message uses a key. A shared fingerprint hides nothing. Pair hunting explains why matches come early. Forcing an old connection is a different topic. Record both inputs and the stronger fingerprint. Do not accept a file only because the short fingerprint matches.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic claims desk hands you a stub when you leave a coat. Two different coats can receive stubs that print the same short number when the stamp is worn out. The clerk later sees the number, not the coat, and hands over the wrong coat to the person holding that stub. The wool is not the same. The number is. A longer stub the worn stamp cannot copy would have exposed the swap at the window. Nobody opened a safe by duplicating the number. The number was only a label. A hash collision is that duplicated label. Two different inputs carry one fingerprint, so a check that trusts the fingerprint alone misses the swap.\n',
        'r4': 'Save both copies of the installer before anyone overwrites the release folder. Write down each fingerprint, the change ticket number, and the path of the file that actually deployed. Note which fingerprint matched the approval and which fingerprint did not. Do not close the change as successful because the short fingerprint agrees. Do not delete the pilot copy until both digests sit in the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Calling every match a birthday attack is the trap. A collision is two inputs with one hash, and that is the whole definition of the match. The birthday attack explains why such a match arrives sooner than a hunt for one input against one fixed hash, because pairs get compared. A downgrade weakens a handshake and needs no shared fingerprint. Hashing is not encryption, so the match reveals no secret key. Equal MD5 values are not proof the files match when SHA-256 differs.\n',
    },
    'Birthday attacks': {
        'body': 'A birthday attack explains why a hash collision becomes likely much sooner than a one-against-one guess suggests. People picture one input tested against one fixed hash and expect the work to be enormous. The attacker is not doing that job. The attacker generates many inputs and looks for any pair that shares a hash. Pair comparisons grow much faster than the list of inputs, so a match arrives early.\n\nShared birthdays are the picture that makes the count obvious. In a room of 23 people and 365 days, the chance that at least two share a birthday is about 50 percent. Matching one specific date, such as your own birthday, stays unlikely until the room is much larger. The early match happens because every person is compared with every other person, and 23 people produce 253 pairs. A hash of n bits has the same shape. Hunting any collision takes on the order of 2^(n/2) evaluations, not 2^n. A 64-bit fingerprint therefore starts to look reachable around 2^32 tries. SHA-256 still has a birthday bound near 2^128, which is not a practical search. A short fingerprint used as the only identity of a file should be rejected even before anyone publishes a sample collision.\n\nThe clinic imaging archive planned to identify studies with a 64-bit checksum printed on the jacket. Priya rejects that design on ticket ARCH-77 because a pair search at that length is too cheap, and two different studies could carry one checksum. She asks for SHA-256 and writes the reason as pair counting, not as a single forged study aimed at one existing checksum. No files have collided yet. The ticket fact is the bit length and the rejected control, recorded before a bad match can reach a patient record.\n\nUse a long hash for the question you are actually asking. SHA-256 is the right teaching choice when you need to know whether this file is the approved file. A 64-bit token must not be the only identity of a document an attacker can generate in bulk. A signature answers who produced the file. The hash answers whether the bytes stayed the same. If a short checksum is already in production, plan its replacement and sample whether any two stored objects already share it. The attack is the cheap search over pairs. A collision, if one appears, is the result of that search, not a second name for the search.\n\nStudents call every matching pair a birthday attack. Two inputs with the same hash are a collision, whether or not you care how the pair was found. This topic is the reason the match gets likely early: pairs are compared, not one chosen input against a fixed hash. A downgrade attack does not use the birthday count. It pushes a connection onto an older method. If the stem shows two files and one weak fingerprint, say collision. Say birthday attack when the point is how little work it takes to find any pair, especially with a short fingerprint.\n',
        'r0': 'With 23 people and 365 days, the chance that two share a birthday is about 50 percent, because that room contains 253 pairs. Matching one chosen date is a harder question. Collision search on an n-bit hash has the same shape: about 2^(n/2) work to find any pair, not 2^n work to hit one fixed hash. A 64-bit fingerprint starts to look practical near 2^32 evaluations. SHA-256 has a birthday bound near 2^128, which is not a realistic hunt. Ticket ARCH-77 rejects a 64-bit study identifier for that reason and asks for SHA-256 instead. The attack is the pair search. A collision is what you hold if a pair turns up.\n',
        'r1': 'People expect matches to be very rare. Shared birthdays show up much sooner than that. You compare every person with every other person. You are not matching one person to one chosen date. Pairs add up much faster than single checks. Short fingerprints have the same problem. Any two matching files are enough for the attacker. Hitting one fingerprint picked in advance is harder. A match you already hold is the result. The early likelihood comes from counting pairs. Reject short fingerprints for file identity. Forcing a weaker handshake is a different topic.\n',
        'r2': 'A waiting room holds twenty-three patients, and the wall calendar has a page for every day of the year. Visitors assume a shared birthday is rare, because matching one famous date would take a crowd. The clerk checks each patient against each other patient, and the pairs pile up. Two people often share a day even though nobody aimed at a date written on the board. The surprise is the counting method, not a broken calendar. Aiming at one date on the board would take far longer. A short label used to identify a file fails in that same way. Collisions show up early when pairs are compared, long before anyone hits one fingerprint chosen in advance.\n',
        'r4': 'Write down the fingerprint length and the question that fingerprint is supposed to answer. If the length is short, note why a search over pairs is cheap and which longer fingerprint should replace it. Do not wait for two stored files to collide before you reject the short value. Do not edit live identifiers until the ticket names the field in scope. Leave the current records untouched while you sample whether any two already match.\n',
        'exam': 'Treating the collision and the birthday attack as the same phrase is the trap. Two inputs with the same hash are a collision. The birthday attack is why that result becomes likely sooner than people expect, because the search compares pairs rather than one chosen input against a fixed hash. A short fingerprint makes the pair search realistic. A long modern fingerprint does not. Pushing a connection onto an older method is a downgrade, not this counting argument. Hitting one already published fingerprint is the harder job.\n',
    },
    'Password spraying': {
        'body': "Password spraying tries a small set of common passwords against many accounts and stays under the lockout on each one. The attacker is not hammering a single door. The attacker tries a password such as Welcome1 once, or a short seasonal list, across a long roster. Each account shows one failure, or two. No name reaches the number that locks it. The attack is built to avoid lockout. Thousands of guesses against one name are a different attack, and that pattern often does lock the name.\n\nThe clinic locks an account after 5 bad passwords. A spray of one password per account never reaches 5, so the lockout stays silent while the attack runs. The signal runs sideways: the same password, many accounts, a short window, often one source address. Each miss is Windows event 4625, commonly status 0xC000006A when the password is wrong and the account exists. A later success is a single 4624 sitting in a field of one-time failures that all carry that password. A second factor still matters after a correct guess, because the spray only needs one account where the common password is real.\n\nBetween 08:10 and 08:40 the domain controller records one 4625 on each of 150 staff accounts. The status is 0xC000006A, the password is Welcome1 on every line, and the source is one address on the VPN concentrator. No account reaches five failures, and no 4740 lockout is written. At 08:41 the account records.temp returns 4624 from that same address. The ticket fact is one password across 150 names. It is not a nurse who forgot a password at the desk, and the help desk has not taken 150 calls.\n\nRemove the password the spray is betting on, and alert on the pattern a per-account lock cannot see. Ban common passwords when they are set, including season-and-year forms. Require a second factor so Welcome1 alone cannot open a session. Alert when one password fails across many accounts inside an hour, even though each account failed only once. A control that watches the source address, not only the account, can slow the sender. Resetting all 150 passwords in the first minute hides the pattern and locks the morning clinic out of its own work. Preserve the 4625 set. Disable records.temp if that success was not the owner's, and call the disable containment. The common password and the missing second factor remain until those controls change. Per-account lockout is the wrong place to declare the attack finished, because spraying is designed to avoid it.\n\nBrute force is the trap. It throws many guesses at one account and often trips the lock on that account. Spraying is a few common passwords against many accounts, and the log shows one miss each rather than a pile on one name. Thousands of failures on a single account are not a spray. Many accounts with one identical failure, and nobody locked, are.\n",
        'r0': 'Password spraying puts a few common passwords against many accounts and is built to avoid lockout. The clinic locks an account after 5 failures. From 08:10 to 08:40, one event 4625 with status 0xC000006A hit each of 150 accounts using Welcome1 from a single VPN address. No name reached 5 failures, and event 4740 never fired. At 08:41 records.temp recorded a 4624 from that address. A brute-force hour would show thousands of 4625 events on one name and would often end at 4740. Ban common passwords, require a second factor, and alert when one password fails across many names. Disabling records.temp after a success the owner denies is containment. A per-account lock will not surface this pattern.\n',
        'r1': 'A few common passwords are tried on many accounts. Each account sees only one or two tries. No single account reaches the lock. The same password shows up across the failures. One account with thousands of tries is the other attack. That other attack often locks the account. This pattern is built to avoid the lock. Banning the most common passwords helps a lot. A second factor blocks a lucky correct guess. Alert when one password fails across many names. A lock on each account will not catch this. Write down the password and the list of names. Do not reset every account in the first minute.\n',
        'r2': 'A night cleaner tries one common key on every office door in a clinic hallway, giving each lock a single twist and moving on. No door gets enough twists to trip the bolt that slams shut after several failures. Most doors stay shut. One supply door opens, because that lock was set to the same common key. A guard who only watches for one door being rattled sees a quiet hall. A guard who notices the same key touch every lock inside half an hour sees the walk. Password spraying is that walk down the hall. A few common passwords meet many accounts, and each account stays under the lock.\n',
        'r4': 'Export the failure events for the hour before you unlock or reset any account. Write down the password value, the source address, the list of names, and any name that succeeded. Note that each account stayed under the lock threshold. Do not reset the whole roster as the first action. Do not disable the lockout policy in order to retry that password on a live account.\n',
        'exam': "Brute force is the trap. Brute force throws many guesses at one account and often causes lockout. Spraying uses a few common passwords against many accounts and avoids lockout by staying under each name's threshold. One failure on 150 accounts, same password, is this attack. Thousands of failures on a single account are not. A missing lock event does not clear the hour. A second factor still stops a guess that happened to be right. Disabling the account that succeeded contains that login and does not ban the common password.\n",
    },
    'Password brute force': {
        'body': "Password brute force tries many guesses against one account until a guess is right or the lockout trips. Online, those guesses are real sign-in attempts, and the account often locks. The clinic threshold of 5 bad passwords fires quickly when the attacker does not slow down. The picture to recognize is one name, a huge failure count, and often a single source address. That picture is the opposite of a spray, which spends a few passwords across many names and tries not to lock anyone.\n\nOnline guessing is visible because every try touches the directory. Event 4625 piles up on one account. A thousand failures in a minute from 198.51.100.40 is not a person at a keyboard. Event 4740 follows when the threshold breaks, and the status moves from a bad password toward a locked account. Offline guessing is the cousin that never trips that lock: the attacker already stole a file of password hashes and guesses on another machine. This topic's usual case is the online burst, where lockout often happens. Keep the offline case in mind so you do not promise that every brute force locks a live account. The lockout only sees attempts that reach the sign-in service.\n\nAt 09:02 the domain controller shows jsmith failing from 198.51.100.40. By 09:03 the 4625 count for that single account passes 4000, and 4740 records the lock. No other account fails from that address in the same minute. jsmith is at the front desk with a phone in hand and did not type thousands of passwords. The ticket fact is the speed on one name, the one source, and the lock. A pattern of one failure on each of a hundred names would belong to the other attack.\n\nSlow the guesses and shrink the prize. Lockout and rate limits cut online speed. A long password widens the space that must be searched. A second factor means a correct guess still cannot finish the sign-in. Alert on failures per account so a thousand attempts in a minute page someone before the next shift. Preserve the 4625 burst and the 4740 before you unlock jsmith. Unlocking so the nurse can work is reasonable once the source is known. Disabling the account is containment if you cannot reach jsmith or successes keep arriving. Neither step erases a stolen hash file on an attacker machine, and neither replaces the second factor. The lock itself is the control reacting. It is not the attack.\n\nSpraying is the trap. A few common passwords go to many accounts and stay under the lock, so 4740 never arrives. Brute force is many guesses against one account, and online it often causes lockout. One failure on each of 150 names is not this topic. Thousands of failures on jsmith are. Offline guessing against a stolen hash is still many tries at one secret, and it produces no lockout event at all.\n",
        'r0': 'Password brute force spends many guesses on one account. Online, a clinic policy that locks after 5 bad passwords often trips. Between 09:02 and 09:03, jsmith generated more than 4000 event 4625 failures from 198.51.100.40, and event 4740 then locked the account. No other name failed from that address during the minute. A spray would place about one 4625 on each of many names and would try to avoid 4740. Offline guessing on a stolen hash file never reaches the sign-in service, so it writes no 4740. Rate limits, a long password, and a second factor reduce the online prize. Preserve the burst before you unlock the nurse.\n',
        'r1': 'Many guesses are thrown at one account. Guessing continues until a hit or a lock. Thousands of failures can land in one minute. Those failures often come from one address. The account often locks before any hit. Trying a few passwords on many names is different. That wide pattern is built to stay under the lock. Lockout slows guessing on a live sign-in page. A second factor still blocks a lucky correct guess. Alert when one name fails many times fast. Guessing a stolen password file never sees the lock. Write down the account, the source, and the count.\n',
        'r2': 'Someone stands at a single clinic door with a huge ring of keys and tries them as fast as the lock allows. The bolt is set to freeze after a handful of wrong keys, and it often freezes. Every other door in the hall stays untouched. Trying one key on every door is a different walk and may never freeze a bolt. Freezing this door slows the ring in the hall. It does nothing if the attacker already copied the lock and is working through keys in a private workshop. Password brute force is that ring aimed at one account. Many guesses hit a single name, and the live sign-in often locks.\n',
        'r4': 'Save the failure burst and the lock event before you unlock the account. Write down the account name, the source address, the failure count, and the clock times of the first and last try. Check whether a success landed before the lock. Do not raise the lockout threshold just to quiet the noise. Do not aim your own long guess list at the live sign-in page.\n',
        'exam': 'Spraying is the trap. A spray puts a few common passwords on many accounts and avoids lockout. Brute force puts many guesses on one account, and online guessing often causes the lock. Thousands of failures and then a lock on one name fit this topic. A single miss on each of many names does not. Offline guesses at a stolen hash still aim at one secret and create no lock event. The lock is a control reacting to the guesses, not the attack itself.\n',
    },
    'Account lockout indicators': {
        'body': 'An account lockout indicator is a pattern in locks, not a verdict that malicious software arrived. The directory locked accounts because bad passwords crossed a threshold. The cause might be guessing, a broken application, or a person who forgot a password. The pattern tells you which. Twenty accounts locking inside five minutes is not one forgetful user. One nurse who locks once and then calls the desk usually is.\n\nRead the shape before you name a cause. One account, one lock, the person on the phone: a forgotten password. One account, a huge burst of failures, then event 4740: online brute force, which often causes a lock. Many accounts, one or two failures each, and no 4740 at all: spraying, which avoids lockout, so this indicator stays quiet on purpose. Many accounts actually locking within a few minutes can be a spray that tried too often, or a script with a stale secret hitting a set of service accounts. A service account that locks every ten minutes from the same server is often a saved password that no longer matches. The caller computer and the interval separate that case from a human campaign.\n\nAt 07:55 the clinic domain writes 20 event 4740 lockouts in five minutes, across front desk, imaging, and billing, from one address outside the building. The threshold is 5 failures. Each of those accounts shows a short run of 4625 events just before the lock, not a single lonely miss. The users are not all calling the desk, and no change window has started. The ticket fact is twenty locks, five minutes, one source. A different Tuesday habit, the scanner account locking at ten past every hour from the radiology PC, is the stale-password shape and should stay on its own ticket.\n\nInvestigate, then contain only what the pattern supports. Write down the account, the time, the caller computer, and the failure count before you unlock anyone. Phone two of the people. If they are at their desks, the outside address is not their typing. Blocking that source, and disabling any account that actually signed in from it, is containment. Unlocking the others so the clinic can work gives access back. It does not prove the event was harmless. Do not reset every password in the building as the first move, and do not call the locks malware. A lock is a control. The guessing that tripped it, if guessing is what you find, is the attack. If you alert only on 4740, you will miss a spray that never locks.\n\nThe trap is to treat every lock as brute force, or to insist that spraying must have produced locks. Brute force often locks one account after many guesses. Spraying sends a few common passwords to many accounts and is built to avoid the lock, so a field of single failures with no 4740 can still be the attack. Twenty locks in five minutes are not one forgotten password. One lock and a phone call often is. The indicator is the pattern of locks, not a program sitting on a PC.\n',
        'r0': 'Lockout indicators are patterns, not proof of a program on a workstation. Event 4740 is the Windows lock record. At 07:55 the clinic logged 20 of those events in five minutes from one outside address, each after a short run of 4625 failures, under a threshold of 5. That shape is not one forgotten password. One account with thousands of 4625 events and a single 4740 fits online brute force, which often locks the name. A spray that tries one password across many accounts is built to avoid 4740, so an alert that watches only locks will miss it. A service account that locks every hour from one PC often has a stale saved password. Record the caller, the count, and the time before you unlock anyone.\n',
        'r1': 'A lockout is a signal, not a verdict. One person can forget a password and lock once. Twenty accounts locking in five minutes is not forgetfulness. A huge burst on one name fits many guesses. Many names with one miss each may never lock. A timed lock on a service account fits a stale password. Read the pattern before you blame an intruder. Write down the names, the times, and the sources. Ask whether the person is sitting at the desk. Do not unlock the whole office before you look. Do not treat the lock itself as malicious software.\n',
        'r2': 'Hallway bolts slam shut after several bad keys. One bolt slamming while the owner stands there embarrassed is a forgotten key. Twenty bolts slamming within five minutes, tripped by one visitor who is not on the roster, is a different morning. A visitor who tries a single key on every door and never slams a bolt will not appear on a report that lists only slammed bolts. A supply-room bolt that slams every hour when the same cart arrives is a stale key left in a machine. The slam means the bolt did what it was set to do. Account lockout indicators are those slammed bolts. The pattern of slams, not any one slam, separates guessing, a broken routine, and a person who forgot.\n',
        'r4': 'List every locked account with its lock time, caller computer, and the failure count that came first. Compare the list with who is actually in the building before you unlock the set. Do not clear every lock before the source address is written down. Do not reset the whole password directory in place of reading the pattern. Leave the lock threshold at the policy value until the ticket describes the shape.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is calling every lock brute force, or expecting a spray to leave locks behind. Many guesses at one account often cause a lock. A few common passwords against many accounts are built to avoid the lock, so a quiet hour can still be that attack. Twenty accounts locked in five minutes are not one forgotten password. One lock plus a call from the desk often is. A service account that locks on a timer fits a stale saved secret. The locks are an indicator, not malicious software.\n',
    },
    'Concurrent sessions': {
        'body': "Concurrent sessions mean the same account is signed in at two places during the same minute. Both sessions are alive together. The overlap can be a shared password, a copied session, or two locations one person cannot occupy at once. It can also be ordinary, such as a phone and a desk computer on the same clinic floor. The finding gets serious when both places cannot be the user. A neighboring indicator, impossible travel, is about distance and the time between sign-ins, and the sessions do not have to overlap.\n\nA second successful sign-in does not always end the first. If the password is shared, two people can each hold a live session. If a cookie was copied, the thief's browser presents it while the nurse is still charting. Compare source addresses, the place those addresses map to, the user agent, and the start times. A known VPN exit can place a clinic laptop in another city's address space while the nurse is local. Write that down as a false lead instead of calling it theft. One session on the clinic floor and another mapped far away, in the same minute, are not a dull phone-and-desk pair.\n\nNurse Adewale is charting at 13:10 from workstation 10.1.4.20. The identity log shows a second session for that same account starting at 13:10 from an address mapped to another country, with a user agent the clinic image does not install. The sessions overlap for the next twenty minutes. Adewale is visible at the desk. No travel request is on file. The ticket fact is two live sessions, one local address, one foreign address, same minute. This account has no VPN profile, so the foreign address is not the clinic concentrator.\n\nRevoke the session the person does not recognize, and find out how the second one started. Ask Adewale, then revoke the foreign session. If the password may be shared or stolen, reset it and revoke the remaining sessions too. Disabling the account is containment when you cannot reach the nurse or the foreign session keeps coming back. That disable is not eradication, and it is not evidence of malware on the workstation. Check whether anyone else knows the password and whether a token was copied after a phish. Alert when a new session appears from an unusual place while one session is already active. Do not excuse the overlap as a personal phone when the second address is not a device you issued.\n\nImpossible travel is the trap. That indicator is two sign-ins too far apart for the minutes between them, and it remains an indicator rather than malware. Concurrent sessions are about the clock overlapping: the account is signed in at two places in the same minute. A nurse who signs out at the clinic and signs in at home that evening can be a normal second place, with no overlap and no impossible distance. The separator is two live sessions at once.\n",
        'r0': 'Concurrent sessions are two live sign-ins for one account at the same time. At 13:10 Adewale is on workstation 10.1.4.20, and a second session starts from a foreign address using a user agent the clinic image does not install. The overlap lasts about twenty minutes. The account has no VPN profile, so the foreign address is not the clinic concentrator. Impossible travel is separate: two sign-ins too far apart in too little time, and that pattern is an indicator rather than malware. A phone and a desk computer on one floor can be a harmless overlap. Revoke the session the nurse denies. Disabling the account if the foreign session returns is containment, not proof of a program on the PC.\n',
        'r1': 'The same account is signed in at two places. Both sessions are alive in the same minute. A shared password can cause that overlap. A copied login can cause it too. Two devices in one building can be normal. A far-away address makes the pair suspicious. A trip that is physically impossible is a different clue. That clue is not malicious software. A blocked page is another kind of clue. Record both addresses and both start times. Ask which session the person recognizes. Revoke the session they do not recognize. Do not wipe a computer only because two sessions exist.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic badge desk has two live badges for one nurse in the same minute. One badge is in use at the charting station. The other opens a door in a building the nurse cannot see from that chair. A second badge for a phone the clinic issued, used in the same hallway, would be dull. The distant door is the problem. Two visits hours apart, with enough time to travel, would be a complaint about speed rather than about two badges at once. Concurrent sessions are those two live badges. The account is signed in at two places during the same minute.\n',
        'r4': 'Capture both session records before you revoke either one. Write down both addresses, both start times, the user agent, and whether a VPN profile exists. Ask the person which session is theirs, and note whether the sessions truly overlapped. Do not wipe the workstation as the first step. Do not reset the password until both records are copied into the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Impossible travel is the trap. That finding is two sign-ins too far apart in too little time, and it is an indicator, not malware. Concurrent sessions need overlap: one account live in two places during the same minute. A later home sign-in, after the clinic session has ended, need not be either finding. A filter deny is blocked content, not a second session. Revoking the unrecognized session contains it. Disabling the account is containment and is not proof a program sits on the computer.\n',
    },
    'Blocked content': {
        'body': 'Blocked content means a filter did its job. A proxy, a web gateway, or a mail control refused a page, a file, or a message because a rule said to refuse it. One person hitting one blocked link is ordinary. A sudden spike can mean a campaign rather than a single misclick: the same new domain, many staff, a short window. The block is not an intrusion that succeeded. The block is the defense. The indicator worth paging on is the wave.\n\nRead the filter log for the action, the category, the destination, the user, and the time. Half the staff blocked on one new domain inside an hour fits a campaign, often a phish, a malicious ad, or a site that one poisoned page loads for everyone. One analyst blocked once on a familiar news site is a misclick or a category drawn too wide. Count distinct people, not only hits, so one person refreshing does not look like a crowd. Note whether any request was allowed before the rule caught up. An allowed twin of a blocked domain is more urgent than a clean block. Do not lift the block on the production proxy to see what the site does while you are still counting.\n\nAt 15:05 the web proxy starts denying updates-clinic-portal.example for malware. By 16:00 the log shows 140 denies, 70 distinct users, both finance and nursing, and zero allows. The mail gateway quarantines a message in that same hour whose link uses that domain. The ticket fact is one new domain, about half the people who browsed that hour, every request blocked. Nobody has a ticket saying they meant to open it. A single deny of a storage vendor at 15:40, on one admin computer, is a separate row and should not be added to this count.\n\nTreat the spike as a lead to the campaign, and leave the deny in place. Preserve the proxy lines and the quarantined message. Find the mail or the page that sent people there, and remove that lure. Tell the affected groups the domain name so the next click is a choice. If any host was allowed through before the rule, inspect that host. Isolating a computer that ran something from the site is containment. The filter rule is already doing prevention, and switching it off is not how you investigate. Do not call the deny itself an attack. The separator from normal noise is the spike: many people, one new destination, one hour.\n\nStudents reach for impossible travel or for a compromised PC on every desk. Impossible travel is two sign-ins too far apart in too little time, and it is an indicator, not malware. A denied fetch shows that the filter refused a destination. It does not put the user in two cities, and it does not prove a program is running. Two live logins would be concurrent sessions. If the stem is a proxy deny repeated across half the staff, the finding is blocked content as a campaign signal.\n',
        'r0': 'Blocked content is a filter refusing a destination, which is the control working. From 15:05 to 16:00 the proxy denied updates-clinic-portal.example 140 times across 70 users, with zero allows, in the malware category. The mail gateway quarantined a message that linked to the same domain during that hour. One deny of a known vendor on a single admin PC is not this wave. Impossible travel is two sign-ins too far apart for the clock. It is an indicator, not malware, and it is not a proxy deny. Leave the deny up. Preserve the proxy lines. Isolating a host that was allowed through before the rule is containment.\n',
        'r1': 'A filter stopped a page or a file. That means the filter did its job. One person hitting one bad link is common. Half the staff blocked on one new site is not. A wave like that fits a campaign. Count the people, not only the clicks. Look at the hour the blocks arrived. Two sign-ins at once are a different clue. A trip no person could make is different too. Do not unblock the site just to test it. Do not ignore a wave because each block worked. Write down the site, the count, and the users. A working filter is not itself an attack.\n',
        'r2': 'The clinic mail room holds back a flyer. One flyer in a single slot means a clerk did the job. A stack of the same new flyer, pulled from half the staff slots inside one hour, means a campaign reached the building. The hold is the room working, not the flyer getting through. Handing the stack out to see what happens would spread it. Two people badged into distant wings at the same minute would belong in a different log. Blocked content is that held stack. The filter refused one new destination for many people, and the refusal is the defense.\n',
        'r4': "Save the deny lines and any quarantined message before you edit the rule. Write down the destination, the category, how many people, how many hits, and whether anything was allowed. Compare that hour with yesterday's deny volume. Do not remove the block on the production filter just to inspect the site. Do not delete that hour from the proxy because the denies look repetitive.\n",
        'exam': 'The trap is calling the filter an attack, or calling one misclick a campaign. The deny is the defense at work. A spike of one new destination across many people in an hour is the campaign signal. A pair of sign-ins no person could finish in the minutes between them is a different indicator, not a proxy line. Two overlapping logins are concurrent sessions. A request allowed before the rule caught up matters more than a clean deny. Switching the filter off is not analysis.\n',
    },
    'Impossible travel': {
        'body': 'Two sign-ins count as impossible travel when the places are farther apart than the clock between them allows. A person cannot be in both places given that gap. It is an indicator, not malware. Nothing in the two authentication records proves a hostile program is sitting on the laptop. The pattern says a credential, or a session tied to it, was used in a way that body could not match. You still check the dull explanations before you call it theft.\n\nPut the two events on one timeline. Note the time in UTC, the source address, and the place the identity system assigned. Chicago at 14:10 UTC and Singapore at 14:58 UTC are on the order of 9400 miles apart, inside 48 minutes. No passenger flight covers that. A VPN exit, a cloud proxy, or a location database that mislabels a mobile carrier can fake the distance. A shared account can put two real people in two real cities. A laptop that slept can show a stale address beside a new one. Write those checks down. If the user has a trip on the calendar, the second city may be the hotel. If the user is standing in the clinic and denies the second sign-in, the indicator has become a stolen credential until you learn otherwise.\n\nJune compares two sign-ins for nurse Adewale. The identity portal shows Chicago at 14:10 UTC from the clinic address range, and Singapore at 14:58 UTC from an address the clinic does not own. Adewale is on the Chicago floor and has no travel filed. The account is not entitled to a VPN. The second event raised a multi-factor prompt, and the phone shows a prompt the nurse dismissed. The ticket fact is the pair of cities, the 48 minutes, and the dismissed prompt. The workstation has no malware alert in that hour.\n\nRespond to the credential, not to an imagined virus. Revoke the Singapore session. If the nurse did not approve it, reset the password and review the other sessions. Disabling the account is containment when the nurse cannot be reached or further sign-ins continue. That disable is containment, not eradication, and it deletes no program, because you have not shown there is one. Look at the host only when a separate behavior alert exists. Record the false path, a VPN or a shared kiosk, when that is what you find, so the next alert is faster. The alert exists so you can see the pattern while the odd session is still useful to someone else.\n\nThe trap on the exam is to answer malware or worm when the stem only describes two distant sign-ins. This finding is an indicator. Concurrent sessions are the overlap case, two live sessions in the same minute. This pair might also overlap if both stayed open, but the defining fact is distance against time. Chicago on Monday and Singapore on Wednesday, with a flight on the calendar, is ordinary travel. A blocked site is not a location. Do not pick a malicious program unless the question also gives you a program.\n',
        'r0': 'This pattern is two sign-ins separated by more distance than the minutes allow, and it is not a malicious program. Adewale signed in from the clinic range in Chicago at 14:10 UTC and from an address the clinic does not own in Singapore at 14:58 UTC, about 9400 miles in 48 minutes. The multi-factor prompt on the second event was dismissed. No VPN entitlement is assigned, which removes one common false lead. A shared account or a bad location database can still imitate the distance. Revoke the odd session. If sign-ins continue, disabling the account is containment, not eradication, and it does not delete a program you have not found.\n',
        'r1': 'Two sign-ins are too far apart for the clock. Nobody can cover that distance in that time. The pattern is a clue, not malicious software. Nothing in the two events proves a bad program. One city and another continent inside an hour fits. A virtual network exit can fake the distance. A shared login can fake the distance too. Ask whether that person actually traveled. Revoke the session they say is not theirs. Disabling a stolen account contains the incident. Containment is not the same as removing a program. Overlapping sessions are a related clue. Do not label the laptop as infected from this alone.\n',
        'r2': 'A nurse cannot stand at the Chicago clinic desk and in a Singapore lobby inside a single hour. The sign-in book shows both lines, 48 minutes apart. The book is not a scanner for hostile programs. It only records where a name was used. A shuttle, a shared name, or a clerk who wrote the wrong city can explain a line. A flight on a later day would make the second city ordinary. Two signatures in the same minute would be a complaint about overlap, not about speed. Impossible travel is that impossible pair of lines. Two sign-ins sit too far apart for the time between them, and the pair is a clue rather than a program.\n',
        'r4': 'Put the two sign-ins on one timeline before you disable anything. Write down both times in one time zone, both places, and whether a virtual network or a shared login could explain the jump. Ask the person whether they traveled and whether they approved the prompt. Do not image the laptop as the first action. Do not record the pattern as a program until a host alert says so.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is answering malware. Impossible travel is an indicator made of two sign-ins too far apart in too little time. Those events do not prove a program on the laptop. Concurrent sessions are two live logins in the same minute, a related clue with a different test. A sign-in the next day after a real flight is ordinary travel. A virtual private exit can fake the distance, so check that entitlement before you declare theft. Disabling the account contains a credential the user denies, and that step is not eradication.\n',
    },
    'Resource consumption': {
        'body': 'Resource consumption is a sudden hunger for processor, memory, disk, or bandwidth that breaks the baseline. The machine is working far harder than its history allows. The hunger can be a coin miner, mass encryption rewriting files, or a flood trying to exhaust the service. The indicator is the spike you can graph. The spike does not, by itself, say the service is already unreachable, and the spike is not a defense.\n\nSeparate the resource from the process. A miner pins the processor and may barely touch the file volume. Encryption in progress pins disk writes and often the processor, while free space falls and names change. A flood fills the network link or the connection table, and the busy process may be the service you meant to run, drowning. Compare the same hour last week. A file server at 98 percent processor for 25 minutes, writing about 180 megabytes a second, is not the weekly index if that job runs at noon and this spike is at 02:10. When the box becomes too slow to use, you also have an inaccessible resource. Name the fact you actually measured.\n\nAt 02:10 the on-call engineer sees file server FS01 at 98 percent processor, disk writes near 180 MB/s, and volume D climbing from 40 percent full toward full. The process list shows a name that is not the backup agent, reading and rewriting user files. The morning shift has not arrived. No change ticket covers a job that fills the disk. The ticket fact is the processor, the write rate, the volume, the process name, and the clock.\n\nContain a host that is rewriting files, and do not describe the spike as healthy. Isolating FS01 from the rest of the share network is containment, so the rewrite cannot walk to the next server. Preserve the process list and a few rewritten names before you stop the process, if the playbook wants that evidence. Stopping the process ends the burn and can be part of containment. Removing the program and closing the hole it used, after that evidence is saved, is eradication. A flood is met with filtering and rate limits. Those are defenses, not names for the hunger. Capacity you planned for a known backup is a baseline exception to document, not an attack. Do not rebuild the server as the first click if you have not recorded what was eating the disk.\n\nResource inaccessibility is the trap. That neighbor means the thing people need is gone, locked, or too slow to use. Consumption is the hunger on the graph: processor, disk, or bandwidth. A share already full of encrypted junk, with the processor idle at 08:00, is inaccessibility even if a spike happened at 02:10. A noisy log at a quiet hour is a different indicator. A flood tries to exhaust resources so the real service fails, and the spike may be how you notice it. The spike is not itself a filter or a rate limit.\n',
        'r0': 'Resource consumption is a measured spike in processor, disk, or bandwidth. At 02:10, FS01 sat near 98 percent processor, with disk writes around 180 MB/s and volume D filling, under a process that is not the backup agent. A miner would pin the processor and barely touch the volume. A flood would fill the link instead. Isolating FS01 is containment so the rewriting cannot spread to the next server. Filtering and rate limits are defenses against a flood, not a label for the hunger. An idle share of renamed junk at 08:00 is a different indicator, even when this spike came first. Record the process name before you stop it.\n',
        'r1': 'The machine suddenly wants too much of something. That something may be processor, disk, or bandwidth. A coin miner can pin the processor. Mass rewriting can fill a disk. A flood can eat the bandwidth. People may still reach the service during the spike. A resource nobody can open is a different clue. That clue can appear after the spike ends. Name the resource and the process behind it. Compare the spike with a quiet hour last week. Isolate a host that is rewriting files. Do not treat high use as a healthy sign. Write down the start time and the peak.\n',
        'r2': 'Every burner in the clinic kitchen is on high, and the pantry is emptying onto the counters, while the dining-room door still opens. A cook running one burner for a side task leaves the pantry mostly alone. A crowd jammed in the doorway is a different fullness, at the entrance rather than on the stove. By breakfast the stove can be cold and the food already ruined, which is a later problem. Resource consumption is the kitchen running far too hot. Processor, disk, or bandwidth is being used far past the normal hour, and that hunger is not itself a lock on the door.\n',
        'r4': 'Record the processor, disk, and network readings and the process name before you isolate the host or stop the process. Write down the start time and how this hour compared with last week. Note whether file names are changing while the disk stays busy. Do not rebuild the server as the first click. Do not ignore a rewrite in progress just because the share still answers.\n',
        'exam': 'Inaccessibility is the trap. Consumption is the spike in processor, disk, or bandwidth. A system people cannot use is the other indicator, and it can remain after the spike ends. An idle server full of renamed junk is not this graph. A flood tries to exhaust a service. Filtering is a defense against that flood, not a name for the hunger. Isolating a host that is rewriting files is containment. High use is not evidence that the system is fine.\n',
    },
    'Resource inaccessibility': {
        'body': 'Resource inaccessibility means a system people need is gone, locked, deleted, or too slow to use. The morning symptom is that the work cannot be done. The share opens and the folders are encrypted junk. A database the front desk needs has been removed. A site times out for every caller. This is an impact indicator. It is not the name of the program that caused the impact, and it is not the processor graph from earlier in the night.\n\nAsk what the user cannot do, and since when. If every folder the morning shift needs now carries a strange extension and a note demanding payment, the resource is unusable because the contents were replaced. The processor can be idle by the time the shift arrives. Any hunger spike may already be over. A share that is merely too slow because a backup holds the disk is unusable in that slow sense and is also a consumption story. Say both only when you measured both. Check the change calendar before you call it an attack. A planned outage at 06:00 that a ticket already describes is not this indicator. An unplanned blackout of the share, with no window, is.\n\nAt 06:50 the morning lead, Elena, reports that the clinic share on FS01 opens and every billing folder is a field of renamed files plus a note demanding payment by Monday. The usual applications will not open the files. The processor on FS01 is near idle, unlike the 02:10 spike already on the graph. No maintenance window is on the calendar. The ticket fact is the unusable share at 06:50, the note, and the idle processor. The earlier spike is related context. It does not replace this observation.\n\nContain, preserve, then restore. Isolate FS01 so a still-running encryptor cannot reach the next share. Isolation is containment. Copy the note and a few damaged samples into the ticket before anyone wipes the volume. Do not pay because the note says to pay. Eradication, after the samples are preserved, is removing the program and closing the hole it used. Recovery is restoring the folders from a known-good backup onto a clean system and watching Elena open a real file. Lessons learned waits until the clinic is working again. Paying to unlock files is not a phase to invent. A hole in the logs may sit beside this finding, and the hole does not rename the unusable share.\n\nConsumption is the trap. Students see the payment note and answer with the processor graph from 02:10. Hunger is the spike. Inaccessibility is the resource people cannot use. An idle server full of junk files is inaccessible. A busy server that still opens the real files is consumption. A gap in the records is not a missing folder. A public post that names the clinic might later match these files, and the post is not the same fact as Elena standing in front of an unusable share.\n',
        'r0': 'Resource inaccessibility means a needed system is locked, deleted, or too slow to use. At 06:50 Elena found the FS01 clinic share full of renamed files and a note demanding payment, while the processor was near idle. The 02:10 reading near 98 percent processor was a consumption spike, a different indicator from these unusable folders. No change window covered 06:50. Isolating FS01 so the damage cannot spread is containment. Copy the note before any wipe. Restoring folders from a known-good backup, after the samples are kept, is recovery. Paying the demand is not one of the response phases. The idle processor does not erase the morning finding.\n',
        'r1': 'A system people need is gone or unusable. It may be locked, deleted, or far too slow. The morning shift finds a share full of junk names. A note on the share demands payment. Heavy processor use is a different clue. That spike may have ended before the share died. Check the calendar for a planned outage first. Save the note and a few damaged samples. Isolate the server so damage stops spreading. Restoring files comes after you know the path in. Do not wipe the disk as your first move. A gap in the logs is a different finding.\n',
        'r2': "The clinic pantry is locked, emptied, or packed with cans nobody can open, so breakfast cannot be served. The stove may already be cold, and the cooks are standing in front of food they cannot serve. Last night's roaring burners were a different problem, about how hard the kitchen worked, not about whether breakfast exists. A closure posted on the door the day before is not a surprise. A sign demanding cash before any can will open is part of what the morning sees. Resource inaccessibility is that pantry. The thing people need is gone or unusable, whether or not the machines are busy right now.\n",
        'r4': 'Photograph the note and copy a few damaged names before you isolate the server or wipe a volume. Write down who cannot work, since when, and whether a maintenance window was on the calendar. Do not pay the note. Do not format the disk until those samples sit in the ticket. Keep backup media away from the affected server until you know the copy is clean.\n',
        'exam': 'A processor spike is the trap answer. Hunger on a graph is consumption, not the same fact as folders nobody can open. Inaccessibility means a needed system is gone, locked, deleted, or too slow to use. An idle box of renamed files still counts. A planned window already on the calendar is not an attack indicator. Isolating the server contains the spread. Wiping before the note is saved throws the evidence away. A public post naming the clinic is a claim to check, not this unusable share.\n',
    },
    'Out-of-cycle logging': {
        'body': "Out-of-cycle logging is a burst of log records, or a change to logging itself, at a time the baseline says should be quiet. The office is empty and the security log triples. Or someone changes audit policy at 02:14 with no change ticket. Attackers add noise to hide, or they edit logging so tomorrow's review is blind. A calm log at 03:00 can be normal. A log that triples at 03:00 is the indicator. A gap where records ought to exist is the opposite finding: missing records, not a burst.\n\nCompare the window with the same hour last week and with the change calendar. The security log on DC02 grows from about 50 MB to 150 MB between 03:00 and 03:20, while last Wednesday that window grew by only a few megabytes. The events on top of the burst may be failed sign-ins, a service start, or a policy edit. Event 4719, the audit policy changed, at 02:14 and with no ticket, is the configuration form of this indicator even when the file barely grows. Who changed it, and from which computer, matters as much as the size. Do not clear the log to spare the disk. Clearing destroys the burst and creates a gap you caused.\n\nSam, the night operator, pages at 03:22 because the security log on DC02 crossed the size alert. The file is 150 MB. At 02:50 it was about 50 MB. Badge records show nobody in the admin hallway. Event 4719 sits inside the burst. The caller is svc.audit, from a server that should not manage policy. No change ticket mentions audit policy this week. The ticket fact is the size jump, the clock, the 4719, and the unexpected caller. A busy Monday morning of a similar size can be the baseline and should not be labeled out of cycle.\n\nCopy the log before you tune anything. Ship it to the collector you trust, and write down the top event types plus the 4719 details. If svc.audit is not allowed to change policy, disable that account as containment, and isolate the source server if it is still making changes. Disabling the account does not put the old audit settings back. Restoring the approved policy, after you have saved who changed it, repairs the control. Do not switch auditing off to stop the disk from filling. Do not treat a large log as a healthy sign merely because logging is running. The volume at the wrong hour is the clue.\n\nA missing stretch of log is the trap. A two-hour hole means the records are absent, and the absence is its own finding. Out-of-cycle logging means extra records, or a logging change, when the baseline expected quiet. Two distant sign-ins are a travel indicator, not a file size. A full disk can come from a hungry process, and you should still say whether you measured processor and disk or an audit change at 02:14. The separator is the log's behavior against the quiet-hour baseline.\n",
        'r0': 'Out-of-cycle logging is a burst of records, or a change to logging, in an hour the baseline calls quiet. The security log on DC02 grew from about 50 MB to 150 MB between 03:00 and 03:20. Event 4719 at 02:14 records an audit-policy change by svc.audit, and no change ticket exists. The same window last week grew only a few megabytes. A two-hour hole with no events would be missing logs, which is the opposite finding. Do not clear the file. Disabling svc.audit is containment when that account must not edit policy. Putting the approved policy back waits until the 4719 details are saved.\n',
        'r1': 'Logs jump in size when the office should be quiet. A logging change at that hour counts too. The security log can triple after midnight. Last week the same hour was calm. A missing stretch of log is a different clue. A gap means you lack records. Extra records are the opposite problem. See which events dominate the burst. See whether the audit settings changed. Do not clear the log to make it smaller. Clearing it destroys evidence and creates a gap. Write down the size, the window, and the top events. Match the window to change tickets. No ticket makes a logging change suspicious.\n',
        'r2': 'The clinic logbook should gain only a few lines after midnight. One night the book triples between three and half past three, and a line says the rules for what gets written down were changed, with no work order on the board. A blank stretch where lines should be is a different fault, a missing page. Tearing pages out to slim the book creates that blank and destroys the burst. A busy Monday that fills the book at the usual pace is no surprise. Out-of-cycle logging is the fat book in a quiet building. The volume, or a change to what gets recorded, arrived when the hour should have stayed calm.\n',
        'r4': 'Copy the log, or confirm the collector already has it, before you touch audit settings. Write down the size before and after, the clock window, the top event types, and who changed policy. Match that window to open change tickets. Do not clear the log to free disk space. Do not turn auditing off in order to stop the file from growing.\n',
        'exam': 'A gap in the record is the trap. Missing logs mean the events are absent, and that absence is its own finding. Out-of-cycle logging is extra volume, or a change to logging, in an hour the baseline calls quiet. Clearing the file to save disk creates the gap and destroys the burst. A Monday surge that matches the previous Monday is baseline. Two far-apart sign-ins are not a file-size clue. Disabling the account that edited policy contains that account and does not restore the old settings.\n',
    },
    'Published attacker claims': {
        'body': 'Published attacker claims are posts, leak-site listings, or open bragging that name your organization. They are a lead. They are not proof. A group can lie to look busy. A group can also post real samples before the clinic notices a loss. The useful move is to verify the claim against your own data and then decide.\n\nVerify without spreading the sample. Record the page address, the time you saw it, and the exact names or snippets shown. Search the file servers for those names. If the post includes file contents, compare a hash of the sample with a hash of the internal file in a closed room, and do not mail the sample around. Check access logs and copy jobs for a matching export. Line the post date up with any gap in logging or any large copy off the payroll share. Names that exist, a hash that matches, and an export nobody ticketed turn the lead into evidence that data left. Names that match nothing, and a generic sample, stay a closed lead with notes. Do not pay because a page demanded payment. Do not publish a denial before the comparison is finished.\n\nA leak site lists Riverside Clinic and shows three file names from the payroll folder: march-overtime.xlsx, nurse-roster.csv, and bonus-grid.xlsx. June hashes the posted march-overtime.xlsx sample and the copy on the payroll share. The hashes match. File-server audit shows an archive of that folder copied to an outside address two weeks earlier, and nobody ticketed the copy. The other two names exist on the share. Their contents are not in the post, only the names. The ticket fact is the matching hash, the three names, the unticketed copy, and the time on the page. The post is no longer a rumor.\n\nLet a match drive the incident, and let a non-match close the lead without a public statement. Preserve the page, the sample hash, and the export log. If data left, contain the path: disable the account that performed the copy, and block the destination if it is still reachable. Those steps are containment. Tell leadership what matched and what did not. A public boast is not a program on a PC, and it is not proof the share is unusable right now. An unusable share would be a person unable to open the folders. A hole in the audit trail would be missing records. You might find either while you verify, and each keeps its own name.\n\nThe trap is to treat the post as proof, or to treat it as nothing. The separator is the comparison with data you hold. A matching sample hash is evidence the named file is yours. A post with no match is a lead you close in the ticket. Distant sign-ins, a pegged processor, and a locked account are other indicators. A screenshot does not replace them. Do not call the claim a confirmed loss unless the stem shows the match, or the stem already hands you the matching files.\n',
        'r0': 'A published claim is a post or leak-site listing that names the organization, and it is a lead until you verify it. The Riverside listing showed march-overtime.xlsx, nurse-roster.csv, and bonus-grid.xlsx. The hash of the posted overtime sample matched the copy on the payroll share. Audit recorded an unticketed archive of that folder sent to an outside address two weeks earlier. Names that exist on no server stay a lead you can close with a note. Do not pay from the screenshot. Disabling the account that copied the archive is containment. The post is not a program on a PC and does not, by itself, prove the share is unusable today.\n',
        'r1': 'A public post names your organization. Leak sites and open bragging both count. Treat the post as a lead, then check it. A lead is not proof of a breach. See whether the sample file names are real. Match those names to a folder you have. Compare the sample with your own copy. See whether anyone exported those files. Some posts are boasts with nothing behind them. A true post can show up before you notice. Do not pay anyone because of a screenshot. Do not issue a public denial before the check. Record the page, the time, and the match.\n',
        'r2': 'A notice on the town board says the clinic lost its payroll papers and shows three file titles. The notice might be an empty brag, or it might be a photocopy of real pages. The clerk does not call a press conference and does not hand over cash. The clerk walks to the cabinet, looks for those titles, and compares one page. A match means the notice included a real document. No match means the notice stays a tip that gets filed. Published attacker claims are that notice. They stay a lead until your own files confirm the comparison or fail it.\n',
        'r4': 'Save the page address, the time, and the sample before you comment in public or pay anyone. Write down each displayed file name and whether that name exists on your servers. Record the hash comparison and any copy the audit log did not already have a ticket for. Do not open the sample on a regular workstation. Do not delete the payroll originals while the match is still underway.\n',
        'exam': 'Treating the post as proof, or treating it as nothing, is the trap. The separator is a comparison against files you already hold. A matching sample hash supports the claim that the named file is yours. No match leaves a lead the ticket can close. The post is not a program on a workstation and is not, alone, an unusable share. Distant sign-ins and a processor spike remain separate indicators. Disabling the account that copied the files contains that path. Paying because a screenshot said to pay is not a response step.\n',
    },
    'Missing logs': {
        'body': 'A gap where a log should be is a finding, not a quiet night. The lines you expected from a source never arrived, for minutes or for hours. Attackers delete logs, and broken collectors drop them, and both stories leave the same hole. The hole does not prove which story is true. You cannot treat a blank page as proof the night was empty. The finding stops a false close. It does not undo whatever ran during the hole, and it does not by itself name a person.\n\nThe record is built in two steps, and either step can fail. The host writes events into a local file or a local channel. A forwarder copies those events to a central store. A gap opens when someone clears the local file, when the logging service stops, when the disk fills, or when the forwarder loses its path. On a Windows domain controller, event 1102 says the audit log was cleared, but only if that audit policy is still turned on. If event 1102 is missing too, the log that should have recorded the clear was part of the same gap. A forwarder that kept sending a heartbeat every minute, while the authentication lines stopped, points at the writer rather than the network path.\n\nPriya Shah takes the clinic incident on a Thursday morning. Payroll folder names changed overnight, and she opens the authentication log for DC-CLINIC-01. Lines are steady until 01:14, then nothing until 03:22. The collector health page shows a heartbeat from that domain controller every minute across the same window, so the forwarder was still connected. She writes the host name, the two timestamps, and the note that event 1102 does not appear in what survived. She does not type a cause into the ticket yet. She asks for any local log backup, and she lists every account that is allowed to clear the Security log.\n\nDefenders close a gap by testing both stories against clocks and files. Compare the host clock to the collector clock before you call the hole an intrusion. Look for a service restart, a full volume, or an agent that crashed at 01:14. Look for a stopped audit policy or a file whose size dropped instead of growing. If a change ticket covers that window, write the ticket number beside the timestamps. If nothing covers it, keep the remaining logs and raise the incident before anyone rotates the file. An alert when a noisy source goes silent for fifteen minutes catches the next hole while it is still small. That alert does not name a culprit.\n\nStudents mix this finding up with out-of-cycle logging. Out-of-cycle logging is extra noise, or a logging change, at an hour the baseline says should be quiet. Missing logs are the opposite shape: lines a busy source should have written, which never showed up. A calm night can be normal. A two-hour hole in a log that usually writes every minute is a failed record, and the failure is the clue.',
        'r0': 'Event 1102 is the Windows Security record that someone cleared the audit log, and it is written only while changes to audit policy are still audited. A hole from 01:14 to 03:22 with no 1102 and no logging-service shutdown can be a cleared file or a writer that stopped. Separate those by the forwarder. Heartbeats every 60 seconds across those same two hours mean the path to the collector stayed up, so the gap sits on the domain controller. A host clock that jumped forward can imitate a hole by parking events under later timestamps. Write the host, both edges of the gap, whether 1102 is present, and whether heartbeats continued. Treating the blank stretch as ordinary quiet time drops the finding.',
        'r1': 'A missing log is a hole in the record. The hole is a clue by itself. It does not mean the night was quiet. A person can wipe the local file. A broken collector can leave the same hole. Check the clock on the computer. Check the clock on the central store. See if heartbeats kept arriving. Write down when the hole starts. Write down when the lines return. Save whatever lines are still there. Do not invent a story for the gap. A quiet hour can still be normal. A hole in a busy log is different. Ask who is allowed to erase logs.',
        'r2': 'A clinic keeps a sign-in book at the front desk. Every visitor writes a name and a time. On Tuesday the book runs normally, then a stretch from just after one until past three is simply blank. The pen is still on the desk. The camera in the lobby kept filming the whole time. You do not shrug and say nobody came. You ask whether someone tore pages out, or whether the book was left unused while the desk was empty. The pages that remain stay in the drawer until you know which story is true. The book is the authentication log. A blank stretch while the collector kept sending heartbeats is a missing-log finding, not an empty night.',
        'r4': 'Open the authentication log for the night in question. Mark the last line before the hole and the first line after it. Write down the host name, both timestamps, and whether a clear-log event remains. Check whether the collector received heartbeats through that window, and write that down too. Do not rotate, clear, or restart logging yet. Do not fill the gap with a theory in the ticket. Save a copy of what is left before anyone changes the source.',
        'exam': 'A gap is not proof that nothing happened. Missing logs and out-of-cycle logging are opposite shapes: expected lines that never arrived, versus a burst in an hour the baseline says should be quiet. A broken collector and a person who cleared the file can look the same until heartbeats, clocks, and event 1102 are compared. Do not call the gap containment or eradication. It is a detection finding. Closing the ticket because the surviving lines look calm skips the hole itself.',
    },
    'Segmentation': {
        'body': 'Segmentation splits a network so a problem in one part cannot freely walk into another. Guest wireless is one part. Payroll servers are another. Between them sits a boundary that is allowed to refuse. The split stops a guest laptop from opening a session to a payroll host. It does not stop an attack that starts inside the payroll part, and it does not repair a weak password on a server that already belongs there.\n\nDesigners make the split real by giving each part its own network and putting a filter on the path between them. A switch can mark the parts so they do not share a broadcast domain. Those marks are not the control by themselves. If a router passes every packet from the guest side to the payroll side, the visitor still arrives. The firewall or the router filter has to drop what the map says should not cross. Segmentation is the arrangement of parts plus a boundary that actually denies.\n\nLuis Ortiz sits in the clinic lobby and joins the guest wireless on his laptop. He tries the payroll file server, the way a curious visitor or a compromised phone might. The session fails. The firewall log between the guest network and the server network records a deny from 10.20.14.50 to 10.40.0.12 on TCP 445. The same laptop still loads a public web page, because that path is allowed. Luis copies the source, the destination, the port, the time, and the rule name into ticket NET-2201. One refused path is evidence. It is not a tour of every rule.\n\nDefenders keep the parts honest by drawing them before a new system is plugged in. They list what each part may reach, then they try those reaches from a host on the less trusted side. They watch the deny log during the test, and they put an end date on any temporary hole a project demands. A server that just needs a spare cable is how a payroll host ends up beside the guests. The boundary limits spread from part to part. Machines that share a part can still harm one another, because no boundary sits between them.\n\nStudents treat least privilege as if it were this split. Least privilege limits the rights on an account. Which machines may talk is the network split. A front-desk account with tiny rights, placed on a flat network that can reach every server, has not created separate parts. An access control list may be the tool that enforces one boundary, and file permissions may protect one document, but neither item is the map of those parts.',
        'r0': 'Guest address 10.20.14.50 should never complete a TCP handshake to payroll host 10.40.0.12 on port 445. The deny belongs on the filter that sits between those two networks, and the log line is the proof, not a diagram alone. A VLAN tag can keep broadcasts apart and still fail the goal if inter-VLAN routing forwards every packet. Segmentation is the parts plus that refusing boundary. Test from the guest side: the file session must fail, and an allowed public web session may still succeed. A temporary permit needs an end date. The split does not harden a weak password on a host that already lives inside payroll.',
        'r1': 'Segmentation splits the network into separate parts. A problem in one part should stay there. Guest wireless is one part. Payroll servers are another part. Traffic between them must cross a boundary. That boundary is allowed to refuse. A guest laptop should not reach payroll. This split does not fix a weak password. It does not protect machines in the same part. A label on a switch is not enough. Something must actually filter the path. Draw the parts before you add a server. Test from the less trusted side. Watch for a refused connection. Least privilege is about accounts, not rooms. File rights do not move a server.',
        'r2': 'Think of a clinic built as two wings with a fire door between them. Visitors wait in the lobby wing. Payroll records stay in the back wing. The fire door can be closed, and a closed door keeps smoke from walking the hall. Painting a sign on the wall does not hold smoke if the door is propped open. A person who already works in the back wing is not stopped by that door at all. The wings are the network parts, and the fire door is the boundary that actually refuses traffic. Least privilege is about which keys a person carries, which is a different decision from where the walls stand.',
        'r4': 'Draw the parts you claim are separate, and pick one path that should fail, such as guest wireless to a payroll server. From a host on the less trusted side, try that path and watch the boundary log. Write down the source, the destination, the result, and the rule that refused it. Try one path that should still work, and write that result too. Do not add a temporary allow while you are only testing. Do not move a server into a new part until the drawing shows where it belongs.',
        'exam': 'Segmentation is the split between network parts, enforced by a boundary that can refuse. Least privilege limits account rights and does not draw those parts. An access control list can be the tool on one boundary, and file permissions protect an object, but neither one is the map. A label on a switch does not segment anything if routing still passes every packet. The control stops cross-boundary reach, such as guest wireless to payroll. It does not stop trouble that starts inside the trusted part.',
    },
    'Access control lists': {
        'body': 'An access control list is an ordered set of allow and deny rules that a device reads from the top. The first rule that matches a packet is the decision, and the rules below it do not get a vote. The list stops traffic that hits a deny, or that falls through to the implicit deny many devices add at the bottom. It does not stop traffic that an earlier, wider allow already accepted. A careful deny buried under a permit for all web traffic never sees the packet.\n\nOrder is the whole mechanism. A typical edge list permits TCP 443 and then denies everything else. A guest packet to port 443 matches the permit and is forwarded. A guest packet to port 22 does not match the permit, matches the deny, and is dropped. If someone later inserts a broad permit for every address and every protocol above that deny, every tighter line underneath goes dark. Operators call the hidden line a shadowed rule. You find it by reading the list from the top and by checking hit counters, not by assuming the last line is the one that mattered.\n\nOwen Brooks reviews the clinic firewall after a vendor asks that every service be opened for an install. The live list still has two effective lines: permit TCP 443, then deny the rest. From a guest laptop he tries a remote shell toward a server and watches the deny counter increase. He tries an ordinary web request and watches the permit counter increase. He writes both results, the line numbers, and the time into the change ticket. He refuses to paste the vendor permit at the top. The list, as it stands, stops the shell attempt. It does nothing about a harmful payload that rides inside the permitted web session.\n\nDefenders treat the list as text you can get wrong quietly. They put specific denies and narrow permits where the reader will reach them, they put the broad cleanup deny last, and they read the lines aloud in order before installing them. They keep the previous list so a bad change can be reversed. They log the denies they care about, because a refusal nobody records is only a rumor. File permissions answer who may touch an object. This list answers which packets may pass. It can enforce one wall in a segmented network, and it is still not the map of the parts.\n\nStudents swap the packet list with file permissions because both end in a yes or a no. Permissions say who may read, change, or run one object. A packet list, used this way, says which flows may pass a device, and the first match wins. A mode on a payroll file cannot drop a guest session to port 22. A line that permits TCP 443 cannot decide that only the payroll group may open a spreadsheet.',
        'r0': 'Read an access list from the top, because the first match wins and later lines stay silent. Permit TCP 443, then deny every other protocol, and a guest packet to port 22 dies on the deny while a packet to port 443 is forwarded on the permit. Many router lists also end in an implicit deny, so a packet that matches nothing is dropped even without a final written line. A permit for all traffic inserted above the deny shadows every tighter rule below it. Check hit counters after a test: the shell attempt should increment the deny, and the web attempt should increment the permit. The list does not set a file mode.',
        'r1': 'An access list is a stack of rules. Each rule says allow or deny. The device reads from the top. The first rule that fits is the one that wins. Later rules do not get a vote. A wide allow near the top can hide a deny. Put the specific deny where it will be seen. A guest on a blocked service should fail. Allowed web traffic can still carry trouble. The list does not decide file rights. File rights do not decide this list. Write the rules in order before you install them. Test a blocked path and an open path. Read the hit counts after the test.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse dock has a clipboard the guard must read from the top. The first line that fits the truck is the decision, and the guard does not keep reading. If the top line says any bakery truck may enter, a later line that bans one bakery truck never gets a look. The banned truck rolls in, and the later line looks strict while doing nothing. A final line that says turn everyone else away only matters for trucks that matched nothing above. The clipboard is the access list, and top-to-bottom order is why a wide allow can hide a deny. File-cabinet keys are a different question from which trucks may cross the dock.',
        'r4': 'Print the live list and number the lines from the top. Mark the first line a test packet would hit, and write down that line number rather than a later line you wish would matter. From a guest host, try a path that should be denied and a path that should be allowed. Write down both results and the hit counts. Do not insert a permit-everything line at the top while you test. Do not delete the previous list until the new one has been read back in order.',
        'exam': 'The first matching line wins, so a broad allow above a careful deny makes the deny unreachable. Students who start reading at the bottom will pick the wrong line. A packet list is not a file permission: mode and group decide who may touch an object, while this list decides which flows pass a device. Permitting TCP 443 and denying the rest stops a guest shell on port 22 and still lets a bad payload through the allowed web flow. The list can sit on a segment boundary and is not the segmentation map itself.',
    },
    'Permissions': {
        'body': 'Permissions state which accounts may read, change, or run a file or another object. On a Unix-style server the mode is a compact form of that statement. Mode 640 gives the owner read and write, gives the named group read, and gives everyone else nothing. The payroll rates file is mode 640 and its group is payroll, so a clerk outside that group cannot read it. The mode stops that outsider. It does not stop a member of the payroll group, and it does not stop a process that is already running as the owner.\n\nThe check happens when an account touches the object, not when a packet crosses a firewall. The system looks at the owner, the group, and the mode, or at the allow and deny entries stored on a Windows object, and then allows or refuses the operation. Share rights and file rights can both exist on the same folder. When they do, the tighter of the two is what the user actually gets across the share. A share opened to everyone, sitting on a file that is mode 640, still refuses the outsider at the file. A world-readable file behind a tight share still opens for anyone who reaches it another way, such as a local logon or a leftover copy.\n\nHelen Nguyen audits the clinic file server after a contractor report. She lists the rates file and sees -rw-r----- with group payroll. Her test account, which is not in that group, receives permission denied on a read. A second test account that is in the group can read and cannot write, which is the 4 in 640. She records the path, the mode, the owner, and the group in the ticket. She also finds a copy of the same rates in a transfer folder with mode 644, so others can read that copy. The tight mode on the original path does not cover the copy.\n\nDefenders inventory sensitive objects and the groups that can touch them. They remove broad entries such as everyone-read when the job does not need them. They check copies, backups, and mail attachments, because a careful mode on one path is wasted if a loose copy sits beside it. They separate read from change: the payroll group here can see the file and cannot edit it. Permissions limit accounts at the object. They do not move a server onto another network, and they do not drop a packet at a firewall.\n\nStudents call a firewall rule a permission because both end in a yes or a no. A firewall access list is ordered, and the first matching line wins for a packet. A file mode is not a top-to-bottom packet filter, and it does not mention port 22 or port 443. Segmentation is a third idea: it places systems in different parts of the network. Mode 640 can be perfect while the server is still reachable from guest wireless, if no boundary exists.',
        'r0': 'Mode 640 is owner read and write, group read, others nothing, which lists as -rw-r-----. Put group payroll on the rates file and a clerk outside that group gets permission denied on read, while a group member can read and cannot write. Mode 644 would add read for everyone else, which is the mistake on the transfer-folder copy. When a share permission and a file permission both apply, the tighter one wins for access through the share. A local logon or a second copy can bypass the share. The mode does not drop TCP 445, and it does not create a network segment.',
        'r1': 'Permissions say who may touch a file. Touch can mean read, change, or run. The owner can have one set of rights. A named group can have a smaller set. Everyone else can have none. A payroll file can be limited this way. People outside that group cannot read it. People inside the group still can. This does not split the network. A firewall list is a different tool. A copy of the file can escape the limit. Check the owner, the group, and the mode. Remove broad groups that should not be there. Do not lock out the job that must read it.',
        'r2': 'A clinic keeps payroll papers in a cabinet. The owner of the cabinet has a key that opens and changes the papers. People in the payroll office have a key that lets them look, not edit. Everyone else has no key at all. That arrangement stops a stranger from reading the folder. It does not stop a payroll clerk who was given the look key, and it does not help if someone already photocopied the papers into an open tray. The cabinet keys are file permissions. A guard at the building entrance is a different control, because the entrance decides who reaches the hall, not who may open this drawer.',
        'r4': 'List the sensitive file and write down the owner, the group, and the mode. With a test account outside the group, try to read it, and write down the refusal. With a test account inside the group, try to read and try to change, and write down both results. Search for a second copy in transfer folders and mail. Do not loosen the mode to make the test easier. Do not remove the payroll group until you know who still must read the file.',
        'exam': 'Mode 640 lets the owner read and write, the named group read, and everyone else nothing. Students who treat 644 as the same mode miss the read right given to others. A packet access list is ordered and the first match wins; a file mode is not that list and does not block a port. Share rights and file rights can both exist, and the tighter one applies through the share. A second copy with a looser mode escapes the original file. Permissions are not segmentation.',
    },
    'Application allow lists': {
        'body': 'An application allow list lets named programs run and refuses everything else. The clinic PC does not ask whether a downloaded viewer looks friendly. If that viewer is not named, it does not start. The list stops unknown executables, including a trojan dressed up as a viewer. It does not stop abuse of a program that is already allowed, and it does not repair a flaw inside an allowed program.\n\nThe decision happens at launch, under a policy on the computer. Strong rules name a publisher certificate or a hash of the exact file. A hash rule no longer matches after the vendor ships a new build, so the new hash has to be added before users need the program. Weaker rules name a folder or a file name, and a bad program dropped into an allowed folder can satisfy a path rule. Teams often start in audit, where the computer records what would have been blocked and still lets it run. They move to enforce only after the audit matches real clinic work. A prevented start is recorded as event 8004 when AppLocker is the tool.\n\nJonah Patel watches a front-desk PC after a user says a new invoice viewer will not open. The file is C:\\Users\\Sam\\Downloads\\viewer.exe. The log shows event 8004 for that path, with the file hash, at 09:18. The hash is not in the allow policy, and the publisher is not one the clinic trusts. Jonah writes the path, the hash, the user, and the time in the ticket. He does not add the hash just to quiet the morning. The viewer stays blocked. Mail and the spreadsheet, both named in the policy, still open normally.\n\nDefenders build the list from a clean image rather than from a PC that has collected junk. They keep an audit log across a full business cycle before they enforce. They prefer publisher or hash rules for software that should survive updates, and they treat a request to allow a new tool as a change with an owner. When someone abuses an allowed tool, they look at the child process and the user, because the parent was supposed to start. Patching still has to close known holes in the programs the list permits. Isolation still has to limit where a risky machine may connect. The list only answers whether a program may launch.\n\nStudents pick the list when the story is really a missing fix, or when a machine can reach too far. The list does not install a vendor patch, and it does not remove a route to the file servers. Software that quarantines known-bad files makes a different bet: unknowns may run until they look malicious. An allow list refuses the unknown even when nobody has called it malicious yet.',
        'r0': 'AppLocker event 8004 means an executable was prevented from running. A hash rule binds that decision to the exact file bytes, so a new vendor build will not run until its new hash is approved. A path rule is weaker, because a file placed in an allowed directory can match without being the program you meant. Audit mode logs what would have been blocked and still allows the start; enforce mode is the real refusal. On the clinic PC, viewer.exe under the user Downloads folder is blocked at 09:18 while named mail and spreadsheet programs still start. The list does not patch those allowed programs.',
        'r1': 'An allow list names the programs that may run. Anything not named is refused. A downloaded viewer fails if it is not named. The computer can show a block message. This is stricter than hunting only bad files. A known good program can still be misused. The list does not repair a buggy program. It does not hide the computer from the network. Build the list from a clean image. Watch what would have been blocked first. Write down the file name when something is blocked. Ask whether that file should be added. Do not add a program just to quiet a user.',
        'r2': 'A hospital kitchen cooks only from cards in a locked box. If a dish has no card, the cooks do not invent it for a visitor. A slick package left on the counter stays uncooked, even if the label looks helpful. A card that is only a shelf name is weaker than a card that names the exact sealed packet, because someone can swap what sits on that shelf. The kitchen still has to throw out spoiled food that was on a real card. The cards are the allow list. A program that is not named does not launch, and a program that is named can still be misused or buggy.',
        'r4': 'Find the block record for the program that failed to start. Write down the full path, the user, the time, and the file hash. Check whether the policy names that hash or that publisher. See whether mail and other named programs still start. Do not add the hash to the list just to finish the ticket. Do not switch a whole machine from audit to enforce until you have read what the audit would have stopped.',
        'exam': 'An allow list refuses programs that are not named, even when nobody has a signature for them. Quarantine of known-bad files is a different bet, because unknowns may still run. A hash rule breaks on a new build until the new hash is added; a path rule can be satisfied by a stranger dropped in an allowed folder. The list does not patch an allowed program and does not isolate a host. Event 8004 is a prevented start, not a firewall deny and not a missing software update.',
    },
    'Isolation': {
        'body': 'Isolation keeps a risky system able to do its job and unable to reach everything else. The clinic still needs the lab instrument. The instrument still runs an old build the vendor will not fix. That placement stops the box from opening the internet and from opening the clinic file shares. It does not remove the flaw, and it does not stop a person at the console from misusing the instrument locally.\n\nThe mechanism is a small network with almost no paths out. The instrument keeps a cable and an address so the lab tech can collect results. It has no default route into the rest of the building or out to the internet. A filter permits only the one historian that must receive readings, and it denies every other destination. If someone later plugs the instrument into the ordinary staff network, the leash is gone even though the flaw never changed. The design has to include the wall port, the patch-panel label, and a rule that this device is not a roaming laptop.\n\nAisha Diaz places the blood-gas analyzer on that tight segment after the vendor confirms no fix is coming. From the instrument she can store a reading on the historian at 10.50.0.2. A test toward the file server fails, and a test toward a public address fails. The filter log shows both denies. She records the instrument name, the single allowed partner, and the failed tests in the ticket. The analyzer still completes a real sample. The old flaw is still in the software. What changed is the set of systems that flaw can touch.\n\nDefenders reach for this pattern when a patch does not exist and the business cannot turn the device off. They write the allowed path in the same ticket that waives the missing fix. They review the filter on a schedule, because a later project will ask for one more destination. They do not treat the waiver as permission to join the guest wireless. An application allow list would limit which programs start, which may be impossible on a sealed instrument. What you limited here is reach, as a stand-in for a hole that patching cannot close yet.\n\nStudents use one word for two different moments. A planned limit leaves a working system in production for as long as the risk remains. Cutting a compromised laptop off the network during an incident is containment: that laptop is not being kept as a production tool, and the goal is to stop spread while you investigate. An allow list and a patch are the other neighbors. Neither one is built by removing routes.',
        'r0': 'The unpatched analyzer has no default route. The only permitted partner is the historian at 10.50.0.2, and every other destination is denied by the filter in front of that segment. A test to the file servers fails, and a test to a public address fails, while a real sample still lands on the historian. Pulling the cable into the staff network deletes the control without touching the flaw. Planned isolation is a lasting mitigation for a box that must keep working. Cutting a compromised laptop away during an incident is containment, a different moment with a different goal. An allow list does not remove routes.\n',
        'r1': 'Isolation lets a risky machine keep doing its job. It stops that machine from reaching everything else. A lab tool that cannot be fixed still has to run. Place it where the work can finish. Do not let it reach the public internet. Do not let it reach the shared files. The flaw inside the tool is still there. You only limited where it can talk. This is not a software fix. This is not a list of programs. Unplugging a sick laptop during trouble is different. Planned isolation stays up on purpose. Check that it cannot open a shared folder.',
        'r2': 'A clinic keeps one fragile machine in a side room so the lab can still use it. The tech can walk in, run a sample, and leave the result in one tray by the door. There is no hallway from that room to the records wing, and there is no door to the street. The machine is still old, and a person standing next to it can still press the wrong button. Moving it back into the open office would undo the room without repairing the machine. The side room is isolation. The instrument can finish its job, and it cannot wander to the file shares or the public network.',
        'r4': 'From the risky system, try the one job it must still do, and write down the success. Then try a file share and a public address, and write down both failures plus the filter lines that denied them. Record the partner address that is allowed. Do not add a default route to make a test more convenient. Do not describe this lasting placement as incident containment. Leave the patch waiver and the allowed path in the same ticket.',
        'exam': 'Isolation leaves a risky system working and takes away paths it does not need. It does not patch the flaw and does not decide which programs may launch. Students confuse it with containment. Containment cuts a suspect host off during an incident so spread stops while you investigate. Planned isolation keeps a production device in service on purpose because no fix exists. A missing default route, with one allowed partner, is the mechanism. Plugging the device back into the staff network ends the control.',
    },
    'Patching': {
        'body': "Patching applies the vendor's fix so a known hole closes. The vendor ships a corrected build, the clinic installs that build, and the flawed code is no longer what runs. The update stops the particular bug the note describes, on the machines that actually run the new build. It does not stop a flaw that still has no patch. Bringing every other package up to date leaves that unfixed flaw open.\n\nThe work is a change with a check, not a hope. You match the vendor note to the product you run, you install inside a window the business accepts, and you read the version back from the machine. A pilot comes first so a bad package hits fifteen desks instead of every desk. If the pilot still shows the old build, the hole is still there no matter what the ticket claims. Machines outside the pilot stay exposed until their turn. A pending reboot can leave new files on disk while the old code keeps running, so the version that matters is the one in memory after the restart the note requires.\n\nNora Berg runs the Thursday 21:00 window for clinic browsers. The vendor note names build 131.0.6778.85 as the release that closes this week's known flaw. On the fifteen pilot PCs she reads that build. A desk outside the pilot still shows 130.0.6723.70. She writes both versions, the pilot list, and the window into ticket CHG-4412. She does not mark the clinic complete. The known hole is closed on the pilot. It stays open on the old build, and any flaw the vendor has not fixed is open on both.\n\nDefenders keep an inventory so a note can be matched to a real product. They pilot, they confirm the running version, and they finish the rest of the fleet on a clock. They hold the previous package until the pilot is clearly healthy, so a rollback exists. When a serious flaw has no fix yet, they choose another control, such as isolation or an allow list, and they record why a patch cannot be the answer this week. During an incident, installing the fix after evidence is preserved is eradication of that vulnerability. Disabling a stolen account, or cutting a live host off the network, is containment, which is an earlier phase.\n\nStudents treat a finished patch cycle as cover against bugs nobody has fixed. Patching closes known holes. It does not stop a flaw with no patch yet. Isolation does not rewrite the binary, and an allow list does not either. A healthy pilot is not the rest of the building. The fact that separates this control from its neighbors is the version you can read on the machine, matched to the version the vendor named.",
        'r0': 'The Thursday window at 21:00 covers a pilot of fifteen browsers. The vendor note names build 131.0.6778.85 as the build that closes the known flaw, and those pilot PCs show that number after the job. A machine outside the pilot still shows 130.0.6723.70, so the same flaw is still present there. Read the running version, not only the installer log, because a pending reboot can leave old code in memory. The package does not close a flaw that has no vendor build yet. Isolation or an allow list can reduce exposure until a build exists, and neither one is a version check. Ticket CHG-4412 should list both builds and the pilot names.',
        'r1': 'Patching puts the vendor fix onto the system. A known hole closes when the fix is really there. A hole with no fix yet stays open. Updating other software does not close that hole. Try the fix on a small group first. Check the version after the install. The pilot group should show the new version. The rest of the fleet may still be old. A patch does not remove a program you do not need. It does not wall a machine off from the network. Write down the version you expected. Write down the version you actually see. Do not call the fleet done after the pilot.',
        'r2': 'A workshop replaces a cracked pane when the glass shop has a matching sheet in stock. The crew tries the new pane on one exam room before they order it for every room. If that room still shows the crack, the delivery did not take. Rooms that have not been visited still have the crack even after the storeroom celebrates. A crack the shop cannot supply a sheet for stays, no matter how many other panes you replace. The pane is the vendor fix, and the rooms are the computers. Patching closes a known flaw where the new build is actually running, and it does not close a flaw that still has no fix.',
        'r4': "Read the vendor note and write down the build it names. After the pilot window, read the running version on a pilot machine and on a machine outside the pilot. Write both versions and the ticket number. Do not mark the whole clinic done from the pilot result. Do not skip the restart the note requires. If the note lists no fix yet, do not pretend another product's update closed this hole.",
        'exam': 'Patching closes known holes on hosts that run the fixed build. It does not stop a flaw with no patch yet. A pilot on build 131.0.6778.85 does not speak for a PC still on the older build. Isolation removes routes, and an allow list blocks unnamed programs. Neither rewrites the binary. During an incident, installing the fix after evidence is preserved is eradication. Disabling a stolen account is containment, an earlier phase. A pending reboot can leave the old code running after the files are on disk.',
    },
    'Encryption as a mitigation': {
        'body': 'Encryption as a mitigation means a stolen copy stays unreadable when the key stayed somewhere else. The clinic encrypts a backup before writing it to tape. A person who walks out with cartridge TP-1884 holds ciphertext. That step stops the carrier from reading the records. It does not stop the walk-out, and it fails if the key was packed in the same case.\n\nLarge backups use a shared-key cipher because the volume is huge. AES-256 is the right choice here: one secret key, fast enough for a full tape, with a 256-bit key. The key has to survive for a later restore, so it lives in the clinic hardware security module, a separate appliance many systems can call for high-value keys. That appliance is not a TPM. A TPM is a chip on one computer that seals material to that single platform. A restore may run on a different host, so this key belongs in the module, not in a chip on the backup server, and never in a file sitting next to the tape.\n\nEvan Silva handles the off-site pickup. The job log says AES-256 and names the module, not a key file on the server disk. The cartridge label and the media log both read TP-1884, and the case pocket has no key material. He writes those facts on the transfer form. Months later a carrier loses a cartridge. The bytes on it stay unreadable without the module. The loss is still an incident, because the copy left. A page about a missing bag would have been monitoring. The cipher did not send that page.\n\nDefenders choose what must be encrypted, where keys live, and who may ask the module to unwrap. They rehearse a restore so the key is not a theory. They can also protect a path across a network, and they still encrypt the stored copy, because a protected path does not protect a tape in a truck. Tight account rights might have limited who may start the backup job. Those rights would not make a finished tape unreadable. A hash is the other mix-up: SHA-256 can fingerprint the cartridge so a change is visible, and a fingerprint does not hide the contents.\n\nStudents describe encryption as watching, or as a tighter set of rights. Monitoring tells you an event happened. Least privilege reduces who can take the copy. Encryption assumes the copy may leave and makes the bytes useless without the key. If the key left with the tape, the mitigation failed even though the cipher was strong. If a screen was unlocked and the file was open, the reader was looking at plaintext the cipher was not covering at that moment.',
        'r0': 'Backup cartridge TP-1884 is encrypted with AES-256, one shared key fast enough for a full tape, and that key is not stored on the cartridge. The key lives in an HSM, a separate appliance many systems use for high-value keys. A TPM would be the wrong home: a TPM is a chip on one computer and seals material to that platform, while a restore may happen on another host. SHA-256 can fingerprint the tape so tampering shows up, and that fingerprint does not make the bytes unreadable. Theft of the cartridge is still a loss. The cipher stops reading, not carrying. A key file in the case pocket would mean the mitigation had already failed.',
        'r1': 'A stolen file stays unreadable when the key is safe. The thief has the copy and still cannot read it. This does not stop the theft itself. This does not help if the key was with the file. An unlocked screen still shows the plain words. A fingerprint of the file is not the same thing. A fingerprint does not hide the contents. Keep the key somewhere else, not on the same tape. Confirm the key is not stored beside the backup. Confirm a spare key exists for the right people.',
        'r2': 'A courier picks up a locked dispatch case. The papers inside are scrambled, and the combination stays in the office safe, not in the case pocket. Stealing the case from the van does not let the thief read the papers. Losing the case is still a problem, because the only copy of the week might be inside, and the lock does not phone the office. A wax seal that shows whether the case was opened is not the lock. The case is the backup tape, and the combination is the key. Encryption keeps a stolen copy unreadable when the key stayed behind, and it does not stop the copy from leaving.',
        'r4': 'Read the backup job and write down where the key is stored. Inspect the media case and write down whether any key material travels with the cartridge. Confirm a restore was rehearsed without copying the key onto the tape. Do not place a key file on the same volume as the backup. Do not treat a checksum of the tape as a substitute for encryption. Do not close a lost-media ticket as harmless until the key location is known.',
        'exam': 'Encryption makes a stolen copy unreadable only while the key stayed safe. It does not stop the theft. Monitoring would page you about the loss. Least privilege would limit who may create the copy. A SHA-256 fingerprint shows change and does not hide contents. AES-256 is the shared-key cipher for the bulk tape. The key belongs in an HSM, a separate appliance, not in a TPM chip tied to one computer, and not in the tape case. An unlocked screen shows plaintext the stored cipher is not covering.',
    },
    'Monitoring': {
        'body': "Monitoring watches logs and other signals so a bad event is seen while there is still time to act. A paging rule reaches a person who can look now. The watch stops the clinic from meeting the problem at next month's review. Watching does not block the attack. Failed guesses continue until a lockout, a filter, or a person disables the account.\n\nCollection, a threshold, and an answer are the three pieces. Domain controllers write event 4625 for each failed logon. A collector gathers them. A rule fires when the count crosses a line the team chose, for this clinic eighty failures in four minutes on one account, and the rule sends a page. A line so high that real attacks never cross it is decoration. A line so low that ordinary typos page the on-call every hour teaches people to ignore pages. A dashboard nobody opens is only storage.\n\nMara Chen is on call when the phone shows eighty-three 4625 events for one front-desk account in four minutes, from 203.0.113.50. She opens the live log, sees the failures still arriving, and notes that no successful 4624 follows them for that account. She writes the account, the count, the source, and the start time into ticket SEC-1190. She does not wait for the weekly meeting. The page brought her in within minutes. The page did not lock the account. If the account is actually stolen, disabling it is containment, which is the next phase, not the page itself.\n\nDefenders choose a few signals that mean harm: failed logins, a new member of an admin group, a noisy source that goes silent, a malware quarantine. They cause a harmless version of the signal and confirm the page arrives. They name who owns an unanswered page. They keep clocks aligned so the count is not a lie. Encryption would make a stolen file unreadable, which is a different outcome from a phone alert. Least privilege would limit what the front-desk account can do after a correct guess. The page is how you learn the guessing is underway.\n\nStudents select monitoring when the question asks what blocks a flood of guesses. The block is a lockout, a firewall, or a disabled account. The monitor is how a person finds out. A log kept for a quarterly review is not this control, because the time to act has passed. Detection and analysis is the incident phase this work belongs to. Containment begins when you actually limit the account or the host.",
        'r0': 'Event 4625 is a failed logon. The clinic page fires at eighty such events in four minutes on one account. One real page showed eighty-three failures from 203.0.113.50 with no following 4624 success, and the analyst opened ticket SEC-1190 in minutes rather than at a monthly review. The rule does not lock the account and does not drop the packets. A threshold nobody can reach will never page, and a threshold that pages on ordinary typos will be ignored. Clocks have to agree or the four-minute window is fiction. Detection and analysis is this phase. Disabling a stolen account is containment and comes after.',
        'r1': 'Monitoring watches signals so you can act in time. A burst of bad sign-ins should page a person. The page should arrive in minutes. A review next month is not the same watch. Watching does not block the guesses by itself. A lock or a filter would do the blocking. You still need the watch to know it happened. A log that nobody reads is not monitoring. Set a line that means this is too many. Write down what the page said. Quiet attacks under that line can still slip by. Missing sources cannot page you at all. Do not treat an alert as the repair.',
        'r2': "A night supervisor walks a ward and watches the board, then calls a nurse when one room's alarm repeats. The walk does not lock the room door. The lock is a separate device. Someone still has to notice which room is ringing before any lock helps. If the supervisor only writes the alarms into a binder for a meeting next month, the walk missed the point. If every cough sets off a call, the nurses stop answering. The board is the stream of signals, and the call is the page. Monitoring is that timely watch, and it does not itself stop the thing it noticed.",
        'r4': 'Open the page and write down the account, the count, the source, and the start time. Compare those lines with the live log before you assume the rule misfired. Note whether any success followed the failures. Do not silence the rule while you are still looking. Do not wait for the weekly meeting to decide whether the burst is real. Do not call the page itself a lockout or a disabled account.',
        'exam': 'Monitoring gets a person to the signal while action is still useful. It does not block the guesses. A lockout, a filter, or a disabled account does the blocking. A quarterly log review is not monitoring, because the time to act has passed. The page belongs to detection and analysis. Disabling a stolen account is containment. Encryption and least privilege change different outcomes: unreadable copies, and smaller rights. Students who say the monitor stops the flood have picked the wrong control.',
    },
    'Least privilege': {
        'body': 'Least privilege gives each account only the rights the job needs, with no standing admin access kept just in case. The front desk can reset passwords for reception accounts and cannot touch a domain administrator. That limit stops a stolen front-desk password from becoming control of the whole domain. Abuse of the reset right they do hold can still happen. The network is not split by this choice.\n\nLeast privilege is not segmentation. Segmentation decides which machines can reach which other machines. This control decides what an account can do after a session exists. You can have tiny rights on a flat network, and you can have a domain admin sitting inside a carefully walled wing. Rights are handed out through groups. Daily mail and browsing use a normal account. Admin work uses a different account that is not the one people read mail with. A shared clinic-admin password on a sticky note is the opposite of the control, even if most people never type it.\n\nChris Okeke tests the reception desk after a role change. Maria is in the group RECEPTION-PWRESET. That group may reset passwords on the reception organizational unit and on nothing else. Chris resets a reception test user and sees event 4724 for the success. The same tool against a domain admin account returns access denied. Maria is not a member of Domain Admins. He records the group, the successful 4724, and the denied attempt. The account can do the job. It cannot do the next job up.\n\nDefenders write down the tasks a role really performs and grant only those. They review membership when someone changes jobs, and they remove rights the new job does not use. They refuse to drop a person into a powerful group because a ticket feels urgent. They keep admin accounts off ordinary browsing, so a phish of the mail account does not hand over the domain. A page about a strange reset would be monitoring. Unreadable backups would be encryption. Neither one withholds a right the job never needed.\n\nStudents answer with the network split when the story is an account that can do too much, and they answer with account rights when the story is guest wireless reaching payroll. The object of the limit is the separating fact. An account and a group are this control. A boundary between networks is segmentation. A firewall line and a file mode are nearby ideas, and the swap that hurts most often is still the one between rights and rooms.',
        'r0': 'Group RECEPTION-PWRESET may reset passwords only in the reception organizational unit. A successful reset of a reception test user writes event 4724. The same attempt against a domain admin returns access denied, and that user is not in Domain Admins. Those are account rights, which is least privilege. They are not a VLAN, a firewall zone, or any other network split. Daily work stays on a normal account so a mail phish does not carry admin rights. A shared sticky-note admin password breaks the control even when the group design looks right. The reset right itself can still be abused, because the job was granted that right.',
        'r1': 'Least privilege gives a job only the rights it needs. Extra admin rights just in case do not belong. The front desk may reset one kind of password. The front desk may not run the whole domain. This is about accounts, not network rooms. Splitting networks is a different control. A small right can still be abused. The abuse stays inside those small rights. A stolen all-powerful account is a bigger mess. Give people a normal account for daily work. Keep a separate account for admin tasks. Test a reset that should succeed. Test a powerful change that should fail. Write down the group the person is in.',
        'r2': "A warehouse picker gets a key to one aisle, the aisle that holds the goods they pack. They do not get the master key to every cage, and they do not get a spare key just because a holiday rush might appear. Walls between the warehouse and the public sidewalk are a different project from which key the picker carries. A picker can still mishandle boxes inside their own aisle. Packing the day's goods does not require a key to every other cage. The aisle key is least privilege. The walls are segmentation, and handing someone a smaller key does not build those walls.",
        'r4': 'List the tasks the role actually performs. Write down the group the person is in and the directory scope that group can change. Reset a test account inside that scope and record the success, including the event number if one is written. Try a powerful account outside that scope and record the refusal. Do not add the person to the domain admin group to finish the test. Do not share one admin password as a shortcut while the groups are being fixed.',
        'exam': 'Least privilege is not segmentation. Rights on an account are this control. Which machines can talk is the network split. A front-desk group that can reset reception passwords, and cannot touch a domain admin, shows the limit: event 4724 on the allowed reset, access denied on the other. Abuse of the rights the job does hold is still possible. A flat network can sit under perfect account rights. Encryption and monitoring answer different questions: hiding a stolen copy, and noticing the attempt.',
    },
    'Configuration enforcement': {
        'body': 'Configuration enforcement puts the approved settings back when a machine drifts. Someone sets a laptop firewall to allow any inbound traffic. Overnight the baseline returns that firewall to the clinic standard. The push stops a bad setting from becoming the silent new normal. A person can change the setting again before the next cycle. A bug in program code is outside this repair.\n\nA declared standard plus a tool that can write, not only read, is the mechanism. Group Policy, a management profile, or another desired-state tool holds the approved values. On a schedule, or at the next refresh, the tool compares the live computer with those values, reapplies the standard where they differ, and records the correction. A document in a shared folder is not the control. Nothing reaches the laptop. Local administrators can still flip a setting in the hours between refreshes, which is why the correction log matters as much as the correction itself.\n\nThe desktop lead reviews host CLINIC-L14 on Friday. At 18:40 the host firewall was set to allow all inbound. At 02:10 the workstation baseline reported a remediation, and inbound policy was back to the standard: block unsolicited inbound, except the one management path the clinic uses. She writes the host, both times, the setting name, and the policy name in the ticket. She does not edit the baseline so that allow-all becomes the standard. The machine matches the decision again. She still does not know who made the 18:40 change, because the tool rewrote the setting and did not investigate the person.\n\nDefenders build the baseline from choices they mean to keep, then they let the tool apply it. They alert when the same host is corrected over and over, because repetition means someone is pushing back. They keep this work distinct from patching. A patch replaces flawed code, while enforcement restores a value such as a firewall profile. They also keep it distinct from retirement. A system you still intend to run should be pulled back to the standard. A system you intend to leave should be removed, not endlessly repaired.\n\nStudents think a written standard is the same as the push. The standard is the decision. The push is what makes a drifted laptop match that decision again. An agent that quarantines a file can leave the firewall wide open. Retirement would take the laptop out of service. Neither one restores the inbound rule at 02:10.',
        'r0': "Host CLINIC-L14 was set to allow all inbound at 18:40. At 02:10 the workstation baseline remediated that drift and restored the standard inbound block, leaving only the clinic's management exception. Group Policy or a similar desired-state tool did the write. A standard that lives only in a document never touches the laptop. The correction does not name the person who drifted the setting, and a repeat the next afternoon can undo it until the following refresh. Alert when the same host is remediated again and again. This push restores settings. It does not install a vendor code fix, and it does not retire the laptop.",
        'r1': 'Configuration enforcement puts approved settings back. Drift is a setting that wandered from the standard. A laptop may be opened to all inbound traffic. Overnight the standard can close that opening. The document in a drawer does not push the change. A tool has to check the machine and correct it. This does not retire a system you should remove. This does not fix a bug in the program code. Someone can change the setting again tomorrow. Watch for repeats, not only the quiet repair. Read the report that names the host. Do not weaken the standard to match the drift.',
        'r2': 'A shop photographs how every shelf should look. A closer then walks the aisles at night and puts drifted boxes back on the marks. The photograph in a drawer does nothing by itself. Someone has to move the boxes. A shopper can shuffle a shelf again before the next walk, so a shelf that must be fixed every night is a clue, not a victory. The photograph is the approved baseline, and the night walk is the enforcement. Putting boxes back is not the same as throwing the shelf away, and it is not the same as repairing a cracked product on that shelf.',
        'r4': 'On the drifted host, write down the setting, the time it was wrong, and the time the baseline restored it. Record the policy name that won. Check whether the same host was corrected on earlier nights, and write that count down. Do not edit the baseline so the drifted value becomes the new standard. Do not assume the rewrite identified who changed the setting. Leave the host in service if the clinic still means to keep it.',
        'exam': 'Configuration enforcement writes approved settings back when drift appears. A document nobody pushes is a decision, not this control. The laptop that allowed all inbound at 18:40 and matched the baseline again at 02:10 is the pattern. Patching replaces flawed code. Decommissioning removes the system. An endpoint agent can quarantine a file and leave the bad setting in place. The push does not name who drifted the host, and a new change can stick until the next cycle.',
    },
    'Decommissioning': {
        'body': 'Decommissioning retires a system on purpose. Access comes off, data is kept or destroyed by a written decision, and the box is no longer a forgotten listener. The retirement stops an old demo server from staying online with live accounts and an unpatched service. Systems you still run are not protected by this checklist. A replacement can be built with the same mistakes if nobody writes them down.\n\nA checklist with an owner is the mechanism, not a power button alone. Confirm the business has finished with the system. Move or delete the data under the retention rule, and wipe disks that will leave the building. Disable and remove the accounts, including service accounts that only that box used. Delete the DNS name, the firewall rules that mentioned the host, and the monitoring checks that would otherwise page forever. Update the diagram in the same week. Then power it off and record the asset as retired. Powering it off first, and saving the rest for later, leaves names and accounts in place, and later often never arrives.\n\nThe clinic retires demo server DEMO-APP-03 under ticket CM-1844. The ticket holds the owner sign-off, the disk wipe, the removal of account svc-demo, and the deletion of the DNS name. The diagram drops the server the same week. A scan that used to see TCP 443 on 10.8.3.40 now gets no answer. The engineer writes those results on the ticket before closing it. Months later svc-demo cannot be used, because the account is gone rather than merely idle. The hardware is not in a closet still joined to the domain.\n\nDefenders schedule the work when a project ends, a vendor demo expires, or a server is replaced. They keep the checklist short enough that people finish it. Drift on a system you mean to keep is a different job: enforcement puts approved settings back. This retirement removes the system from the live environment. During an incident, cutting a host off the network is containment and assumes the host may return after you understand it. You do not invent a retirement in the middle of detection just to avoid the investigation.\n\nStudents accept a shutdown as a finished retirement. Shutdown is one line. Accounts, names, rules, data, and the diagram are the rest. A system that is off and still has a domain account has not been retired. An agent on a server you meant to keep is not retirement either. The separating fact is whether the organization still claims the box as part of the live environment.',
        'r0': 'Ticket CM-1844 retires DEMO-APP-03 in the same week: owner sign-off, a disk wipe, removal of account svc-demo, deletion of the DNS name, and a diagram that no longer shows the host. A scan of 10.8.3.40 no longer finds TCP 443. Powering the server off without those steps leaves the account and the name behind. Containment during an incident isolates a host that may return. Retirement means the organization no longer claims the box. Drift repair on a keeper system is configuration enforcement, not this checklist. A replacement built next month can repeat every mistake if the notes were never written.',
        'r1': 'Retire a system because you mean to, not by accident. Take the accounts off, not only the power. Decide what happens to the data. Remove the name from the network records. Change the map before the week ends. A demo server left running is still a door. Powering it off is only one step. Old accounts can still be a path. This is not the same as fixing live settings. Live settings are for systems you keep. This does not protect the machines that remain. A replacement can repeat the same mistakes. Write the ticket before you wipe the disk. Confirm the host no longer answers.',
        'r2': "A pop-up counter closes when the fair ends. The crew returns the keys, takes the sign down, settles what happens to the stock, and tells the map maker to erase the stall. Switching the lights off and going home is only one chore. If the keys stay in a stranger's pocket and the sign stays up, people will still find the counter. What happens to the leftover stock has to be chosen, not left in a box under the counter. The stall is the old server. Decommissioning is the full closeout: access gone, data decided, name removed, and the diagram updated, not a dark power light alone.",
        'r4': 'Open the retirement ticket and write down the owner sign-off. List every account, name record, and firewall rule that still mentions the host. Record the data decision and whether the disk was wiped. Confirm a scan no longer sees the host, and confirm the diagram dropped it. Do not wipe the disk before that data decision is written. Do not call a power-off the end of the work while accounts remain.',
        'exam': 'Decommissioning retires a system on purpose: access removed, data handled, names and rules gone, diagram updated the same week. Shutdown alone leaves accounts behind. Configuration enforcement keeps a live system on approved settings and does not retire it. Incident containment cuts a host off that may come back after you understand it. A demo server that still has a service account is not retired. The checklist does not protect systems you kept, and a replacement can repeat the old mistakes.',
    },
    'Endpoint protection': {
        'body': 'Endpoint protection is software on the computer that watches for malware and for suspicious behavior. The agent can quarantine a trojan a user just downloaded, and that action stops the file from running. The pop-up does not explain how the file arrived. A current agent is not a reason to skip patches. A hole in a trusted program can be used without a file the agent has ever seen.\n\nFiles, process starts, and some behaviors are what the agent inspects. Known hashes and signatures catch familiar malware. Behavior rules catch some files that are new but act in a blocked way. Neither view sees a stolen password typed into a real site, because nothing malicious landed on disk. A new sample that matches no rule can also slip through. Quarantine holds the file so the double-click fails. The ticket stays open. In incident handling, eradication is the later removal of the malware and the closing of the hole, after a sample is preserved. The pop-up is not that whole phase.\n\nSam on CLINIC-L22 downloads a viewer, and the agent acts at 14:06. The console says C:\\Users\\Sam\\Downloads\\viewer.exe was quarantined. Sam can no longer open it. The analyst copies the path, the time, the host, the user, and the detection name into the ticket, and keeps the sample. The same ticket asks which message brought the file in. A search of other hosts shows whether the same name landed elsewhere. The agent did the block. The mail path and any unpatched reader that may have been the real door are still open questions.\n\nDefenders keep the agent current so signatures and behavior rules are not months old. They still patch, because the browser vendor and the agent vendor are closing different gaps. They treat a quarantine as a lead, and they do not close the ticket on the pop-up alone. A host firewall would ask whether a connection was allowed, which is an address and port question. Host intrusion prevention would ask whether one process was caught in a blocked behavior. This result is a file held aside.\n\nStudents treat the agent as a substitute for the patch cycle. The agent did not rewrite the vulnerable program. A fleet with a green console can still be missing the browser fix. Other students treat the quarantine as eradication. The block stopped the file from running. Eradication still means removing it cleanly and fixing how it arrived, once the sample is saved. Skipping fixes because the agent seems to have it leaves the known hole in place.',
        'r0': 'At 14:06 the agent on CLINIC-L22 quarantines C:\\Users\\Sam\\Downloads\\viewer.exe and the double-click stops working. That is a file control: a signature or a behavior rule matched, and the sample should be kept. The event does not name the message that delivered the file. A stolen password typed into a real site may never drop a file for the agent to see. A brand-new sample that trips no rule can run. Quarantine is not eradication. Eradication removes the malware and closes the hole after evidence is preserved. The agent is not a reason to skip the browser patch. Also search other hosts for that same file name before closing the ticket.',
        'r1': 'Endpoint protection watches the computer for bad programs. It also watches for suspicious behavior. It can quarantine a file the user downloaded. Quarantine is not the end of the story. Ask how that file arrived. This software is not a reason to skip fixes. A brand-new bad file may not be recognized. A stolen password may not drop a file at all. A filter on ports is a different control. Keep a copy of the blocked file for the ticket. Do not delete the clue before anyone looks. Check whether the same file reached other people.',
        'r2': 'The mail room sets a suspicious parcel aside and keeps it for someone who knows parcels. Setting it aside stops the clerk from opening it at the desk. It does not say which truck brought it, and it does not repair a broken lock on the loading door. A parcel of a kind the room has never seen may still go through. Fixing that broken lock is a separate job from checking parcels. Staff still write down which truck arrived and at what time. The parcel check is endpoint protection. Holding the bad file is not a reason to skip the fixes those computers still need.',
        'r4': 'Open the quarantine event and write down the path, the host, the user, the time, and the detection name. Keep a copy of the sample. Search for the same file name on other machines, and write down where it appears. Do not delete the sample to make the console look clean. Do not close the ticket until you know how the file arrived. Do not skip the patch cycle because the agent showed a block.',
        'exam': 'Endpoint protection can quarantine a trojan and stop that file from running. It is not a reason to skip patches, and the quarantine is not eradication. Eradication removes the malware and closes the way in after the sample is kept. A stolen password with no dropped file may never appear in the agent log. A host firewall question is about addresses and ports. Host intrusion prevention is about blocked behavior such as injection. This evidence is a held file, plus a ticket that still asks how it arrived.',
    },
    'Host firewalls': {
        'body': 'A host firewall filters traffic on the computer itself. Host firewalls still matter when a network firewall already stands at the building edge, because laptops leave and a neighbor on the same subnet may never cross that edge. The filter stops inbound connections the policy does not name. A host firewall is not an intrusion-prevention system. It does not judge one process injecting into another, and it does not judge whether an allowed session carries a harmful payload.\n\nRules on the host are the mechanism: direction, address, protocol, and port, sometimes tied to a program, with state so replies to connections the computer started are allowed. The clinic laptop denies unsolicited inbound traffic and permits one management port, TCP 22, only from the admin subnet 10.8.1.0/24. A scan from a peer hits the deny. An administrator on that subnet can still open the management port. Outbound browsing stays allowed when the policy says so, which means the filter will not save a user from a site they chose to visit. Once the laptop is on a cafe network, the building firewall cannot see that choice. The host rule still can.\n\nA loaner is checked before it goes home with a nurse. From a test machine at 10.20.4.15, a probe to port 445 on the laptop is blocked. The laptop records Windows Filtering Platform event 5157, with that source and the local port. From 10.8.1.20, a management session to the same laptop succeeds. The technician writes both results on the build sheet. The laptop is enforcing a door policy. Nothing in the test shows whether a running program is injecting into another program. That question is one this filter cannot answer.\n\nDefenders turn the policy on in the image, then they lock it so a local click cannot leave it off. They keep the management exception narrow: one port, from one admin subnet. They log the blocks they mean to investigate, and they treat an allow-all inbound change as a fault to reverse, not as a convenience to keep. Separate network parts are still a different design. This control limits what one computer accepts when those parts fail or when the computer roams. Turning a service off is stronger than filtering it, because a filter can be opened while the listener remains.\n\nStudents call this filter an intrusion-prevention system because both can stop something on the computer. The filter stops connections that fail its address and port rules. Intrusion prevention stops behaviors, such as a memory injection, even when no new connection exists. A block that names a remote address and a port is the firewall. A block that names two processes and an injection is not.',
        'r0': 'The laptop denies unsolicited inbound traffic and allows TCP 22 only from 10.8.1.0/24. A probe from 10.20.4.15 to port 445 is blocked and logged as Windows Filtering Platform event 5157. A session from 10.8.1.20 to the management port succeeds. State lets replies to connections the laptop started come back, which is still not behavior analysis. A host firewall is not an intrusion-prevention system: it does not see one process injecting into another. The building firewall cannot help once the laptop is on another network. Turning the listener off is a stronger step than filtering a port that remains open. Record both results on the image sheet.',
        'r1': 'A host firewall is a door policy on the computer. It still matters behind the building firewall. A laptop leaves and sits on other networks. The computer should refuse most inbound connections. One management path from the admin group can stay. This filter looks at who is talking and on which service. It does not judge a program injecting into another. That judgment belongs to intrusion prevention. Allowed traffic can still carry a bad payload. A person already logged in is not stopped by the door. Read the block log on the laptop. Note the address that was refused. Do not open every inbound path to make a test easier.',
        'r2': "Each office keeps its own door rules, even though the building has a guard at the street. A nurse who carries a case of files home still needs those door habits, because the street guard is not on the trip. The door can refuse strangers and still allow one manager from the admin hall. The door does not watch whether someone inside is tampering with a coworker's desk. Allowed visitors can still carry trouble in a permitted bag. The office door is the host firewall. Intrusion prevention would be the person who stops the tampering, and a door policy is not that person.",
        'r4': 'From a non-admin address, try an inbound connection that should fail. Write down the block, including the source and the local service. From the admin range, try the one management path that should work, and write that result too. Read the rule on the computer rather than guessing from a diagram. Do not switch inbound traffic to allow everything while you test. Do not treat a packet block as proof the host stops process injection.',
        'exam': 'A host firewall is not an intrusion-prevention system. It filters connections by address, port, and direction on that computer, and it still matters behind a network firewall because hosts roam and neighbors may share a subnet. Event 5157 that names a remote address and a blocked port is this control. An event that names two processes and an injection is host intrusion prevention. The filter does not stop a bad payload inside an allowed flow, and it does not replace turning off a service you do not need.',
    },
    'Host intrusion prevention': {
        'body': 'Host intrusion prevention blocks suspicious behavior on the machine, not only files whose signatures are already known. One process starts to inject into another, and the agent stops that action. The block can fire even when the file hash has never been catalogued. A network scan is a different question, about connections. A flaw the vendor has not patched can remain in the program after the injection is stopped.\n\nThe agent watches process starts and memory writes that cross from one process into another. A signature scanner compares a file with a list. This control compares an action with a block rule. When a spreadsheet process asks for write access into the memory of the mail process, the rule treats the request as an attack technique and terminates it. Real admin tools sometimes inject too, so exceptions exist, and each exception has an owner. Switching the whole agent off to quiet one false block removes the control for every user on that computer. A host firewall never sees this event, because no packet has to leave the machine for one process to tamper with another.\n\nOn CLINIC-L07 at 11:42 the agent blocks excel.exe while it attempts to write the memory of outlook.exe. The user is rlee. The log names both processes, the action blocked, and the host. The analyst pastes that line into the ticket and does not file it as a firewall deny. There is no remote address and no port. She checks whether rlee was doing an admin task the exception list should cover. No change ticket exists. The injection did not finish. A signature scan of the spreadsheet might have called the file fine, because the behavior was the signal, not a known-bad hash.\n\nDefenders write exceptions down and review them. They keep blocking mode on behaviors they understand, and they use alert-only while a new rule is being proven. They still patch, because a technique that matches no rule can succeed, and a patch removes the bug that technique would have used. They still run a host firewall, because a probe against a listening port is not an injection. An agent that quarantines a known trojan file is acting on the file. This control acts on the behavior, including behavior from a file nobody has hashed yet.\n\nStudents see the word prevention and choose it for a port-filter question. A host firewall is not this system, and this system is not a host firewall. Denied packets belong to the firewall. A stopped injection, with two process names and no packet story, belongs here. A signature check that lets the behavior finish is not prevention. Prevention means the action was blocked.',
        'r0': 'On CLINIC-L07 at 11:42 the host prevention agent blocks excel.exe writing the memory of outlook.exe for user rlee. The log names the two processes and the block, and it names no remote port. A signature scan can miss that case when the file hash is unknown and the behavior is the signal. A host firewall would not see it, because no packet has to cross the network. Exceptions for real admin tools need an owner. Turning the agent off for one complaint drops the control for the whole computer. The block does not patch the bug the technique wanted, and it does not filter port scans.',
        'r1': 'Host intrusion prevention blocks bad behavior on the machine. It does more than match a known bad file. A program that injects into another program is a signal. The agent can stop that injection. A door filter would not see it. The door filter watches connections, not memory. A purely signature check can miss a new trick. Behavior rules can catch the new trick. Normal admin tools can look similar and get blocked. Do not turn the whole agent off for one complaint. This does not close a hole the vendor has not fixed. Read the block, the two program names, and the user.',
        'r2': "A floor lead on a workshop watches people, not the door lock. When one worker starts prying open a coworker's bench, the lead stops those hands. The door lock never noticed, because nobody walked through a doorway. A checklist of known bad tools might miss a brand-new pry bar used the same way. Sometimes a real repair also looks like prying, so the lead needs a written list of allowed repairs instead of going home. The lead is host intrusion prevention. Stopping the tampering is not a door policy, and it does not fix a weak bench that still needs a proper repair.",
        'r4': 'Open the prevention event and write down both program names, the user, the host, and the time. Note whether any remote address is present. Check for a change ticket that would explain an admin tool. Do not turn the whole agent off to clear one alert. Do not relabel the event as a firewall deny when no connection was blocked. Keep the blocking rule in place while you ask why the injection started.',
        'exam': 'Host intrusion prevention blocks behavior, not only known file signatures. A stopped injection, with two process names and no packet, is this control. A denied packet with an address and a port is a host firewall. A host firewall is not this system. A signature check that lets the action finish is not prevention. The block does not patch an unfixed flaw and does not replace a firewall. False blocks on real admin tools call for a written exception, not for switching the agent off.',
    },
    'Disabling unused ports and protocols': {
        'body': "Disabling unused ports and protocols removes listeners and old protocols the system does not need. A server that only publishes the clinic site should not offer file sharing or an ancient remote login. The change stops attacks that need those listeners. Attacks against the web service you kept are still possible. The change also does not encrypt that service.\n\nTurning the service off, and removing the protocol, is the mechanism, so nothing is bound to the port. On the clinic web server, file sharing is disabled, the old SMBv1 dialect is removed, and Telnet is not installed. A socket listing then shows TCP 443 and does not show TCP 445 or TCP 23. That result differs from a firewall rule that drops those ports while the daemons keep running. If a later change opens the firewall, a daemon that is still listening is exposed again. A daemon that was disabled is not waiting. Local programs also lose the unused service, which a packet filter aimed only at the network does not always accomplish.\n\nThe platform lead builds the web image and checks it with a socket listing before the server is advertised. The listing shows 443 listening. Probes to 445 and 23 from a test host get no service. She does not use a firewall log as her proof. She uses the empty listing. She records the image name, those three facts, and the date on the build ticket. A developer asks for Telnet for the weekend. She refuses and points at the management path the image already has. The unused listeners stay absent. The site on 443 still carries whatever flaws the web software has.\n\nDefenders start from the server's job and disable what that job does not require. They repeat the socket listing after every image change. They treat a firewall deny as a useful extra, not as proof the protocol is gone. They remove old dialects such as SMBv1 even on a file server that must keep a newer file-sharing build. On a server that should not share files at all, they remove the service itself. A new password does not close a listener. Uninstalling a game does not close a listener either, unless that game was what had been bound to the port.\n\nStudents describe a firewall drop as if the protocol were disabled. The separating fact is whether anything is still listening. A filtered port can be one rule change away from exposure. A disabled protocol does not answer. Host intrusion prevention is not this step either. It blocks behaviors of processes, including processes you intentionally kept. You still need the web listener. You do not need Telnet beside it.",
        'r0': 'A socket listing on the web image shows TCP 443 listening and does not show TCP 445 or TCP 23. File sharing is disabled, SMBv1 is removed, and Telnet is not installed, so those daemons are not waiting. A firewall deny of the same ports is a different state: the listener can still be up, and a later rule change exposes it. Local programs lose a service that was disabled, which a network-only filter may not achieve. The web software on 443 can still be attacked. A changed password does not remove a listener. Deleting a game does not either, unless the game itself was the listener.',
        'r1': 'Turn off listeners you do not need. Turn off old protocols you do not need. A server that only serves the web should not share files. It should not offer ancient remote login. When the listener is gone, nothing is waiting. A door filter can hide a service that is still on. If the filter is later opened, that service returns. A service that was disabled stays gone. This does not protect the service you kept. The web service you need can still be attacked. Look at what is actually listening. Write down anything you did not expect. Do not disable a service the business still uses.',
        'r2': 'An old office has spare phones on every desk. The useful move is to unplug the phones nobody should call and take them away, not merely to tell the receptionist to refuse those lines. If the phone is still on the desk, a new receptionist can transfer the call tomorrow. The one phone the clinic actually uses stays. Unplugging does not make that remaining phone wise or private. Hauling the extra phones out of the building means a later receptionist has nothing to transfer. The unplugged phones are disabled listeners. A receptionist who only refuses calls is a filter in front of a phone that is still there.',
        'r4': 'Run a listener listing on the server and write down every service you did not expect. For each one, write whether the business job needs it. Confirm the needed site still answers after the change. Do not accept a firewall deny as proof a program is gone. Do not turn an old remote-login tool on for a weekend test. Leave the unused services disabled in the image, not only on the one server you are touching today.',
        'exam': 'Disabling unused ports and protocols removes the listener. A firewall rule can drop packets while the daemon still runs, and opening that rule later brings the service back. A listing that shows only the web service, with file sharing and old remote login absent, is this control. A host firewall filters. Host intrusion prevention blocks process behavior. Neither one proves the protocol is gone. Attacks on the service you kept are still possible, and a new password does not close the port.',
    },
    'Changing default passwords': {
        'body': "Changing default passwords replaces the published vendor secret before the device joins the real network. Switches, cameras, and printers often ship with a starting password in a manual or on a sticker. Anyone who can read that source can try it. The change stops that published secret from working. Theft of the new password later is a different problem. The change also leaves the account's power untouched.\n\nTiming and storage are the mechanism. The technician powers the device on a bench, not on the clinic LAN. She replaces the starting secret with one from the team vault, stores it where the on-call roster can reach it, and only then connects the uplink. She proves the published secret is rejected and the vault secret is accepted. A device that is cabled in first, with a promise to change the password after lunch, is already exposed during lunch. The account may still be a full administrator. Shrinking what that account may do would be least privilege, a separate decision. This step only kills the secret the vendor published.\n\nJordan builds switch SW-CLINIC-EDGE on the bench. The vendor sheet in the box describes the starting admin password. Jordan sets a vault-generated replacement, confirms the sheet's secret now fails, and confirms the vault secret opens the management session. The uplink stays unplugged until those two tests are on ticket NET-778. The switch then joins the clinic network. A month later a guess that uses the vendor's published secret fails. That failure means the control is in place. The same guess would succeed if it carried the vault secret, because this step does not detect a stolen good password.\n\nDefenders apply the step to every new switch, camera, phone, and printer, not only to servers people remember. They rename or disable well-known vendor accounts when the product allows it, and they still change the secret if the name must stay. They do not leave management open to the whole internet and call the password enough. Removing an unused management protocol takes a door away. A host firewall limits who may knock. A new secret does neither. Firmware defects belong to patching. The sticker secret is this problem.\n\nStudents think a long password on day thirty repairs a device that spent a month on the sticker secret, or they think the change is least privilege. The secret and the rights are different facts. An all-powerful account with a new password is still all-powerful. A published secret that still works is a door for anyone who has the manual, however small the account's other rights are. Order is the other trap: change the secret before the device is reachable, not after the first incident.",
        'r0': "Switch SW-CLINIC-EDGE stays off the clinic LAN until the vendor's published admin secret is rejected and the vault secret works. Ticket NET-778 records both tests before the uplink is connected. The account may remain a full administrator, so this is not least privilege. A guess a month later that uses the manual's starting password should fail. A guess that uses the vault password would succeed, which means theft of the new secret sits outside this step. Firmware holes are a patching problem. An open management service facing the whole internet is a firewall or listener problem, not proof the sticker secret was changed.",
        'r1': 'Change the published starting password before go-live. Vendors print that secret in a manual or on a sticker. Anyone who read it can try it. Do not cable the device in until the secret is changed. Store the new secret in the team vault. Test that the old secret now fails. This does not stop theft of the new secret later. This does not shrink how much power the account has. A full admin with a new password is still a full admin. This does not patch the device firmware. Check every new switch, camera, and printer. One forgotten sticker password is enough for a stranger.',
        'r2': "A safe arrives with the combination printed in the maker's booklet and on a tag tied to the handle. Before any valuables go inside, and before the safe is bolted into the public hall, the owner sets a new combination and stores it where the right staff can find it. Trying the booklet combination afterward should fail. A clever new combination does not decide how many people should know it, and it does not repair a cracked hinge. The booklet combination is the default password. Changing it before the device joins the real network is the control, and it does not shrink the account's power.",
        'r4': "On the bench, before any production cable is attached, try the vendor's published secret and write down the refusal. Try the vault secret and write down the success. Record the vault location and the ticket number. Do not connect the uplink until both results are written. Do not leave the sticker secret in place because the change feels easy to postpone. Do not treat the new secret as a smaller set of admin rights.",
        'exam': 'Changing default passwords kills the published vendor secret before the device is reachable on the real network. An all-powerful account with a new password is still all-powerful, so this is not least privilege. A change on day thirty does not repair a month spent on the sticker secret. Theft of the new secret is outside this step. A firewall or a disabled management protocol limits who can knock. Patching closes a firmware hole. The order is to change the secret, record it in the vault, and only then connect the cable.',
    },
    'Removing unnecessary software': {
        'body': "Removing unnecessary software shrinks the set of programs that can be buggy or abused. Games, old runtimes, and trial tools arrive on images and on vendor laptops. Each one is code the clinic did not ask to maintain. Removal stops flaws and lure screens that live only in those programs. A hole in the browser you still need stays open. A person with install rights can put the extra tool back.\n\nThe image is the mechanism, not a speech at handoff. The standard build lists what may be present. Games, the retired runtime, and the trial PDF suite are uninstalled before that build is approved. A vulnerability scan of the finished image should no longer report the runtime you removed. A laptop issued from an older image still has the problem, so the control is the pipeline plus a check, not a one-time cleanup of a single desk. Patching would update a program you keep. This control deletes a program you do not keep. An allow list can also stop a leftover installer from running if a copy remains. Removal means the files are gone.\n\nThe desktop lead opens the gold-image checklist before a batch of laptops goes out. The game pack is absent. The old runtime is absent. The trial PDF suite is absent. A scan of the image no longer lists that runtime as an installed product. She writes the image version and the three absences into the release ticket. One laptop from last year's image still shows the runtime, and she pulls that laptop back rather than calling the batch finished. The new image has fewer programs to watch. The browser that remains is still the browser, and it still needs its own fixes.\n\nDefenders tie the list to the job. A registration desk does not need a compiler or a trial remote-control tool. They review the image when a vendor changes the bundle it ships. They remove the software and also remove ordinary users' ability to install replacements, or the pile returns by Friday. Closing an unused listening port is a different cut. A port can belong to a system service you are not uninstalling, and a removed game may never have listened. Firewalls and behavior blocking still have work to do on the programs you keep.\n\nStudents say to patch a program that should not be installed, and they say to remove a program the clinic depends on when a fix exists. Whether the business needs the program is the separating fact. Needed software gets patched. Unneeded software leaves the image. A firewall rule that blocks a port does not uninstall the game, and a clean patch report does not prove the game is gone.",
        'r0': "The gold image drops the game pack, the retired runtime, and the trial PDF suite before laptops are issued. A vulnerability scan of that image no longer reports the runtime as installed. A laptop built from last year's image still shows it, so one clean desk does not define the fleet. Patching would raise the version of a program you keep. Removal deletes a program you do not keep. An allow list can block a leftover installer that is still on disk. Ordinary users who can install software will refill the image by the end of the week unless that right is gone too. The browser you kept still needs its own fixes.",
        'r1': 'Remove programs the job does not need. Every extra program can be buggy or abused. Games, old runtimes, and trial tools are typical extras. Take them off the standard image before handoff. A laptop should arrive without those extras. This is not the same as patching what you keep. Kept programs still need their fixes. This is not the same as closing an unused door. A needed program can still listen on a port. It does not make the remaining pile perfect. Check the installed list against the image plan. Write down anything extra you still see. Do not delete a tool the clinic still relies on.',
        'r2': 'A kitchen throws out the spare fryer, the broken blender, and the trial gadget nobody ordered. Fewer machines can catch fire or confuse the cooks. The stove the kitchen still uses can fail tomorrow, and throwing out the blender does not repair the stove. A cook who is allowed to wheel new gadgets in will refill the room. Unplugging a spare phone down the hall is a different chore from hauling appliances away. A checklist on the wall does not by itself carry the blender to the dumpster. The hauled-away gadgets are unnecessary software. Removal shrinks the set of programs that can be buggy, and it does not patch the programs you kept.',
        'r4': 'Compare the installed programs on the image with the approved list. Write down every extra item, including old runtimes and trial tools. Confirm a scan of the image no longer reports the runtime you removed. Check one laptop from the new batch and one from an older image. Do not approve the batch while an old image is still going out. Do not uninstall a program the clinic still uses just to make the list look short.',
        'exam': 'Removing unnecessary software takes unneeded programs off the image, so their bugs leave with them. It does not patch the browser you still need, and it does not close a listener that belongs to a service you kept. Needed software gets patched. Unneeded software is removed. An allow list can block a file that is still installed, while removal means the files are gone. Users who can reinstall will undo the cleanup unless install rights are withheld. A clean patch report does not prove the extra program is gone.',
    },
}
